--- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $10$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-5$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-4$** | **$-2$** | **$0$** | **$2$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $10$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-5$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & (-4)^{2} & (-4)^{3} \\\\ -2 & (-2)^{2} & (-2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id 17592186400832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24daf051-a969-5853-92c4-9336c99d9eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75$ meters" :db/id 17592186400834} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c5c0b9-e64a-579b-9d0a-5faca271c7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.15$ meters" :db/id 17592186400835} {:answer/id #uuid "b25e0551-5bff-5a95-b67a-7ec0fc2e22ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.25$ meters" :db/id 17592186400836} {:answer/id #uuid "4166a757-6ff8-5e2d-b4fe-2e6fc3e044b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.25$ meters" :db/id 17592186400837} {:answer/id #uuid "00805968-8b23-52f9-a5f4-d3708a27362f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.75$ meters" :db/id 17592186400838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24daf051-a969-5853-92c4-9336c99d9eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75$ meters" :db/id 17592186400834} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b23bec-c550-5bbf-bae4-d3fc66059766" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ba26ac4-59dd-55c3-b644-94e5b96251c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139749" :question/problem "A bungee jumping instructor measures how much a bungee rope stretches (in meters) when one end of the rope is attached to the bridge railing while a weight of $x$ kilograms is hung on the other. This data is shown below.\n\n| Weight $(x)$ | **$10$** | **$20$** | **$30$** | **$40$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the rope stretch if the instructor uses a $65$-kilogram mass, assuming the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{500}[\\begin{aligned}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 20 \\\\ 1 & 30 \\\\ 1 & 40\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}[\\begin{aligned}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0.05\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.5+0.05x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the rope will stretch if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it, we substitute $x=65$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5+0.05(65) \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the rope will stretch $3.75$ meters if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it.\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=65$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id 17592186134117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c12a9093-8b33-506e-8833-5f1ad6a9261f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.5$ feet" :db/id 17592186400840} {:answer/id #uuid "032b222d-40e4-532e-af62-dfdc2dcc37e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id 17592186400841} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce327d5-8771-5e11-b6ab-ff3533542dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ feet" :db/id 17592186400842} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d9746c-39bb-5726-8a32-8963780d412e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.5$ feet" :db/id 17592186400843} {:answer/id #uuid "a99677dd-5b51-522d-a3d5-8b761133f62a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ feet" :db/id 17592186400844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032b222d-40e4-532e-af62-dfdc2dcc37e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id 17592186400841} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc15713a-b900-5d20-8415-c6b1dd1f9e84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f426df0-1f38-5a2e-b701-5e427a0357ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-139750" :question/problem "A biologist has recorded data on a pine tree's age (in years) and height (in feet). This data is shown below. \n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.2$ | $2.5$ | $3.6$ | $4.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the tree's height after $8$ years, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id 17592186400845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8b3d28b-5596-5d57-80ab-f683e124341e" :an