e hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 30 & 45 \\\\ 2 & 20 & 54 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 65 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 77 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 90\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id 17592186400873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28142e26-f6c7-54c9-9c16-035c75f7659f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With A Zero Row/Column or Two Identical Rows/Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f03dd7eb-af5f-5f22-813b-7f3d5b84651c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|B|$ only" :db/id 17592186400875} {:answer/id #uuid "ab86d5e2-989c-5d49-9b2d-7a183fd53e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|B|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id 17592186400876} {:answer/id #uuid "da8626ef-a38f-59a8-a718-2155b988e445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|C|$ only" :db/id 17592186400877} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fe0f31-e9db-50f1-a23b-a66578e23d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|A|$ only" :db/id 17592186400878} {:answer/id #uuid "1485ab74-1a6a-53e5-9aaf-e407a69b362d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|B|$ only" :db/id 17592186400879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab86d5e2-989c-5d49-9b2d-7a183fd53e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|B|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id 17592186400876} :answer-field/id #uuid "d252fa00-ae50-5d73-8bce-ecc15224f399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c1bd5a4-43fd-5d63-843c-209b976b49d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-140046" :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 18 & \\sqrt{6} & \\sqrt{3} & 4\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -10 & 4\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The $A$ is a triangular matrix, we obtain\n\n$$\n|A|=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 4=8\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has identical columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- Since $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, its determinant is:\n\n$$\ndet(C)=5\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot (-10)=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $|B|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5304084-5020-5bac-afe0-a4eaf6f0272d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Scale Factors and Angles of Rotation of Rotation-Scaling Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d416963d-0812-5442-bfb3-5143f8304cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $5$" :db/id 17592186400882} {:answer/id #uuid "25b68c79-4c84-5a8a-b3cb-c873ab4bbeec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ and $9$" :db/id 17592186400883} {:answer/id #uuid "399087ad-ae5c-50a9-b3f6-f634f021724e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ and $9$" :db/id 17592186400884} {:answer/id #uuid "abe1e0eb-b3fa-5749-9d80-faecfb2587cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $6$" :db/id 17592186400885} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b9e256-2bbe-5a6c-804a-deec95112ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $6$" :db/id 17592186400886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abe1e0eb-b3fa-5749-9d80-faecfb2587cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $6$" :db/id 17592186400885} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9bcdf0a-7ee5-512b-8b69-1c9ff761e1cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2b946dc-503a-51e5-98d7-7e814f7abb9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140401" :question/problem "Let $v$ be a non-zero vector from ${R}^{2}$ and let $A$ be the rotation-scaling matrix given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe description of $Av$ in terms of geometric transformations in the plane is given below. What are the missing entries?\n\n*To compute the vector $Av$, we rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\theta =\\text{_____}$ and then scale the result by a factor of $r=\\text{_____}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+18} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =6[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the missing entries are $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $r=6.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "289ae874-f555-50ee-896f-eb2188004124" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 3x3 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c340906-07d4-57b0-9046-295287f1840f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {3,0,6}" :db/id 17592186400889} {:answer/id #uuid "735c9554-2122-554a-9c6d-88f5c542af17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {3,4,9}" :db/id 17592186400890} {:answer/id #uuid "cf676211-b439-541c-a870-387de50e341e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {3,-2,6}" :db/id 17592186400891} {:answer/id #uuid "5f97e983-0e63-57dd-acda-842c7a7cfb12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {3,4,6}" :db/id 17592186400892} {:answer/id #uuid "812db70d-b4af-5153-8bf4-a315c5a42142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-3,4,6}" :db/id 17592186400893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f97e983-0e63-57dd-acda-842c7a7cfb12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {3,4,6}" :db/id 17592186400892} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ed14d3f-a85e-59d0-a5e0-cd86b0195649" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f772a19b-8ab2-5f26-9ef1-47231e01914d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140449" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )(6-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal."}] [{:db/id 17592186400894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b0f0ad-077e-5490-8903-2b75e6400fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id 17592186400896} {:answer/id #uuid "c9962984-13d2-5e7a-a101-99ffb98df884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An imaginary ellipse" :db/id 17592186400897} {:answer/id #uuid "c26a296a-362d-571b-bc2a-b4d7be4f7f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $4$ and $3$" :db/id 17592186400898} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9951ca-7918-51ae-8e54-71674985b355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id 17592186400899} {:answer/id #uuid "70050e40-2d4d-5f43-8504-d04583bd8a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $4$ and $3$" :db/id 17592186400900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9962984-13d2-5e7a-a101-99ffb98df884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An imaginary ellipse" :db/id 17592186400897} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d7df124-dbc5-56d7-8581-3302e8d894bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9992e79-a8b1-5b6c-bb33-ddbd38d4ce02" :question/math-academy-id "q-140555" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-7\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+4-4)+({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}+1-1) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+({x}_{2}-1)^{2}-4 & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+({x}_{2}-1)^{2} & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+2, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich defines an empty set. Notice that the equation is equivalent to $\\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(2\\text{i})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})^{2}}=1,$ which has imaginary solutions, so we can interpret this as an imaginary ellipse with semiaxes $2\\text{i}$ and $\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abcdf400-bcb6-5e0b-9ec6-dab2bf54761d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $8$ bugs after $4$ days" :db/id 17592186400903} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9c13c6-09fb-581a-8fa8-e20695647309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $27$ bugs after $5$ days" :db/id 17592186400904} {:answer/id #uuid "7235e376-c628-51f5-8028-177047d2588a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $15$ bugs after $4$ days" :db/id 17592186400905} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2d7c8e-9f10-5045-82f9-c14578f74b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $16$ bugs after $2$ days" :db/id 17592186400906} {:answer/id #uuid "6f60dfcf-9cc7-5683-856d-afa629682956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $11$ bugs after $2$ days" :db/id 17592186400907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f60dfcf-9cc7-5683-856d-afa629682956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $11$ bugs after $2$ days" :db/id 17592186400907} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e151de4-60bd-5c74-b6e4-910b2f3a14f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cbfc231-381e-525d-b699-91d7448dd5be" :question/math-academy-id "q-140822" :question/problem "Scientists are observing a colony of bugs. The population $P(t)$ of the colony is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(t)=27+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is the number of bugs, and $t>0$ is the time (in days) since the observation started. \n\nAt what time is the population of the colony a minimum, and what is the number of bugs at this time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the population is a minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(27+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(27)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0$ for $t>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =4 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=2.$\nTo find the number of bugs when $t=2,$ we figure out $P(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =27+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27+[{x}^{3}-12x]{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =27+[(8-24)-(0)] \\\\  & =27-16 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $11<27$, we conclude that the minimum population is $11$ bugs, and this happens after $2$ days."}] [{:db/id 17592186400908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b279401-b2e9-52e3-8e8a-5ed7f4ab0fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186400910} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9a7c28-297a-59f1-8324-969697fcb145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186400911} {:answer/id #uuid "6872e2bc-f5d0-5fd1-8dae-d3fd4d09b23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186400912} {:answer/id #uuid "432384f9-da06-525e-baa9-7350f4251ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186400913} {:answer/id #uuid "67ba60db-8cbf-553b-b63c-195669a23aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186400914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6872e2bc-f5d0-5fd1-8dae-d3fd4d09b23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186400912} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9d3fb41-4434-5c99-a083-41daf1b7f0a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2172cdc2-fb35-5393-84ba-3c3dbc3c7926" :question/math-academy-id "q-140997" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =6,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3559bdb9-c007-500b-83db-63f2d91cd407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id 17592186400917} {:answer/id #uuid "f1984a87-93e4-523e-80b4-a37c28b7dc5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id 17592186400918} {:answer/id #uuid "3f15fef9-83c3-50e8-980b-72c67ab596d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id 17592186400919} {:answer/id #uuid "f52c0001-2df6-56e0-ae3c-2dca7d79a58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id 17592186400920} {:answer/id #uuid "d7950f6f-fd87-5dac-ba8c-650fe8a19888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root" :db/id 17592186400921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7950f6f-fd87-5dac-ba8c-650fe8a19888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root" :db/id 17592186400921} :answer-field/id #uuid "5806f142-c711-5da8-b1d4-68c8001a3f1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab70a1a-e04b-524f-9866-e86f88144126" :question/math-academy-id "q-141140" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b362831ba2248e03537551c412ae7537646dcd09f0d00a598905f0d52a258da9.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-3.$ Therefore, $x=-3$ is a *simple* root."}] [{:db/id 17592186400922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc419917-fe34-5bf9-8bf0-1b022fce11ff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Less Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c694a7cb-1273-5aef-bab1-b00405cb06a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5<x<1" :db/id 17592186400924} {:answer/id #uuid "19263d22-de7b-5ef3-9798-b98080320005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-5$ or    $x>-1$" :db/id 17592186400925} {:answer/id #uuid "ee071af0-120e-5c4a-b72d-0c54e26d3687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>5" :db/id 17592186400926} {:answer/id #uuid "395b066b-730c-5a11-889e-cc63987fd8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-1$ or    $x>5$" :db/id 17592186400927} {:answer/id #uuid "3271e9c8-ddaf-5be7-852f-5b3fd22e072b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<x<5" :db/id 17592186400928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19263d22-de7b-5ef3-9798-b98080320005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-5$ or    $x>-1$" :db/id 17592186400925} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f3297c0-8b61-583d-8e5e-b17352fc3208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66cebf22-0b82-5cf0-a87d-5c8eaa25290f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141187" :question/problem "![](images/46/46edcf2265f41b8fac7931802875e1b9849006834b3509a186ffc7acb510959f.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(-x-5)(x+1)<0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(-x-5)(x+1)<0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola  \n\n$$\ny=(-x-5)(x+1)\n$$\n\n  \n\nis *below* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e830af773829b1712c6a87955e74411b6ad9921ae1aea58029ca968ab354c0f1.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-5$ or    $x>-1.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the General Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d912e9d9-60dd-53f8-84b5-6a01262be9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id 17592186400931} {:answer/id #uuid "5e69372e-e868-57ad-81a9-235542604c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $3$" :db/id 17592186400932} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3cf5dc-1a5e-5ca5-bb04-89cb5648d292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id 17592186400933} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f4c2df-fdff-5c81-86c1-ed79a5590ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $-\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id 17592186400934} {:answer/id #uuid "ee524a7c-b00b-54ff-b0db-1379d3f0eb87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id 17592186400935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d912e9d9-60dd-53f8-84b5-6a01262be9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id 17592186400931} :answer-field/id #uuid "a83cb541-9ba7-558c-804e-5b78b4a327b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c5b5131-1801-5dfb-aff8-f50ca940df15" :question/math-academy-id "q-141310" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{4}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bc4897-6558-52b5-bb24-657fa38a810c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=8" :db/id 17592186400938} {:answer/id #uuid "62f6fb47-ecea-56a8-9070-83fc55e7254f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-5" :db/id 17592186400939} {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id 17592186400940} {:answer/id #uuid "bdeaea36-9352-52ca-8483-6b276a77d472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=7" :db/id 17592186400941} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc66d56-1a8c-5626-b30a-e9774929570d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-4" :db/id 17592186400942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id 17592186400940} :answer-field/id #uuid "95bbc964-13ea-55a0-9065-a7c49f46fb65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd1c73ef-6818-5b51-baee-cca065c2f0da" :question/math-academy-id "q-141539" :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}+6x+14$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+14\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}-{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=3$ and $q=5.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d53fe67-7a37-5135-9bda-59dc26da7eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=10" :db/id 17592186400945} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5c7488-0854-5209-9e72-78867a6a1be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id 17592186400946} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ec775e-a603-50a4-a821-b02cc8bdf4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-10,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id 17592186400947} {:answer/id #uuid "91f01cc1-7dda-5a0a-a09b-a1edcdb5224f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id 17592186400948} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e81c67-49e3-5deb-9f3d-4bfdd5f4f433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\text{ }q=-5" :db/id 17592186400949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f01cc1-7dda-5a0a-a09b-a1edcdb5224f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id 17592186400948} :answer-field/id #uuid "c559c184-d951-52c2-ae9b-1fd6b5cd4853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a81398bc-73ed-504a-b8b3-ef512650d7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141622" :question/problem "If the expression ${y}^{2}-10y+30$ is written as $(y+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52634bca-83b3-54b7-95a5-9e44e545d6f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186400952} {:answer/id #uuid "01dbf8ef-0546-56c6-8042-b514ff678457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186400953} {:answer/id #uuid "13883436-b2ee-5552-ac8d-52c81b763494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186400954} {:answer/id #uuid "b001b5b2-84bb-5ff9-8a1e-ad85dd8dd376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186400955} {:answer/id #uuid "34858f57-efb3-57e6-9b45-36ddb2034af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186400956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34858f57-efb3-57e6-9b45-36ddb2034af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186400956} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc09fe3d-b285-5568-9b7d-7d325bd277b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9955f0fd-f850-5637-ae70-443f1b225d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-141738" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d2f7497d6a11b4ceb994755498ef49bf0ce414601ab812dc55555c769a076ec.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave up?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graph II curves upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs."}] [{:db/id 17592186400957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7059f97-c797-51ad-be0a-f96acec895ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186400959} {:answer/id #uuid "1e18716e-efdf-5322-a98c-f7741d67d8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186400960} {:answer/id #uuid "028c6bbc-5127-5736-b3ba-9f94a0f28ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186400961} {:answer/id #uuid "3daa75bf-f787-56ff-9dca-1b80759e9c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186400962} {:answer/id #uuid "d4912560-949d-5a8e-9911-c20ce554555e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186400963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e18716e-efdf-5322-a98c-f7741d67d8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186400960} :answer-field/id #uuid "b71eae7c-91a7-5647-b98b-5b82bc79007f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8be6241f-30ce-53dd-8d31-e6a859245baf" :question/math-academy-id "q-141740" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f07f2751398a101b41bc2c923fcdd7f6a5144da00008904297c36b2d8d44a30.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave up?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graphs I and III curve upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs."}] [{:db/id 17592186400964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbe80595-c807-506c-a356-566f1edb3402" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Less Than (Or Equal To) Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb10726b-626d-526b-b3f9-de6bad9f3f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 5" :db/id 17592186400966} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f8c3f4-5711-584d-9263-fed31acf6c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -6$ or $x\\ge 1$" :db/id 17592186400967} {:answer/id #uuid "bce75758-abe3-5789-8de5-5d18996c87a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 1$ or $x\\ge 5$" :db/id 17592186400968} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8d0e9c-0806-580a-b031-8a01a725abe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\le x\\le -1" :db/id 17592186400969} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a43c47-c698-531e-a0a3-0b2da98a2630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -5$ or $x\\ge 1$" :db/id 17592186400970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce75758-abe3-5789-8de5-5d18996c87a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 1$ or $x\\ge 5$" :db/id 17592186400968} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b2ce6b8-4640-53b8-b17b-3464a0fe587d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f59dad02-8b17-598a-a754-71bfff242018" :question/math-academy-id "q-142315" :question/problem "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}+6x-5\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+6x-5\\le 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}+6x-5\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-6x+5) & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=1$ and $x=5,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/7c/7cfa66b5aa6a3f29e7154e329926a47bbbb0eb45dce3251148986785f09ee383.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le 1$ or $x\\ge 5.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebabf742-2b8d-5392-a077-f34e8f4bd034" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Mass Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bfd97b-bea2-5ab6-9084-e9f94b5ae836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $0$ |" :db/id 17592186400973} {:answer/id #uuid "b14388b2-8f17-5b6d-bd37-dce823b24383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.25$ | $0.35$ | $0.4$ |" :db/id 17592186400974} {:answer/id #uuid "e285d986-7f75-5785-9aea-c3e63564aefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ | $0.3$ |" :db/id 17592186400975} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fd822c-1f8c-5028-b56a-da7d3e74b8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.5$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |" :db/id 17592186400976} {:answer/id #uuid "1632a6d2-71a6-5531-bc91-795371f646d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |" :db/id 17592186400977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4bfd97b-bea2-5ab6-9084-e9f94b5ae836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $0$ |" :db/id 17592186400973} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e2d2d25-c810-5851-af0b-d59a08144e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a2eced6-9cd5-5469-b3dc-6168e1ec698d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142915" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=4)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=x\\,|\\,Y=4)=\\frac{P(X=x,Y=4)}{P(Y=1)}=\\frac{f(x,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $x\\in {0,1,2},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(0,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.25}=0.4 \\\\ P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.15}{0.25}=0.6 \\\\ P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(2,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0}{0.25}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $0$ |"}] [{:db/id 17592186400978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebabf742-2b8d-5392-a077-f34e8f4bd034" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Mass Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24afd0b0-d6de-56bf-ae8f-4be3381c967b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |" :db/id 17592186400980} {:answer/id #uuid "f992bbff-d9b3-5a36-9c82-3cc33ed8858f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |" :db/id 17592186400981} {:answer/id #uuid "c365283b-bc38-5d73-b350-7938e68cf363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |" :db/id 17592186400982} {:answer/id #uuid "29ea203b-ba7b-591f-b3f5-324bd7be1407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ | $0.3$ |" :db/id 17592186400983} {:answer/id #uuid "98bfc8a2-c396-5e52-ace3-f6d5e9d34641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |" :db/id 17592186400984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98bfc8a2-c396-5e52-ace3-f6d5e9d34641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |" :db/id 17592186400984} :answer-field/id #uuid "232c8a87-8c1b-58ee-be3e-6552e315bf0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d63f5e38-dd7f-5597-b7d6-4c39a2fe3945" :question/math-academy-id "q-142928" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=0)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=0)=\\frac{P(X=0,Y=y)}{P(X=0)}=\\frac{f(0,y)}{{f}_{X}(0)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.5$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in {0,2,4},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,|\\,Y=0) & =\\frac{f(0,0)}{{f}_{X}(0)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.5}=0.4 \\\\ P(X=0\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(0,2)}{{f}_{X}(0)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.5}=0.2 \\\\ P(X=0\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(0,4)}{{f}_{X}(0)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.5}=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |"}] [{:db/id 17592186400985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1888cd0-9005-51e4-8f23-a9c29cc22081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k\\sqrt{V},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400987} {:answer/id #uuid "f928b778-da6a-5b79-9658-dc18dc898f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt{V},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400988} {:answer/id #uuid "143789ed-0846-5adc-8244-a09df213d77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{V}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400989} {:answer/id #uuid "3941f316-7785-5f8b-8014-322641f53352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400990} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8de928-580b-5d72-8ae9-f3db96bd744c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{V},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3941f316-7785-5f8b-8014-322641f53352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400990} :answer-field/id #uuid "4527b519-e235-578b-ba4e-e12b67a2bec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b30d46be-646e-5361-a604-5fb66c710b5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143034" :question/problem "The value $V$ of a particular art object increases with time. The rate of change of the object's value is proportional to the square of the value. What differential equation describes the change of the value at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **value** $V$ of a particular art object **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the object's value is **proportional** to the **square of the value**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *value* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *value squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝{V}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the value *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3609cc0-6b67-5266-9b4a-2f29d8232471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k\\sqrt{H},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400994} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6cd918-68d8-52d0-9155-4d41f4fd2598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k{H}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400995} {:answer/id #uuid "add74caf-c6d7-5a05-a30c-9b03f535871e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{H}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400996} {:answer/id #uuid "e048b22c-334e-593c-a1bb-448f1e70ae12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-kH,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400997} {:answer/id #uuid "1b08abf8-2a85-5a9c-9728-5bed51d13ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k{H}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b08abf8-2a85-5a9c-9728-5bed51d13ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k{H}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186400998} :answer-field/id #uuid "5afc3f62-82b2-5ca2-8452-04ff173cc875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae491dc9-9063-5d70-aa00-00c9f3a98d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-143037" :question/problem "Construction waste stored in a large pile is taken to a landfill. The height $H$ of the pile decreases with time. The rate of change of the pile's height is proportional to the square of the height. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the height at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **height** $H$ of the pile **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the pile's height is **proportional** to the **square of the height**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *height squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}∝{H}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k{H}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1df005b-79a7-5108-a856-5625e567e6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id 17592186401001} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e2d5da-8670-5e49-9815-d8ca8944853f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id 17592186401002} {:answer/id #uuid "50d5da3e-822f-53d4-8c03-30602011d2c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id 17592186401003} {:answer/id #uuid "78a853e7-8361-5bd7-86dd-a6c97855e4cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id 17592186401004} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6fd1bd-e8a9-5868-be9f-9b06301f3ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id 17592186401005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a6fd1bd-e8a9-5868-be9f-9b06301f3ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id 17592186401005} :answer-field/id #uuid "3031e52c-a4c8-5e7c-bd98-d40e649d233d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fb709d8-90a5-57a1-95a3-b0285559ae32" :question/math-academy-id "q-149548" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(1-x)(y+2)^{2}.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>-2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-2$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=1$ and $y=-2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/34/345665733b6554a7cea9b54a4ca4e93b6d478d67231cfaf3f6180cf0a75c4b1a.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/40/4058b0f027297faf71756da7cad372b76aae980657f053b95318115889c1d614.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $1-x$ | $(y+2)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(0,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(2,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(0,-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(2,-3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/fc/fc54a4044465d485d4eb55aa8dd81c0e952e8dd56528bc2874bcc67dcffbf3c8.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-2$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-2$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |"}] [{:db/id 17592186401006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6177b568-97a0-5f84-8134-18b2017427a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id 17592186401008} {:answer/id #uuid "825b5cbf-2e29-5716-8a33-a26e05345bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id 17592186401009} {:answer/id #uuid "c60f0014-ba02-5163-a857-2124830c98bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id 17592186401010} {:answer/id #uuid "f8eb0c0b-f6a6-50a4-a44e-7d150fa9f126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id 17592186401011} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce2b948-cc0f-5fa8-9825-7b8309ae482b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id 17592186401012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c60f0014-ba02-5163-a857-2124830c98bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id 17592186401010} :answer-field/id #uuid "63d7c60f-8a44-503d-b169-b9efe3a77416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "377570ea-bfa8-57c5-ba4b-badf50a02969" :question/math-academy-id "q-149594" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=-{x}^{3}y.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/93/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-{x}^{3}$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n \nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/fa/fa7402608c175237bfed11ddf032d867a0685e2ad5cf31f848809f796b705780.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |"}] [{:db/id 17592186401013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9ccfc2e-0db3-5e5a-a113-ee578e578636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id 17592186401015} {:answer/id #uuid "46620d78-5b91-5c4a-8c6d-533ad78b344e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id 17592186401016} {:answer/id #uuid "e0836e8c-818d-5b8d-afd0-edf4ef3be7f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id 17592186401017} {:answer/id #uuid "4b709ee7-e72e-5aa2-9c92-1da1a32