bf7812b7ae5db9a43efe59f4.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |"}] [{:db/id 17592186401020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c77b2f36-6be8-50c0-ae22-868fa6cf86a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401022} {:answer/id #uuid "09d3bae4-8fa4-5410-960c-88cdf9ccbafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401023} {:answer/id #uuid "e29d1f8d-e673-5fab-ab5a-b94d3c279c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401024} {:answer/id #uuid "d634400e-7fcc-5b2f-8882-b33255d21d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401025} {:answer/id #uuid "33483e89-3247-5b5b-b6c3-9a62773834b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c77b2f36-6be8-50c0-ae22-868fa6cf86a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401022} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d94100e-63ab-58cf-b2c0-0f485eafe140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a434871-4fea-52cd-80a4-1fd81c159fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15133" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a760f55b6437a39010e4d1a00bdb5fb5390d848dd74347e7736e2ac49cd562b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}+(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+144{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta ))}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d63901ad-848e-5f8f-b7f4-a6f0cabcd6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401029} {:answer/id #uuid "802cfcd0-a88b-5f37-b901-56f2fa1fec34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401030} {:answer/id #uuid "3d75caa2-eae0-5f0c-a250-6aa6ab5b7726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401031} {:answer/id #uuid "892fe229-a636-5af2-bb7c-28f7e30053e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401032} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7af40e-74ed-51e2-be9d-1cf5c95c728a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d7af40e-74ed-51e2-be9d-1cf5c95c728a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401033} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aecd8d8-445d-5023-ad24-6e7f3a0a5ec4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c659d13d-a024-51d5-b83b-b004bef94416" :question/math-academy-id "q-15136" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(1+{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186092577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbc2c061-b810-50f4-998c-b4c24f1e568e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+4\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401035} {:answer/id #uuid "2da42875-db42-5de8-9dff-ef55f911995f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401036} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0f9249-e37d-56d1-8c90-4ef1bb8df2ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{4{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401037} {:answer/id #uuid "21b19373-36de-5c05-9a8d-58dcde77b9ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401038} {:answer/id #uuid "293c2bba-fb9f-5a3b-a596-e4fc57546fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "293c2bba-fb9f-5a3b-a596-e4fc57546fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id 17592186401039} :answer-field/id #uuid "61740183-1d83-54ca-8ff3-51d0ccdbf570" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd646452-89ff-5f38-8817-208b877e41c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-15144" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b03822d65ec9f5bea3a1e47d25e4831470754fa09cef510e7c452cc292e9e40.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }+\\theta {e}^{\\theta }=(1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\theta {e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{((1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+(1+2\\theta +{\\theta }^{2}){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "925080f9-1488-5ed3-9988-57bac8e25aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+2t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401042} {:answer/id #uuid "8b14ae74-d72b-5c6f-bfb4-9fdf19b4b592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401043} {:answer/id #uuid "c911c453-c1b3-506d-a7de-c8341e32d5e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401044} {:answer/id #uuid "69aeb839-370f-503b-8dde-d870cf822808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401045} {:answer/id #uuid "e1915447-2ced-5598-8af5-d918f3a8a810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+24t+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c911c453-c1b3-506d-a7de-c8341e32d5e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401044} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac60323-fbe0-5435-bfb2-619a71c7a175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dddf8a10-d360-5144-aae4-5bd6e2bd6915" :question/math-academy-id "q-15174" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})=3{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})=3{t}^{2}-2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2}+2t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2}-2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+12{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}-12{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{18{t}^{4}+8{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b85309c-59d7-5810-84c4-7ea68f827ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401049} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd04e4b-26ba-5e0d-9091-43c5f06e6bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401050} {:answer/id #uuid "eb350998-6cb3-5e21-b128-0e21ffcf76a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401051} {:answer/id #uuid "9f297950-b005-5b22-b845-7190b859cf3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401052} {:answer/id #uuid "a99448ba-c640-5e57-a298-7dcd50a71e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb350998-6cb3-5e21-b128-0e21ffcf76a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401051} :answer-field/id #uuid "9286f2bc-6874-57c4-a822-f4b4566f80e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a2f2890-829e-5660-aa0e-71dea52c1f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-15178" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca290e8fd51edddf4381ec267b8e0a5692d0e52bd2a36b7d43ab89cbeb5cf00d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41c46a2e-4e00-5991-99c9-9dc68c9a9598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401056} {:answer/id #uuid "2499ce46-f979-5a96-92be-fd0c7ac31bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401057} {:answer/id #uuid "61eb1149-6ab5-5e3a-8377-404854e1b082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401058} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed903c6-dd0e-5b89-95d6-7cb6b868a842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401059} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b764bb-1a8d-5c44-bf30-3132c5443550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2499ce46-f979-5a96-92be-fd0c7ac31bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401057} :answer-field/id #uuid "d340f787-388c-5296-b3a4-86e39060db0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "243b404d-a4f2-57e8-bc56-d916439edf0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15182" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d683c3960705fa32d11826008a9844c51c8e59e38f5158fce7f4e44c31b97ca9.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "606fd559-6309-5ad0-9d39-b14bac651a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186401063} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7d81c0-3670-53fa-96c1-92db4f5667c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186401064} {:answer/id #uuid "b70dd52d-4f80-5155-8124-3b4a4b1be40d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186401065} {:answer/id #uuid "0d34d36a-3d69-5d6d-803b-25c205cff3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186401066} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c20d18-1824-50eb-b1e3-665bfefc7f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186401067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6c20d18-1824-50eb-b1e3-665bfefc7f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id 17592186401067} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3dd9699-b980-5dfd-8981-2e277c08e891" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "676c520e-2bd4-5a9f-a56a-7aedf1c83e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-15323" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ on $[1,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bead1cb0-40bc-5682-8aea-b1306552b221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186401070} {:answer/id #uuid "86056585-0e06-5b86-b0c0-36b6f0375c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186401071} {:answer/id #uuid "7c15ff06-b974-57e0-9d0d-91476444df9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186401072} {:answer/id #uuid "552b9d8c-6c80-5fa5-9717-bf514c6bbbca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id 17592186401073} {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186401074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186401074} :answer-field/id #uuid "4182a726-9587-5280-b0ae-9a47c4e9e265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16fc9f31-33f6-5972-9529-3afa5f1e1dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-15465" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1+0} \\\\  & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b60199c4-dc25-5568-af40-de39cd26589d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id 17592186401077} {:answer/id #uuid "41c812bd-cb3b-5fc5-aef0-fb585602e943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id 17592186401078} {:answer/id #uuid "ae37cc4a-dbda-5fe8-9ffa-a6b2a950bdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id 17592186401079} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fc02f7-9666-528e-9191-ebdcde2135fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id 17592186401080} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea8ed64-c406-5164-b57d-847952b47323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id 17592186401081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae37cc4a-dbda-5fe8-9ffa-a6b2a950bdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id 17592186401079} :answer-field/id #uuid "afbc7c1c-c03b-5382-bd6b-5a9beec491d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f9e0331-ffe6-5489-b436-faf1f0bea590" :question/math-academy-id "q-15469" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}+1,\\,y={t}^{4}+2{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t({t}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{4t({t}^{2}+1)}{6{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({t}^{2}+1)}{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2((1)^{2}+1)}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d256b930-0dee-50c4-bd81-41d64217fa7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id 17592186401084} {:answer/id #uuid "2759b4ff-6fe0-5744-9043-700096ff43f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,6-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,6+\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id 17592186401085} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3a7f86-7353-5871-ab3c-151f2b4eb6c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,5-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,5+\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id 17592186401086} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc673dc-55ce-532c-a658-37120012f1ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,5-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,5+\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id 17592186401087} {:answer/id #uuid "01d470c7-780c-5f9a-a462-fc902c6b68dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id 17592186401088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01d470c7-780c-5f9a-a462-fc902c6b68dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id 17592186401088} :answer-field/id #uuid "62252cab-6046-5d37-8592-a5f805f44339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b39c00d5-06a8-557a-8fa0-abcc975f62c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-15692" :question/problem "![](images/18/185393f07b920bf9b72799f1c17c4245017b500ff1ca92a0104fe95ce621bde4.png)\n\nFind the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=9,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (0-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ 4+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =9-4 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5}).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bbc1cc8-8336-57ba-beda-f232f5e9222e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id 17592186401091} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7b05bc-51cf-52c8-a8de-4a77f3929ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id 17592186401092} {:answer/id #uuid "56289067-d38c-5f93-8493-5a4b64b6dbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\pi y,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id 17592186401093} {:answer/id #uuid "b49fe862-0b93-571b-b320-daffcc013018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id 17592186401094} {:answer/id #uuid "94ddc772-4e3c-53f3-95c3-4e3a3d691c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id 17592186401095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b49fe862-0b93-571b-b320-daffcc013018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id 17592186401094} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f2a13be-e010-5c10-884d-b42e9a33ee3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "428cd283-358d-5b3a-86aa-024fc5c3885c" :question/math-academy-id "q-156978" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $r(y,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={sin}^{2}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Which of the following could be $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)={sin}^{2}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f67bd72e-fdc1-5a03-8447-bfd09a70f376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $Q$" :db/id 17592186401098} {:answer/id #uuid "01a67471-d12d-5153-bc2d-d06208bd68e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$" :db/id 17592186401099} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dbeaf3-ad0e-5cbe-a0fc-7d2689833b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $R$" :db/id 17592186401100} {:answer/id #uuid "66035904-de9d-5004-9133-f65b6dd564b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id 17592186401101} {:answer/id #uuid "f8434c0c-cb29-5697-82e4-05952a54d5fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and ${R}^{T}$" :db/id 17592186401102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66035904-de9d-5004-9133-f65b6dd564b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id 17592186401101} :answer-field/id #uuid "474be29c-51a5-5850-8515-5dee1fd67caa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f81ecce3-b49e-5261-aee2-21f0b16a1f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-157052" :question/problem "$$\n\\boxed{}\\,x=\\boxed{}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $R$ and ${Q}^{T}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fdcd9d7-d7a2-5fbc-b814-a5cdbab0aba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id 17592186401105} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac28ba0-e324-56db-ad83-179ae52ae559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and $Q$" :db/id 17592186401106} {:answer/id #uuid "7921d298-b867-528e-8983-2fecc690fc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$" :db/id 17592186401107} {:answer/id #uuid "b00b89ef-c0e1-594a-b11a-06139f3f787e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id 17592186401108} {:answer/id #uuid "7148d690-eb6d-590f-9666-9f4226696830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and ${R}^{-1}$" :db/id 17592186401109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fdcd9d7-d7a2-5fbc-b814-a5cdbab0aba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id 17592186401105} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d1a3359-653b-5fbe-99d8-82db0139d073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d32f936e-03bc-5f70-a00c-2926fd51fe56" :question/math-academy-id "q-158036" :question/problem "$$\nx=\\boxed{}\\,\\boxed{}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & =\\,\\,\\,{R}^{-1}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${R}^{-1}$ and ${Q}^{T}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c893f0-7ed9-50d0-9479-4bfb6c5c6906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id 17592186401112} {:answer/id #uuid "5e681a2e-8cd8-5ca9-b5d3-8777f4728c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id 17592186401113} {:answer/id #uuid "075c1360-2993-54ea-a5ab-60fe6486955e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id 17592186401114} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1e5ed6-4c6c-5020-bbc5-7c83a63fb116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id 17592186401115} {:answer/id #uuid "d396b6f8-3098-5eff-a463-bdfe23d65067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id 17592186401116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e681a2e-8cd8-5ca9-b5d3-8777f4728c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id 17592186401113} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c104c61-200a-5174-998d-683d5dd55c83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfa05974-d33e-5f3c-90f3-bf0b329bfc3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15805" :question/problem "![](images/de/de12c0b5be8318cc21c62615d24125998c22d55d37abafdee37c103c3840cb71.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ intersects the line $x-3y=2$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4)}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{10}{3})}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+10 & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-4,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-4,-2).$ Next, we plug in $x=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=2$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ at the points $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186193185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8042759c-34e5-5005-b223-6ae432924393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id 17592186401118} {:answer/id #uuid "005523ea-9cd4-5f90-a22e-334d6a25f40b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id 17592186401119} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcc1510-f906-5cf2-aee6-a593897199ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<y<1" :db/id 17592186401120} {:answer/id #uuid "54b76232-1674-5242-9acf-123f7bdcf11e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<y<0" :db/id 17592186401121} {:answer/id #uuid "3512bb23-ec85-559a-bb44-2c0d0c3ebdfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le y\\le 0" :db/id 17592186401122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b76232-1674-5242-9acf-123f7bdcf11e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<y<0" :db/id 17592186401121} :answer-field/id #uuid "071a160d-4402-56e8-b9f3-e34425bb2388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "846d6059-ad4f-5e01-8a65-610c7d23e3a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-158314" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b95c538613ee8672e0f1cac970b7af230ec40bf3075e609b1e03f7cad4382fb1.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/19/19e8e52881066864cba562ed2a8cdc2944b9dae416c7124fdee207cdad1d8bdf.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/90/907e996fb7aea36e0c924feb0f5d9dc6804c768be775953d277e84b0145ef5a0.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186152438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a5f50b7-1c4b-5f7a-b602-f12beaae89e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=49\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id 17592186401124} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c1e0e9-11c9-5b29-a0fa-ad459d001600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id 17592186401125} {:answer/id #uuid "c7046323-aec7-5353-9ec7-55a1fb762409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id 17592186401126} {:answer/id #uuid "12304df3-e4be-5122-93c7-3516406cb605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id 17592186401127} {:answer/id #uuid "7476a25d-d04e-55b7-83f1-d52ec7ba798e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id 17592186401128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12304df3-e4be-5122-93c7-3516406cb605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id 17592186401127} :answer-field/id #uuid "500e2e4d-f73d-516b-9ffd-1e74864efbb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46c88046-0f62-50bb-8a31-ae13a6c535ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-15856" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c46972a3968d7110cb08d4b16bfff9267e49b102cf005cdbb21a3630d15c49d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=7$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d17359d3-8c16-5f6a-b127-4635699145de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne 2x}" :db/id 17592186401131} {:answer/id #uuid "723d1468-5b49-57b7-aca8-480bc0d17b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<2x}" :db/id 17592186401132} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee0d3a5-a4a1-5cee-a062-442dab6411d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>2x}" :db/id 17592186401133} {:answer/id #uuid "ab4e594f-0e4b-5943-92bb-bf8c40588e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id 17592186401134} {:answer/id #uuid "20fdbcce-ba79-5860-9e27-a2583b78aa49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge 2x}" :db/id 17592186401135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab4e594f-0e4b-5943-92bb-bf8c40588e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id 17592186401134} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a4d4ae3-6796-52c1-a3b7-76a9cd48622e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3d7c34b-eed1-5181-a45d-9a598cc7cc31" :question/math-academy-id "q-158569" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)={2}^{y-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- $y-2x$ is defined for all $x$ and $y,$ and\n\n- ${2}^{g(x,y)}$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, ${2}^{y-2x}$ is defined for all values of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the domain of $f$ is the entire $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id 17592186401136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9b6b53-5c92-51b1-b36f-2a3cc651f319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 8 & 25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186401138} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcb6806-97c3-5c5b-a067-e8db96db6a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186401139} {:answer/id #uuid "c58e92d6-356e-5987-82a0-c4ca864d02f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186401140} {:answer/id #uuid "2efd5c91-0f33-5144-8814-8a5bf4712f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186401141} {:answer/id #uuid "47e7f5fa-1b69-57b5-bdb7-eab59dcc0807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 22 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 17 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 9 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 13 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 25 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186401142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2efd5c91-0f33-5144-8814-8a5bf4712f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186401141} :answer-field/id #uuid "55328507-f46f-5e80-84e7-a9cf12738872" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "646237af-1831-56a9-a4f0-279fefdaeaf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159730" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$22$** | **$17$** | **$15$** | **$11$** | **$8$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$9$** | **$13$** | **$25$** |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.  Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$22$** | **$17$** | **$15$** | **$11$** | **$8$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$9$** | **$13$** | **$25$** |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 8 & 25\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id 17592186401143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf99414e-f1cf-528f-adbb-407cf6e82c4c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Linear Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ad4e4e-04cb-51fe-abe1-4e867e431a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection in the line $y=x+\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id 17592186401145} {:answer/id #uuid "5a15a75a-e128-599b-b3b9-5645e7305697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection in the line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x$" :db/id 17592186401146} {:answer/id #uuid "f0452ac4-63df-52cb-a5ba-4c3d6f3508db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id 17592186401147} {:answer/id #uuid "4708c967-b8d8-54c4-ada0-7e3e2fab1f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id 17592186401148} {:answer/id #uuid "d94c5e9f-ee52-566e-9640-baf243e6ea0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection in the line $y=\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id 17592186401149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0452ac4-63df-52cb-a5ba-4c3d6f3508db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id 17592186401147} :answer-field/id #uuid "7629eca5-066a-5cf0-88fc-0ed79060b5bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0bb92e93-2cdf-508a-a231-165363849a16" :question/math-academy-id "q-159764" :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=\\frac{2}{3}.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85936915-4c9c-572c-af99-025fec0f7570" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Image of a Line Segment Under the Action of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136e59b4-89b9-509f-b353-97e57960580a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-5,7)$" :db/id 17592186401152} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f7fd7a-68ed-5ca3-93c6-f651afc3e675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ and $(5,-7)$" :db/id 17592186401153} {:answer/id #uuid "ff57b12a-81c8-5a7a-bb27-04eafe1c6040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(-5,7)$" :db/id 17592186401154} {:answer/id #uuid "5f869935-6196-536e-a49b-7e27025831a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ and $(-5,5)$" :db/id 17592186401155} {:answer/id #uuid "bad06300-be92-5a4d-91eb-84900e7f65b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-5,5)$" :db/id 17592186401156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "136e59b4-89b9-509f-b353-97e57960580a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-5,7)$" :db/id 17592186401152} :answer-field/id #uuid "51865f85-e4e0-52bf-a6e9-60f97627cefe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9f71d03-299c-533e-bd5f-2dfe8453c281" :question/math-academy-id "q-159797" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ has endpoints at $A(1,1)$ and $B(-1,2).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}}$, the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are ${A}^{′}(2,2)$ and ${B}^{′}(-5,7).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fe5d8aa-8a79-5de4-8566-1432a640d78f" :knowledge-point/title "Equating Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Number to Solve For an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b234a49-b204-5ed4-bfaa-41d6080ef1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{4},b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id 17592186401159} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6d77b0-4258-5c91-8afc-7ef838e14fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\sqrt{3},b=-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id 17592186401160} {:answer/id #uuid "d2159356-f202-541b-9892-eebd1e60d1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{4},b=-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186401161} {:answer/id #uuid "d6145a7d-35ad-5386-85f1-fee66f6da6b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\sqrt{3},b=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id 17592186401162} {:answer/id #uuid "5360b829-a541-532f-8d6c-81155ed6dcd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{4},b=\\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186401163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5360b829-a541-532f-8d6c-81155ed6dcd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{4},b=\\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186401163} :answer-field/id #uuid "76c64cb5-e76d-53eb-a0e8-6195df2d94ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78111624-f670-5df8-b6e2-bb5a5dde19ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-160013" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{3}{4}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}=a+b\\text{i}$, calculate the value of $a$ and the value of $b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we equate the real parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we equate the imaginary parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $b=\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bbe2fe7-b69c-54f3-9f1c-3369e3f1395d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Unknowns By Computing a Product and Equating Real and Imaginary Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71361279-b548-5a5f-a16d-dcbf0424a5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2$, $q=3$" :db/id 17592186401166} {:answer/id #uuid "76723f95-9757-537d-ba64-6f70bb2a0084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=8$, $q=2$" :db/id 17592186401167} {:answer/id #uuid "2783fb88-e21b-5c34-a806-1f0e4be8a043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=-4$, $q=2$" :db/id 17592186401168} {:answer/id #uuid "f9389cd2-410b-5bd7-bb2d-afd430456c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=-3$, $q=2$" :db/id 17592186401169} {:answer/id #uuid "6176aa1c-2946-59f3-b638-214d03147fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2$, $q=1$" :db/id 17592186401170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76723f95-9757-537d-ba64-6f70bb2a0084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=8$, $q=2$" :db/id 17592186401167} :answer-field/id #uuid "e20e0aba-a96a-54f7-bcde-ebba07a66ed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2bd6d81b-eb1e-5f54-9fb0-b04584c1dccf" :question/math-academy-id "q-160037" :question/problem "Given that $3+5\\text{i}=\\frac{p+\\text{i}q}{1-\\text{i}},$ calculate $p$ and $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $1-\\text{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+5\\text{i} & =\\frac{p+\\text{i}q}{1-\\text{i}} \\\\ (3+5\\text{i})(1-\\text{i}) & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 3-3\\text{i}+5\\text{i}-5{\\text{i}}^{2} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 3+5+2\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 8+2\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n\n$$\n8=p.\n$$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=8$ and $q=2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9f40a0e-83f3-5814-b9a1-8b1265fe93cc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Unknowns By Computing a Quotient and Equating Real and Imaginary Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1d54338-26b9-548b-83c0-2a886b9ea3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{3}{2},q=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id 17592186401173} {:answer/id #uuid "3d44482d-1c30-5ad6-854c-691cae820272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{2},q=-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id 17592186401174} {:answer/id #uuid "1b7850c0-574e-58be-a128-8a2349e114cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{5},q=-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id 17592186401175} {:answer/id #uuid "e5144ebf-c2b3-5349-b8ce-5873d55100c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{2}{5},q=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186401176} {:answer/id #uuid "930309ca-50d5-5254-aa8c-b2c15baae0ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{2},q=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186401177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "930309ca-50d5-5254-aa8c-b2c15baae0ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{2},q=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186401177} :answer-field/id #uuid "56ae83c2-5ded-53be-a27e-61769cd01afe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "050685d3-106a-556e-94cf-f2ab6b33d435" :question/math-academy-id "q-160049" :question/problem "Given that $5=(p+q\\text{i})(3+\\text{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $3+\\text{i}$ to isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(p+q\\text{i})(3+\\text{i}) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3+\\text{i}} & =p+q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(5)(3-\\text{i})}{(3+\\text{i})(3-\\text{i})} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{15-5\\text{i}}{{3}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{15-5\\text{i}}{9+1} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{15-5\\text{i}}{10} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i} & =p+q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $\\frac{3}{2}=p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $-\\frac{1}{2}=q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $q=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "499fe0bc-77df-53df-8493-7d4c107031d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eafa79bc-674a-58d4-b671-ca48a2d8af37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no equilibrium solutions" :db/id 17592186401180} {:answer/id #uuid "bf12bc70-5702-514d-a81e-85a39e9d74bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ only" :db/id 17592186401181} {:answer/id #uuid "a206451d-8757-5268-a092-52b0e62ff345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y(x)=0$ only" :db/id 17592186401182} {:answer/id #uuid "1237e62c-9ff7-54ad-b0a2-1816c8134906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=-\\pi $ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\pi $" :db/id 17592186401183} {:answer/id #uuid "65a7f74e-7edd-5a89-8720-8a9b642df82e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=0$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\pi $" :db/id 17592186401184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eafa79bc-674a-58d4-b671-ca48a2d8af37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no equilibrium solutions" :db/id 17592186401180} :answer-field/id #uuid "68ec482b-0fe8-570d-98b4-c811d283be06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a785f16-be14-5d27-b10a-d2d350268268" :question/math-academy-id "q-160429" :question/problem "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2,$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡c+2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡c & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, note that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡c|\\le 1$ for all $c.$ Therefore, the above equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the differential equation has no equilibrium points."}] [{:db/id 17592186401185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfe7ddf8-1d90-5732-b4e0-9b6cb53cfed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-2v.$" :db/id 17592186401187} {:answer/id #uuid "6b355cde-9299-54ff-9082-f28b3a088a6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=-2u.$" :db/id 17592186401188} {:answer/id #uuid "a417c7cf-6062-5b00-9bc3-e0de419c5534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=2u.$" :db/id 17592186401189} {:answer/id #uuid "ac39e8c8-f1ca-5eec-9d60-f35deacbe886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-v.$" :db/id 17592186401190} {:answer/id #uuid "0f39009f-2aaf-5fb6-b951-14bb531af992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/id 17592186401191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f39009f-2aaf-5fb6-b951-14bb531af992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/id 17592186401191} :answer-field/id #uuid "003d00c4-a45e-580b-a2b2-1a8ab41f3386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6940edb0-4ff9-5079-afa0-d099bcae85dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160465" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2v-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ u & =v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=v$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id 17592186401192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "879a74f3-cdb2-5fc9-83e0-82f1953090a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=6u.$" :db/id 17592186401194} {:answer/id #uuid "b598f6a3-a6bb-5202-8ed8-ac9c42eb7279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $2{v}^{2}-3{u}^{2}=1.$" :db/id 17592186401195} {:answer/id #uuid "bca486aa-25ef-5c7c-9609-2ed1a4c5553a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=6.$" :db/id 17592186401196} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3464e3-f53d-53d8-877c-0141084f3eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $2v=3{u}^{2}.$" :db/id 17592186401197} {:answer/id #uuid "14c27fbe-cfe3-5924-a55c-3737ccd260a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/id 17592186401198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14c27fbe-cfe3-5924-a55c-3737ccd260a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/id 17592186401198} :answer-field/id #uuid "753118cd-0a71-5bb3-aaee-283497ceb3e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f05de000-4a38-5b78-b8b6-ab15cb5a35af" :question/math-academy-id "q-160473" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 4v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 1-4v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6u-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 6u-4v & =0 \\\\ v & =\\frac{3}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=\\frac{3}{2}u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id 17592186401199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b93a84-d633-56d5-acf0-64e865f690ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id 17592186401201} {:answer/id #uuid "a58ae1f1-f6a6-59d0-9751-fca06c5ac749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id 17592186401202} {:answer/id #uuid "062653e1-93bc-5e7e-a802-152e2de8a4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id 17592186401203} {:answer/id #uuid "d40073c8-1340-52d4-b3a8-b1c6162defe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id 17592186401204} {:answer/id #uuid "37e2e5c1-32d5-5a19-92b2-99790aa5b623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id 17592186401205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "062653e1-93bc-5e7e-a802-152e2de8a4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id 17592186401203} :answer-field/id #uuid "1398b2db-d522-591e-9af9-65f757fcf9ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f849a8c-d5a9-5291-8dbf-bee7c6e87ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160608" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v-{u}^{2},\\,y=u+v.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v-{u}^{2},\\,u+v) \\\\  & =(v-{1}^{2},\\,1+v) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,v+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=v+1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)=t+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,1)$ and $(0,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/f3/f304c409cfc62de3d296690c0acd2cec5b9726c03d8a196ed69de36d8ebbefd2.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186172381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5256297-c928-54e3-bf4d-35f5734f5632" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c6491ad-31df-5c44-8510-45289f4f4d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{u+v}" :db/id 17592186401207} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2a58c0-318a-559b-8de4-48cf1599122b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id 17592186401208} {:answer/id #uuid "66a3fc66-6953-52e2-8b17-93c3ff13ddf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id 17592186401209} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fed922-03a1-59c5-b2d8-bcf5a4514733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id 17592186401210} {:answer/id #uuid "ecef412c-1fbd-55f7-a2eb-dcc4e3d8418d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id 17592186401211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecef412c-1fbd-55f7-a2eb-dcc4e3d8418d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id 17592186401211} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b5472ad-00ad-5af8-bfaf-97021ee46355" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25e44fde-444d-5880-ba2e-43dd4bc039db" :question/math-academy-id "q-160711" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xyz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & v \\\\ 1 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-v)^{2}+(-u)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xyz)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =u\\cdot v\\cdot uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186167132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cee874e3-9f03-5ca9-8149-5c55b3896851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy-yz+2xz" :db/id 17592186401213} {:answer/id #uuid "395d9de5-d538-52d5-94b9-1ccab518693d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+2yz" :db/id 17592186401214} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ff34b0-c12c-5102-97f3-b0e91c2d28e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy+yz+xz" :db/id 17592186401215} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdf9b87-84fe-5783-b107-f4fa60e017e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+3yz" :db/id 17592186401216} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0c95b-6fc9-5c49-82c2-19c95577125c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+yz" :db/id 17592186401217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0c95b-6fc9-5c49-82c2-19c95577125c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+yz" :db/id 17592186401217} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8eb37c1-2270-5997-8c21-89c990ae0c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7492e5fe-6cc9-5d4b-8d1f-20d212147cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-160838" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{(2y)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(2x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2yz-yz+2{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac04977-1a37-5d3c-9110-290be918a633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}" :db/id 17592186401220} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7eb081-9f2c-5f53-a5b0-e0278d5a43e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2" :db/id 17592186401221} {:answer/id #uuid "8b485d3f-8ad7-53a4-b507-d079c7107b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}" :db/id 17592186401222} {:answer/id #uuid "0e733731-6884-58f7-9e73-fde236398200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id 17592186401223} {:answer/id #uuid "a04db056-3485-5f6c-b6b6-d2d4f8e80aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id 17592186401224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b485d3f-8ad7-53a4-b507-d079c7107b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}" :db/id 17592186401222} :answer-field/id #uuid "09e4fe92-85aa-505b-a8c5-4df7656265ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd6b23cf-0f4d-571e-a46d-8551c4a4d4b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161072" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={x}^{2}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{2},$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2{x}^{3},\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "419c0982-db99-5289-b52f-a761ea3d1bae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-x-2{z}^{2}" :db/id 17592186401227} {:answer/id #uuid "d412c461-4939-5aa5-9ddd-df78a8e85bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+{z}^{2}" :db/id 17592186401228} {:answer/id #uuid "a93a034f-199c-5c52-af3b-9d0a37596a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+{z}^{2}" :db/id 17592186401229} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbb580a-2ddc-5e43-a8f4-c46a6440cff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-x" :db/id 17592186401230} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a49a16-7b19-549f-8e35-18199cc77301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2{z}^{2}" :db/id 17592186401231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfbb580a-2ddc-5e43-a8f4-c46a6440cff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-x" :db/id 17592186401230} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21eed7a-f07f-5d01-b24f-3b6ba15f83ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd68aee4-128c-55a1-8849-b847e40933c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161198" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{z}^{2}-x+2(y-{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y-x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2y-x}{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}2y-x\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed482a3a-a562-53c9-9952-b7f4ae64a9f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3})" :db/id 17592186401234} {:answer/id #uuid "292ba469-4e9c-5812-b40c-813e27620124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3y}(-{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id 17592186401235} {:answer/id #uuid "1139e147-84fe-5bb5-a30a-874f5c401de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y)" :db/id 17592186401236} {:answer/id #uuid "25a694b0-85e4-50a9-9c0d-fb4f6db18268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id 17592186401237} {:answer/id #uuid "60287cb2-73a4-5d70-9afa-49321e3ddf3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3x}({x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id 17592186401238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a694b0-85e4-50a9-9c0d-fb4f6db18268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id 17592186401237} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb2d063b-2ed3-5177-a74e-32306bee1975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8e5c369-0d01-5527-87a6-9d6d08f8cf0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161314" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-x+y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y(-x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fef4addc-f714-5c73-ba79-b8bbf017a6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186401241} {:answer/id #uuid "32ada5ea-3508-5fad-8e67-d2275d868693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{4}{y}^{4}+{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186401242} {:answer/id #uuid "0adb517f-11c6-5796-89c8-e402d79f5011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186401243} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c7988a-9786-511c-9fd8-d31cb890bb80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-4{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4x+4y}" :db/id 17592186401244} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5ced82-50c7-5506-b6d6-5d386e556ca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186401245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0adb517f-11c6-5796-89c8-e402d79f5011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186401243} :answer-field/id #uuid "63dd6eb1-0216-5ca0-946f-dc8a7cfe578a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5d98cd9-310e-5d64-ad53-16fad07f2de6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161320" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2x)}^{2}+{(-2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd2fdb-4cae-5f0b-bea0-e845d51190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral Using a Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6ce404-ebcc-51cc-943b-4e4e79eb614d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2R$ and $-2P$" :db/id 17592186401248} {:answer/id #uuid "44cf614b-483e-57c6-b48c-bdea43bdf8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$" :db/id 17592186401249} {:answer/id #uuid "920e8870-c9e3-5c16-bd67-bc6987066749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $-6R$" :db/id 17592186401250} {:answer/id #uuid "7051dd66-a8a1-5bd2-a47a-cbbfa2e9ad6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $-R$" :db/id 17592186401251} {:answer/id #uuid "23d98ea3-b274-5a12-a721-30dfede024ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $6R$" :db/id 17592186401252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d98ea3-b274-5a12-a721-30dfede024ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $6R$" :db/id 17592186401252} :answer-field/id #uuid "d028cbae-7e56-54b5-84a6-c407d88b99d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "75438633-2fed-5dc6-8f93-bd3571d872a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161455" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y=2x{z}^{3}$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\boxed{}\\,{z}^{3}+Q-\\boxed{}\\,x{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=2x{z}^{3},$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,2x{z}^{3},z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,2{z}^{3},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,6x{z}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2{z}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6x{z}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨2{z}^{3},\\,-1,\\,6x{z}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨-2{z}^{3},\\,1,\\,-6x{z}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,R\\,⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{z}^{3},\\,1,\\,-6x{z}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2P\\,{z}^{3}+Q-6R\\,x{z}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd2fdb-4cae-5f0b-bea0-e845d51190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral Using a Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4fc5fe-cb91-578b-8dbf-4a5ffd8c3fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂Q" :db/id 17592186401255} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9077fe-1593-50c2-8f89-3d6b0dcabdfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂z" :db/id 17592186401256} {:answer/id #uuid "1c8485ee-e4b0-5c7e-96b8-4bdcc10e86bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂z" :db/id 17592186401257} {:answer/id #uuid "09583e22-9aba-582a-80e6-e8808ccbeebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id 17592186401258} {:answer/id #uuid "3581b172-6284-5dbd-9767-909757324a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id 17592186401259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3581b172-6284-5dbd-9767-909757324a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id 17592186401259} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee6b27a-bcd5-52e8-9493-e839011ba707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "417f7e3b-659c-5f7d-913c-decf384148f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161457" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{}{∂x}-\\frac{∂g}{}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd2fdb-4cae-5f0b-bea0-e845d51190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral Using a Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffab214-a531-5f16-9548-4aba64a14e09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂z$ and $∂x$" :db/id 17592186401262} {:answer/id #uuid "27be7768-bf01-5a25-8f76-a7b3aa6827e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂z$" :db/id 17592186401263} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b36ca-8d50-5d13-b2d4-01dff2d1189e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂y$ and $∂z$" :db/id 17592186401264} {:answer/id #uuid "8c295efa-f53d-5757-9ea8-624ce01afe74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂y$" :db/id 17592186401265} {:answer/id #uuid "401f8a2b-7596-5052-8ec8-449369280a45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂y$ and $∂x$" :db/id 17592186401266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c295efa-f53d-5757-9ea8-624ce01afe74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂y$" :db/id 17592186401265} :answer-field/id #uuid "0585e533-2d5e-5fa5-b53e-85ce1c70e498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2128236-dd4e-5cec-8d28-c07554df0f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161463" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{\\boxed{}}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\boxed{}}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa6b152-86d3-5f96-8a94-57f86a17a84d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Over a Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aab621f-8cde-5ff7-83da-d05fc13356ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id 17592186401269} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1077bb-f87e-5cca-9aef-16e4fb33aef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id 17592186401270} {:answer/id #uuid "2adea1b0-35c6-5e84-940d-c02c34a5479d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id 17592186401271} {:answer/id #uuid "6170ad11-316c-53bb-b473-67e1f8f56f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id 17592186401272} {:answer/id #uuid "cccdc272-4ba4-53bf-8e7e-06633eea609a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e-\\frac{13}{8}" :db/id 17592186401273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2adea1b0-35c6-5e84-940d-c02c34a5479d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id 17592186401271} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d473e4-fb62-570e-a504-252ea46fe0d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "682d2ef7-9cff-5a52-921d-70cd49bbb080" :question/math-academy-id "q-161476" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x-y}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}{e}^{x-y}={e}^{x-y} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}{e}^{x-y}=-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩{|}_{z={e}^{x-y}} \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,xy{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,{e}^{x-y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{{e}^{y-x}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{xy{e}^{y-x}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{e}^{x-y}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{e}^{y-x}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-(xy{e}^{y-x})\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-y+\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-{e}^{x-y}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-1+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-x+{e}^{x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{8}-1+e)-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{8}+e\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "388d2b25-435c-5296-9aa6-b2d86268ab07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<y\\le x" :db/id 17592186401276} {:answer/id #uuid "f7697d7e-3437-54cb-8f6c-2a92ca54ced0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\le y\\le x" :db/id 17592186401277} {:answer/id #uuid "715a11d5-48d4-58bf-b2c8-7bc968bb12ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\le y<x" :db/id 17592186401278} {:answer/id #uuid "6585c463-6545-5cb0-b223-cfd5eb13f4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x<y<x" :db/id 17592186401279} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc09c1e-549e-5605-9dc7-40a23cf11591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<y<x" :db/id 17592186401280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6585c463-6545-5cb0-b223-cfd5eb13f4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x<y<x" :db/id 17592186401279} :answer-field/id #uuid "41473911-1989-5683-9728-285b3c146754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cae3814-ea99-54e9-86e7-c0bc019bdf37" :question/math-academy-id "q-161718" :question/problem "![](images/af/af1e6e7b384732a527fc77e9729c6e02aaa288d4076c030c9fae58742cb10f74.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a0b92621d83d6395926a75b5cae34f479a8b9e64dfe82711094c3724b3b3220.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3ddef68bf4b4284c1b68896920fddb8d7fb0e70eba15dee103cdbc0134a9ed20.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,-x<y<x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f14d31-90ea-5326-97dc-45022d0835ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u+v-2w=0.$" :db/id 17592186401283} {:answer/id #uuid "b740b568-485c-5311-a423-33ea2f3eeffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the elliptic cylinder ${u}^{2}+2{w}^{2}=1.$" :db/id 17592186401284} {:answer/id #uuid "a6795027-5482-5bff-8214-2dd2cc097fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={u}^{2}.$" :db/id 17592186401285} {:answer/id #uuid "5adbc1f1-362c-537c-bb50-2cdecc09b123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$ plane." :db/id 17592186401286} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a89ced-7070-57be-88cf-4d4b894bd3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2w.$" :db/id 17592186401287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3a89ced-7070-57be-88cf-4d4b894bd3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2w.$" :db/id 17592186401287} :answer-field/id #uuid "65676948-b2fa-585d-8619-ca89f50c6f34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8752c18-2285-5bd2-a3b2-ba4d0420bd04" :question/math-academy-id "q-161814" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+2w \\\\ v-u \\\\ uw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=uw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ w & 0 & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ w & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(u-0)+2(0-w) \\\\  & =u-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ u-2w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u-2w=0$ defines the plane $u=2w.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76c85a4-c631-5c36-941d-c14613479d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id 17592186401290} {:answer/id #uuid "10a3627e-14c0-553d-8005-b8e65337d0b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=-2{v}^{2}.$" :db/id 17592186401291} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ffd8d2-f852-57f7-9970-8454309f0281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $2u+v+w=0.$" :db/id 17592186401292} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fe9b5d-4fbe-56d7-9627-c5c1df64a715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=-2u.$" :db/id 17592186401293} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cbb05e-806b-56aa-8136-04b088a6dc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w={u}^{2}.$" :db/id 17592186401294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b76c85a4-c631-5c36-941d-c14613479d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id 17592186401290} :answer-field/id #uuid "175d9200-8100-5e54-b984-b7451df1967c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc380b3f-d92e-5c45-8651-fac240787b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-161816" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{u+w} \\\\ uv+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={e}^{u+w},\\,z(u,v,w)=uv+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u+w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{u+w} & 0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ v & u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(0-u{e}^{u+w}) \\\\  & =-u{e}^{2u+w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -u{e}^{2u+w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."}] [{:db/id 17592186129532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59ecd82e-c85b-5221-81f4-5aa437d23a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id 17592186401296} {:answer/id #uuid "1680c5f5-1c51-52cd-a03a-ad30d3c07c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=2{w}^{2}.$" :db/id 17592186401297} {:answer/id #uuid "92c1139b-4ea6-5a6e-91ff-1cd1ee2688f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2v.$" :db/id 17592186401298} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea440aa-4bbc-5828-a750-3ec0ce76a35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$" :db/id 17592186401299} {:answer/id #uuid "b1928d32-8a83-5bc0-a68f-3b8d2a17bd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=w.$" :db/id 17592186401300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea440aa-4bbc-5828-a750-3ec0ce76a35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$" :db/id 17592186401299} :answer-field/id #uuid "17caa721-6c88-5948-86f1-24aa0f77f9b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "685e989d-8721-5a8c-a4d8-0e44efe4bc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161819" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{w}^{2} \\\\ w{e}^{u} \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-{w}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u},\\,z(u,v,w)=u-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 & -2w \\\\ w{e}^{u} & 0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2w|\\begin{aligned}w{e}^{u} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u(0+{e}^{u})-2w(-w{e}^{u}-0) \\\\  & =2u{e}^{u}+2{w}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u) & =0 \\\\ {w}^{2}+u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${w}^{2}+u=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdff4620-93ff-550e-afdc-c665cb0060db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 9,\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }" :db/id 17592186401303} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa29027-2d4f-5367-917f-52c169af3941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id 17592186401304} {:answer/id #uuid "9a681f25-a3d3-54aa-8220-4047401f3201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id 17592186401305} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb3ebdb-8986-5120-bc0e-293da834f2da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 9,\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" :db/id 17592186401306} {:answer/id #uuid "ec03a2a0-b115-5ed7-a6b0-20a8edc98e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id 17592186401307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fa29027-2d4f-5367-917f-52c169af3941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id 17592186401304} :answer-field/id #uuid "07b9fac4-36f4-5471-8875-46834f232265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0831acd-ddeb-5e04-811c-4d36ad3d1496" :question/math-academy-id "q-161827" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f7ed51c1273b73243beb574535686f4ce3498b28c84cbe76833a3096674792c.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/d3/d338181a6631c513330ef2dd3899cfb9b01cb6f4c4ff7d9cd65038b381e7fec4.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,3]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a898f345-9210-5976-9a42-f415a0e7eaf5" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables in a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e70215a-59ba-5fc4-8869-08fd079debf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id 17592186401310} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6baa46-2362-5360-8c5f-7031d62e1411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id 17592186401311} {:answer/id #uuid "7adad8b8-d3ef-52ef-b487-e4018ac1507b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡(u-v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id 17592186401312} {:answer/id #uuid "06bed278-63d1-5662-b5c3-13bc65e1ffc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id 17592186401313} {:answer/id #uuid "03678aa6-e1ae-5bec-8d2a-faf8e1de8ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡(uv)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-u" :db/id 17592186401314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e70215a-59ba-5fc4-8869-08fd079debf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id 17592186401310} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b627816-1293-53a9-a8e8-93bf2fb0fcef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4004275e-59e4-54d2-bbaa-29203ac29308" :question/math-academy-id "q-161879" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z-3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df4d06f-b85b-5df3-8dc8-2bb8f495582f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y" :db/id 17592186401317} {:answer/id #uuid "1a198055-107c-5684-9d14-e7c623094522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id 17592186401318} {:answer/id #uuid "4925a5be-07f7-506e-b9ae-faf7c7f0dd10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id 17592186401319} {:answer/id #uuid "83f96e24-8d6b-5eba-89cf-5696e34a9895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y" :db/id 17592186401320} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa2c4ff-8c1b-548e-992e-27aec7e25032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id 17592186401321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83f96e24-8d6b-5eba-89cf-5696e34a9895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y" :db/id 17592186401320} :answer-field/id #uuid "531e02f0-754c-57e9-98aa-5097314bafb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f21c0998-8903-5ed2-945d-a8c8324dedb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161902" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,y,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9$ and the plane $z=y-x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=y-x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-x)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=y,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-1)-y\\cdot 1+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower cone ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9)=\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9)=\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=y,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y\\cdot \\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186106074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e72b8eba-d97d-557f-a8cb-b177b5a694e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id 17592186401323} {:answer/id #uuid "239808c3-6662-5144-b216-bf28876d8c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id 17592186401324} {:answer/id #uuid "d95c8151-cc29-5467-9bd0-4f7145939834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id 17592186401325} {:answer/id #uuid "a539e88c-cfb9-5231-b8f6-481fa85848fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id 17592186401326} {:answer/id #uuid "ace8a67a-e74f-5f27-9582-9a2b3428a829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id 17592186401327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e72b8eba-d97d-557f-a8cb-b177b5a694e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id 17592186401323} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd41be4-e0dd-5cb0-830a-bc68816c31ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba678a53-c013-5d44-b391-2d5cc0a9cc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-162150" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id 17592186401328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e986629f-0cb1-5b73-9387-7e5c6bf680ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p={x}^{2},\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id 17592186401330} {:answer/id #uuid "7747ad8c-9dab-5071-9712-612e09ef49ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{x},\\,\\,\\,q=e,\\,\\,\\,r=x" :db/id 17592186401331} {:answer/id #uuid "365f610c-9ced-572c-8125-8961f4f896a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=2x,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r={e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186401332} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0cb55f-4047-582e-8d1c-338eb7a2d14f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p={x}^{2},\\,\\,\\,q=e,\\,\\,\\,r=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id 17592186401333} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2f91e1-1b84-5255-9e54-b6238d498937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=2x,\\,\\,\\,q=e,\\,\\,\\,r={e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186401334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe0cb55f-4047-582e-8d1c-338eb7a2d14f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p={x}^{2},\\,\\,\\,q=e,\\,\\,\\,r=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id 17592186401333} :answer-field/id #uuid "06475241-fc29-5967-a1f6-50975e64e9e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a894708-53b5-506d-832d-c14b94f740a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162322" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{{e}^{z}}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{p}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{q}{\\int }_{r}^{1}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(x)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(x,z)}^{{u}_{4}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le {e}^{z},\\,\\,{z}^{2}-1\\le y\\le x+z}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{{e}^{z}}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(x)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41b06d98498273a03b9df8eeaa60f4e74cb00168e8dd0b3b395c5b0a23518e57.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le {e}^{z}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](images/07/07a37b90df8b37d07c40af03ecba8e665797cfff368758db7ae17e45f11683cf.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $z=z(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $z=0\\,$ and $\\,z={x}^{2}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $z=z(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,$ and $\\,z=1.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}^{1}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f703bd0-2dd8-50a8-84be-59480e209b05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r={z}^{2}-4" :db/id 17592186401337} {:answer/id #uuid "16f6fbe8-a535-5960-a6c7-b5dd576f6b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z" :db/id 17592186401338} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9b1939-7256-56eb-8c7a-9b505da6122c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\,q=1,\\,\\,\\,r={z}^{2}-4" :db/id 17592186401339} {:answer/id #uuid "e24c4541-f269-56ad-b95a-224e52a4e088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\,q=3,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z" :db/id 17592186401340} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a313c4-3307-5cf2-9cf8-be47e0fcc064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=1,\\,\\,\\,r=z-2" :db/id 17592186401341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16f6fbe8-a535-5960-a6c7-b5dd576f6b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z" :db/id 17592186401338} :answer-field/id #uuid "f079fa33-36ea-54da-8c9a-cfed3ba497cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f9c68a00-265c-5c7e-8372-6f380b398f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162332" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}-1}^{2-y/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-1}^{p}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{q}{\\int }_{0}^{r}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(xy,z)}^{{u}_{4}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2},\\,\\,-2z\\le x\\le y+3}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}-1}^{2-y/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4de9d9df51efcec67253a1c7118466f29459ffd5c9136c144d33500ec5557bc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](images/18/18bb3e55f076e022176bb60df27d5665ed2d91d75d618969c04ce192378db922.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=4-2z.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-2z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2z}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69eaa3fe-4c03-532c-9d1a-cd84b8703ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{4}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401344} {:answer/id #uuid "460112aa-e36b-52a6-a02b-783fee9d05b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401345} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2fbcee-821a-5cd9-8a6e-f9412bd4ab5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401346} {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401347} {:answer/id #uuid "1540b931-3add-5e5c-99b4-77aa9c86e5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401347} :answer-field/id #uuid "17de6bba-7478-572d-9fe3-ada91a6e9edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a28026ac-9427-5db7-b40f-31513274adc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162365" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78fb291c-c31f-5ac0-9d77-7a2e07aadd0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}" :db/id 17592186401351} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc16458-ce9f-50c1-8fbf-48870b72fe4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}" :db/id 17592186401352} {:answer/id #uuid "1153c6c7-bf78-52f2-9e1f-684f7eccb765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({sin}^{3}⁡t+1)}" :db/id 17592186401353} {:answer/id #uuid "17520325-a116-5295-9221-ca1e38e5c07a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}}" :db/id 17592186401354} {:answer/id #uuid "0447c179-7aef-50a6-824a-496d140ba7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}}" :db/id 17592186401355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78fb291c-c31f-5ac0-9d77-7a2e07aadd0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}" :db/id 17592186401351} :answer-field/id #uuid "b978e6f7-e64f-5b80-8951-81925452e0ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5ac123c-3663-55fc-a172-6e4b0415bb6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162414" :question/problem "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{},\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 0-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 1|}{{((\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|}{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\sqrt{{({sin}^{2}⁡t+1)}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because cosine is positive for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "814655d8-f459-5df5-b722-3024b87065db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57815f7b-af61-528d-a997-36687ced0737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186401358} {:answer/id #uuid "d4db65b8-6f9c-5494-a06b-92bcdce809b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186401359} {:answer/id #uuid "eb263125-4fe2-540b-a807-c4ea79034c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186401360} {:answer/id #uuid "9709336c-60b5-5d73-aab9-35c162acf334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186401361} {:answer/id #uuid "7440cb05-c5ac-59cb-a23c-02e409faf53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186401362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9709336c-60b5-5d73-aab9-35c162acf334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186401361} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0797b50-1acd-58f0-8079-590c6e07a834" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac9e9607-a2dd-5594-94f3-d6c7ba9a234a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162418" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-{t}^{2}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{2\\cdot (\\,\\,)}{\\sqrt{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(t)=-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2)-(-2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{{((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{\\sqrt{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffa79034-bdcc-5524-aa39-e81a79b48ded" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Stream Function for a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc634ad5-e010-5c6c-accf-cdd1bfc87d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}" :db/id 17592186401365} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee290fe-8425-5228-94c2-57bd0a2da175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=(xy)^{2}" :db/id 17592186401366} {:answer/id #uuid "726bbe70-cba3-547c-bf90-2310b15873c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id 17592186401367} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3e8097-9217-5fb9-b7d3-fa8eb7bed46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}" :db/id 17592186401368} {:answer/id #uuid "1354e106-6bc4-5c0a-9398-0cd9067aa706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id 17592186401369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ee290fe-8425-5228-94c2-57bd0a2da175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=(xy)^{2}" :db/id 17592186401366} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c61b134-7c57-530f-8b97-97a808179f81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d5fedbb-f6c9-5abe-b595-3219e3d242a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162517" :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y\\,i-2x{y}^{2}\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y\\,i-2x{y}^{2}\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=-2x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=4xy+(-4xy)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}=(xy)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8b48905-c922-5f0b-904d-031f2f366d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186401372} {:answer/id #uuid "07837613-c6cb-599f-a1fe-60f06f0661aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401373} {:answer/id #uuid "823a0eb9-049b-54c7-87a1-4762c639c714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401374} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f740a3-7481-581f-876f-a404eea190e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401375} {:answer/id #uuid "730966c2-9ffc-5f1d-9533-075afe811e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "730966c2-9ffc-5f1d-9533-075afe811e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401376} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2067c3d-0d1e-5094-9c41-cc29fa1d1f8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c16eaa20-dd31-5858-a148-07284cbf2219" :question/math-academy-id "q-162632" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=2{t}^{3}\\,i+(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $2{t}^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id 17592186401377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2be7336-43db-505e-abb5-f6af2521593a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186401379} {:answer/id #uuid "4e991727-db18-5685-87e2-0cd52a14f4de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401380} {:answer/id #uuid "69604832-174f-53c1-981d-5cd931a50089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401381} {:answer/id #uuid "16e55850-0a5e-5ef0-8d3f-6ca688ae95ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186401382} {:answer/id #uuid "9f552a50-0a13-51c1-93c6-c2242cda08ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e991727-db18-5685-87e2-0cd52a14f4de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401380} :answer-field/id #uuid "50528b4c-2db1-5768-b4f5-380b6c2e954d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66bd138c-5fd7-52b5-b1a8-7c242f9884ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-162633" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ , $t\\ne n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component ${t}^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id 17592186401384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ede8e16-494c-5ead-ba64-5838ef07def5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186401386} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb8a9be-6f0a-565b-b6b2-11bc4789ac9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401387} {:answer/id #uuid "70995bca-c45d-5efe-9c86-d0ad7a6058b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401388} {:answer/id #uuid "50f624a9-d806-5717-a7e5-2f6fcc28ebd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186401389} {:answer/id #uuid "2e3fe947-d5c0-5bd5-8ca5-786edda0e0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e3fe947-d5c0-5bd5-8ca5-786edda0e0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id 17592186401390} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a56ee90-9fa8-5ebf-a7cd-271d63b533b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7adf6f23-c582-5c46-b1a7-7edbfe6210af" :question/math-academy-id "q-162637" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(1-{t}^{3})\\,i+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $1-{t}^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id 17592186124820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "383b8761-c8df-5055-82bc-b135e677607f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id 17592186401392} {:answer/id #uuid "6a24a551-0d67-54fa-a898-81b6b8a70318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id 17592186401393} {:answer/id #uuid "54876cd1-0314-5deb-ab1d-b12e5527f92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186401394} {:answer/id #uuid "0963c530-23fc-5035-8a90-8f3a474c35e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186401395} {:answer/id #uuid "0d45bd81-d2af-5d91-9d5a-2b641a8316e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $D$ only" :db/id 17592186401396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d45bd81-d2af-5d91-9d5a-2b641a8316e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $D$ only" :db/id 17592186401396} :answer-field/id #uuid "f27a5b4e-3727-5966-9e47-39feb88a654c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0151a70d-ce12-5172-8abb-638426f2b102" :question/math-academy-id "q-162747" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b698fe735caaf3bc260c1e921bb95596df8869eba67ec9ce72484b4d86cc8c69.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A$ and $D.$ Hence $A$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/0a/0a371aa559222bd28c37677bace18eb912bdc978e364bab4cb3d83f7a310de3f.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186401397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b59bf81-336b-5b77-a5ab-993e5da369cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $2z$" :db/id 17592186401399} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2b1eac-ff7d-50ba-8e40-931f60aee6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $y$" :db/id 17592186401400} {:answer/id #uuid "372a272b-dcaf-5ac5-a75d-2792619b147e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x$ and $2y$" :db/id 17592186401401} {:answer/id #uuid "92eb8681-34a4-5d3f-9b19-cabe81d62073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x$ and $2z$" :db/id 17592186401402} {:answer/id #uuid "eda1895b-7d76-519b-8bab-824851119ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $1$" :db/id 17592186401403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "372a272b-dcaf-5ac5-a75d-2792619b147e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x$ and $2y$" :db/id 17592186401401} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ec7c466-5f47-5c18-934e-2ba7c46ca3fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8afa6e7b-08b3-5fd6-b960-921c9d4bc534" :question/math-academy-id "q-162811" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and ${y}^{2}+z=9$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}⟨2x,\\,2yz,\\,{y}^{2}⟩=\\lambda ⟨\\boxed{}\\,, \\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\boxed{}\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+z=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+z=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,\\,2yz,\\,{y}^{2}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a26a345-848a-52d6-9cff-309d48842caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z\\,$ and $2x$" :db/id 17592186401406} {:answer/id #uuid "0336a2fb-21ec-5932-b4bf-96df7edcc264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,$ and $x$" :db/id 17592186401407} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3832c6-972f-5f02-873d-640f27294ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id 17592186401408} {:answer/id #uuid "f73da70f-25e8-5f76-94d0-6c2f5b70918f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,$ and $2$" :db/id 17592186401409} {:answer/id #uuid "13062583-c8cd-58e7-92bd-33acf71104ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\,$ and $-y$" :db/id 17592186401410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d3832c6-972f-5f02-873d-640f27294ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id 17592186401408} :answer-field/id #uuid "6944c355-576e-5c8e-b344-9b3d5fd644ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a114a88-7824-55e2-8ac4-08f991d5f004" :question/math-academy-id "q-162812" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y-x=0$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩=\\lambda ⟨-1,\\boxed{}\\,, \\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\boxed{}\\,⟩ \\\\ y-x=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y-x=0,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x,\\,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a818e04-786a-56b6-90cf-dcb573ccfa64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0982cdc8-9442-57fe-b7fa-346753e57354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$" :db/id 17592186401413} {:answer/id #uuid "d02b2ee1-2d41-522a-96b3-adfd227f36c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$" :db/id 17592186401414} {:answer/id #uuid "9b952bee-251b-539e-8f7d-67e73938f6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$" :db/id 17592186401415} {:answer/id #uuid "03a4583d-e5ff-5e79-9be7-41b39f117fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$" :db/id 17592186401416} {:answer/id #uuid "71423d40-798c-5652-a081-532957655dd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $x,\\,$ and $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$" :db/id 17592186401417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03a4583d-e5ff-5e79-9be7-41b39f117fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$" :db/id 17592186401416} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bc9a4e2-1d70-54d1-8a2a-b5a3fd7bf284" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e019c258-2f09-55a3-ab9c-d440f05baea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163092" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}xy \\\\ {x}^{2}-y \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y & \\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{} & -1 \\\\ \\boxed{} & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 2x & -1 \\\\ y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, the missing entries are $x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a818e04-786a-56b6-90cf-dcb573ccfa64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d959d5d7-cbc1-5318-b797-c37a0afbbf37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x{e}^{xyz},\\,$ $z,\\,$ and $z$" :db/id 17592186401420} {:answer/id #uuid "b30dc2cc-68a4-5912-8a3e-df5e08613c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${e}^{xyz},\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y$" :db/id 17592186401421} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd45803-73b4-58a2-b054-1d9f41e88d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$yz{e}^{yz},\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $y$" :db/id 17592186401422} {:answer/id #uuid "43123342-28a0-58f0-8627-2ed0371a4ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$yz{e}^{xyz},\\,$ $z,\\,$ and $y$" :db/id 17592186401423} {:answer/id #uuid "1c151508-a070-5be8-9646-29a3b6c0d0e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z{e}^{xyz},\\,$ $z,\\,$ and $z$" :db/id 17592186401424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43123342-28a0-58f0-8627-2ed0371a4ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$yz{e}^{xyz},\\,$ $z,\\,$ and $y$" :db/id 17592186401423} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cbe7815-5a90-5779-9948-27720bbf467a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4436f3f-18fe-5775-815d-04fe166d17a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163100" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xyz} \\\\ x+yz\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} & xz{e}^{xyz} & xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ 1 & \\boxed{} & \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing entries?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xyz})=yz{e}^{xyz},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xyz})=xz{e}^{xyz},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xyz})=xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+yz)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+yz)=z,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+yz)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}yz{e}^{xyz} & xz{e}^{xyz} & xy{e}^{xyz} \\\\ 1 & z & y\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a1cab09-af4c-5681-b572-7073c44cadd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id 17592186401427} {:answer/id #uuid "79bc2964-acb4-51b0-b007-9d90d00e18e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id 17592186401428} {:answer/id #uuid "17c52ede-9d07-5f7f-8b20-6215a7509689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id 17592186401429} {:answer/id #uuid "869eb4f4-f69e-53fa-8018-2d9ffbf80a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id 17592186401430} {:answer/id #uuid "e113570c-5c60-57ef-a5c6-a06bf8ad264a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id 17592186401431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "869eb4f4-f69e-53fa-8018-2d9ffbf80a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id 17592186401430} :answer-field/id #uuid "c860e53e-f867-5749-aa4f-e7a554c597fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9ce8c10-95e8-5421-97ed-611d96bbf7c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163771" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =2>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d12bfd22-9e9d-5d37-bbd0-35e563e5e30a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,-2)$ and $(2,3,0)$" :db/id 17592186401434} {:answer/id #uuid "99f7cc57-ad3a-5493-ba20-b02d01c3c072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,1.2)$  only" :db/id 17592186401435} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4d1de4-cb9d-5b3a-b919-1ee28c961db6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,3,0)$  only" :db/id 17592186401436} {:answer/id #uuid "27f2d2ad-7cab-5c68-bfaf-a31ab980901d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,1,3)$ only" :db/id 17592186401437} {:answer/id #uuid "d947cbda-24ab-58ef-b437-c399ba283d13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,1.2)$ and $(-2,1,3)$" :db/id 17592186401438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27f2d2ad-7cab-5c68-bfaf-a31ab980901d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,1,3)$ only" :db/id 17592186401437} :answer-field/id #uuid "32d7053b-a2c6-58ee-8efc-3a03178f6bd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c163c8c-12fc-59a2-9e1d-9f264f8ad03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163834" :question/problem "![](images/21/215b322e06280dd01e5858df9a17cd56673b5cdec69521decca205e03771ac21.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=3.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $3.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-2,1),$ so the global maximum point is $(-2,1,3).$\n\n![](images/d5/d5f948483a80f37257b285d48cd1e6bbf1d8a6ba9b66d1fea5edd865c787711a.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186088038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bcf009-6fca-5110-8ae8-643678b3cb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id 17592186401440} {:answer/id #uuid "eb82a4b2-58af-556c-a366-f92624768704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id 17592186401441} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a26a57-987c-5668-b531-e2f3db2b75bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id 17592186401442} {:answer/id #uuid "b6972414-a235-5987-855e-567ba571b605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id 17592186401443} {:answer/id #uuid "e08cc03c-cea4-583d-b68b-e5c8622f76e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}=(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y" :db/id 17592186401444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4bcf009-6fca-5110-8ae8-643678b3cb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id 17592186401440} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef647b19-79e2-5b54-b8df-1bf5fe9db9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5c269fd-ead8-50e4-b16e-9d9e9b4a244c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16400" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475ce117-63c5-5ee4-b359-fc20c9c51651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/id 17592186401447} {:answer/id #uuid "cc880a4e-5176-5965-abb2-4d59cb691ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id 17592186401448} {:answer/id #uuid "e2bd567a-a4c4-543c-81f0-2381cab6d5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186401449} {:answer/id #uuid "56368805-72a8-566e-a53d-bb48170c39e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186401450} {:answer/id #uuid "41a0973a-78e5-5b21-83f7-bcf5f3ddb6d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id 17592186401451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41a0973a-78e5-5b21-83f7-bcf5f3ddb6d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id 17592186401451} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8d87059-ea13-55bb-b541-23109862b322" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2740d2d6-bc06-5779-9308-da4a9d560214" :question/math-academy-id "q-164152" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c460920f6b2516ac8603852e12d15840cca051736555ea43cf15a278ef9e0155.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $C$ and $D.$ Hence $C$ and $D$ are local maxima.\n\n![](images/cc/cc526bb1cf73fb5b5579966e91077ccd096aea475a4cf13196166bd65ac88d7b.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186401452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64e7d2fb-003c-58c0-ab80-159e50748cc7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Intercepts of Spheres and Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd89ecad-0fc0-5ba5-a620-05712d3a1b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id 17592186401454} {:answer/id #uuid "d50858b8-1fd8-5038-a72e-57fc3037d5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2},0)$" :db/id 17592186401455} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd95cab-9721-51ba-b9c4-0bf0be760265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1,0)$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id 17592186401456} {:answer/id #uuid "99d03f44-97f1-5754-9f11-086ea13d97fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2},0)$" :db/id 17592186401457} {:answer/id #uuid "4408e820-21f8-5788-8399-5558cbe467ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2},0)$" :db/id 17592186401458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fd95cab-9721-51ba-b9c4-0bf0be760265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1,0)$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id 17592186401456} :answer-field/id #uuid "de275abf-f996-5e86-903d-b371c046139e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50783562-e3df-58d6-82c6-67c9daca87a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-164267" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{16}=1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=-1$ and $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-1,0)$ and $(0,1,0).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf44a846-8458-5e2f-bef3-72cf87a6035f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intercepts of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bb29e98-356d-5e90-ac97-d29d6178c40e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-3)$ only" :db/id 17592186401461} {:answer/id #uuid "6a12cd1c-6682-5df8-923c-6c5944da1e21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,1)$" :db/id 17592186401462} {:answer/id #uuid "6dea4a01-2c2d-5563-9522-56b73624cc0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-2)$ and $(0,0,2)$" :db/id 17592186401463} {:answer/id #uuid "9addb0b5-d438-50d6-917d-38fb314ead17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-1)$ only" :db/id 17592186401464} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7a3e71-8adf-5c96-a5b6-a2346b9e5122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The hyperboloid does not intersect the $z$-axis" :db/id 17592186401465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a12cd1c-6682-5df8-923c-6c5944da1e21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,1)$" :db/id 17592186401462} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c38e30b-5bcd-54ca-8ea6-28e179828a4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b589cdc8-22e6-5881-b7f2-9fb3725db0fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-164272" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{4}=-1,$ find its $z$-intercepts." :question/worked-solution "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ z & =-1\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-3$ and $z=1.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,1).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c2d581-8b12-575d-bb78-d8bb293a7339" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Sphere or Ellipsoid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e71a948-a9c5-5faf-b47c-17e50a3f8324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,1)$" :db/id 17592186401468} {:answer/id #uuid "1e85c958-d38e-5452-bf22-85abbc7a75bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(1,-3)$" :db/id 17592186401469} {:answer/id #uuid "df3c0953-d686-59f3-a7d7-fe00ebde4e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(-3,1)$" :db/id 17592186401470} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c4b9ff-1b21-54cc-b53e-7eb6f046091b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(3,2)$" :db/id 17592186401471} {:answer/id #uuid "598580ec-0b00-5ab0-a4fe-2e46a90d75e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(1,3)$" :db/id 17592186401472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e71a948-a9c5-5faf-b47c-17e50a3f8324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,1)$" :db/id 17592186401468} :answer-field/id #uuid "182e0ed3-43fe-59b0-b733-03b03b4eb82f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e6a384d-7ca2-5551-9c7a-30830f5ff58a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164311" :question/problem "Given the sphere $(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-2)^{2}=9,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (z-2)^{2} & =9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})} \\\\ z & =2\\pm \\sqrt{9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=9\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id 17592186401473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbeda26-2fc0-5195-be26-ae79c72fc7bf" :knowledge-point/title "Traces of Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff45c2a2-3a41-5311-99ca-5e68d6a5cab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The parabola $(x+3)^{2}=6z$" :db/id 17592186401475} {:answer/id #uuid "de23290b-1126-571c-a0e8-9a9fda87468d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The line $y=3$" :db/id 17592186401476} {:answer/id #uuid "86e5f373-6dc5-578d-9dab-a014632c2480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The parabola ${x}^{2}=6(z-3)$" :db/id 17592186401477} {:answer/id #uuid "017e8e93-5da0-5960-ba95-563c27bee3c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The line $y=\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id 17592186401478} {:answer/id #uuid "defd7bf9-9f21-5237-ad4c-3feffa02e09e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $y=-3$ and $y=15$" :db/id 17592186401479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "017e8e93-5da0-5960-ba95-563c27bee3c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The line $y=\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id 17592186401478} :answer-field/id #uuid "770068c0-e321-5534-9c7a-f0ff2132d3c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c06a8e8-6e3d-50f3-ae2e-200f38471a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-164400" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the parabolic cylinder $(x+3)^{2}=6y?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =6y \\\\ 9 & =6y \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{6} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a line in the $yz$-plane."}] [{:db/id 17592186401480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbeda26-2fc0-5195-be26-ae79c72fc7bf" :knowledge-point/title "Traces of Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddfe34f7-e006-5620-84fe-1db6fc963651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The line $x=4$" :db/id 17592186401482} {:answer/id #uuid "b838d302-7461-5223-8e33-c740bbf20ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The line $x=6$" :db/id 17592186401483} {:answer/id #uuid "161fe7ca-7c93-5282-b813-a0a45f65d7ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $x=2$ and $x=6$" :db/id 17592186401484} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfcfb48-24ef-595a-a479-2f2b1a35f56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1$" :db/id 17592186401485} {:answer/id #uuid "b309b4aa-316e-5e47-b444-65898ffba0f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{2}=1$" :db/id 17592186401486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "161fe7ca-7c93-5282-b813-a0a45f65d7ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $x=2$ and $x=6$" :db/id 17592186401484} :answer-field/id #uuid "53177c21-6f8b-5255-86cb-d89db3a9a51a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98ad452f-818a-5095-bf60-3f83ccbaae0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-164401" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =4\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of two lines $x=2$ and $x=6$ in the $xz$-plane."}] [{:db/id 17592186401487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9840265f-02bf-58fa-94fd-77fb970af297" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces and Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91eeb41c-1501-568e-81cf-b44bd4f6787f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A paraboloid" :db/id 17592186401489} {:answer/id #uuid "2b39f102-3558-5351-90f8-01a7c7dbb286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cylinder" :db/id 17592186401490} {:answer/id #uuid "9d64cbe0-4403-549f-94d2-cf3c73ceb779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperboloid" :db/id 17592186401491} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffa2b0d-b957-5c7c-a016-40f0594d1ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id 17592186401492} {:answer/id #uuid "01e9c23e-64f6-5d8c-a235-0d406e419811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipsoid" :db/id 17592186401493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01e9c23e-64f6-5d8c-a235-0d406e419811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipsoid" :db/id 17592186401493} :answer-field/id #uuid "903d6dd5-4634-519d-8334-fc52eabb5e80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ef46611-6066-5608-b7b6-cb53d9ef1a50" :question/math-academy-id "q-164403" :question/problem "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of an ellipsoid centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand an ellipsoid centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is an ellipsoid with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,-2,0),\\,a=3,\\,b=\\sqrt{7},\\,c=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36c5224a-eccc-5ea9-8719-2b0dc2ddb44d" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bf04dd9-c165-5182-ac9b-5676db24ff34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id 17592186401496} {:answer/id #uuid "c793d63c-12e4-5933-97de-c7ed09f6bb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(0,-1)$" :db/id 17592186401497} {:answer/id #uuid "c61e78a2-beab-5682-9dcd-c175e8fd13fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }x-y>12}" :db/id 17592186401498} {:answer/id #uuid "cb19e2aa-343d-50f8-8950-85499a932e8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(1,0)$" :db/id 17592186401499} {:answer/id #uuid "011e26cf-a2f4-5e6c-9930-618f8d6227cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(0,1)$" :db/id 17592186401500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c793d63c-12e4-5933-97de-c7ed09f6bb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(0,-1)$" :db/id 17592186401497} :answer-field/id #uuid "5199bacc-b680-59e5-9422-f0c044740659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57563ddb-cece-5702-b61c-95f3f8e1ba05" :question/math-academy-id "q-164416" :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(0,-1).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36c5224a-eccc-5ea9-8719-2b0dc2ddb44d" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74d4f589-684f-54c3-98d4-d4a82b6d1f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id 17592186401503} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b1b394-3eb1-53d4-aa43-d93c484ebf19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(5,1)$" :db/id 17592186401504} {:answer/id #uuid "52adf993-254f-51e6-8678-427a0e39d183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:xy=1}" :db/id 17592186401505} {:answer/id #uuid "92358f72-d64b-514a-80c8-595390361c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(-5,0)$" :db/id 17592186401506} {:answer/id #uuid "1238065d-225e-5276-ac70-011b60b63f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(5,0)$" :db/id 17592186401507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92358f72-d64b-514a-80c8-595390361c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(-5,0)$" :db/id 17592186401506} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ccdc892-8830-5973-bdbd-4e9a1948683b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a066ac1-6120-502f-9195-eb35e4541908" :question/math-academy-id "q-164417" :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $(x+5)^{2}-{y}^{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x+5)^{2}-{y}^{2}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x+5)^{2}-{y}^{2}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(-5,0).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9840265f-02bf-58fa-94fd-77fb970af297" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces and Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "917478a6-9f85-5c2f-9abc-0e94e7aaed04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperboloid of two sheets" :db/id 17592186401510} {:answer/id #uuid "1549a164-9e45-5667-87ff-e1b8a78bcfbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone" :db/id 17592186401511} {:answer/id #uuid "2993d1fe-f7db-55c9-8175-8657b7efaf2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperboloid of one sheet" :db/id 17592186401512} {:answer/id #uuid "b72085af-190b-5b99-82c7-f2f831e98d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic cylinder" :db/id 17592186401513} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b6af54-c6f0-5204-a41b-545422423ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipsoid" :db/id 17592186401514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "917478a6-9f85-5c2f-9abc-0e94e7aaed04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperboloid of two sheets" :db/id 17592186401510} :answer-field/id #uuid "25021911-f878-5c6e-8755-61db8e40936f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cc40487-6e8d-55c3-bbc9-868108f22530" :question/math-academy-id "q-164476" :question/problem "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-(z-2)^{2}=-1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of a hyperboloid of two sheets centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=-1,\n$$\n\nand a hyperboloid of two sheets centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=-1,\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is a hyperboloid of two sheets with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(-1,0,2),\\,a=\\sqrt{3},\\,b=2,\\,c=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9840265f-02bf-58fa-94fd-77fb970af297" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces and Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a76405be-1788-5e72-94d6-117c29e7aadb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone" :db/id 17592186401517} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d6b36f-0169-5c05-b658-232c406af7ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperboloid" :db/id 17592186401518} {:answer/id #uuid "548a6000-5c46-5dd3-9714-417aaea346a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cylinder" :db/id 17592186401519} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e1f556-c2cb-575c-8407-5c88361da39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id 17592186401520} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8fd86a-c981-5765-9ec0-cde9abb54c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic cylinder" :db/id 17592186401521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "548a6000-5c46-5dd3-9714-417aaea346a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cylinder" :db/id 17592186401519} :answer-field/id #uuid "962dfec3-feb3-5f4c-9675-1a5ee6fae954" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37f3129a-ae32-5711-8b8d-cb83602d7d4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-164477" :question/problem "Identify the following surface:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1,\\,-\\infty <z<\\infty .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of an elliptic cylinder centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is an elliptic cylinder with $a=b=\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9840265f-02bf-58fa-94fd-77fb970af297" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces and Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea6ba4f0-2d64-5c1c-b8ce-059c4ac25b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone" :db/id 17592186401524} {:answer/id #uuid "94d5b7bc-b4a2-5f44-8d1f-5fae8941458e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic paraboloid" :db/id 17592186401525} {:answer/id #uuid "224f132d-2588-5cb3-93cf-da8c9190c61d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cylinder" :db/id 17592186401526} {:answer/id #uuid "8f342e45-8c79-5472-a082-fe42127fb510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic paraboloid" :db/id 17592186401527} {:answer/id #uuid "6db8c8a8-71c1-5bf5-8418-3d407d9ca9f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic cylinder" :db/id 17592186401528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db8c8a8-71c1-5bf5-8418-3d407d9ca9f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic cylinder" :db/id 17592186401528} :answer-field/id #uuid "4009320d-45e7-537c-bae9-47fb3fceb5ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a3947ec-3744-5af7-8ce2-1be319e6d154" :question/math-academy-id "q-164479" :question/problem "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1,\\,-\\infty <z<\\infty .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of a hyperbolic cylinder, whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is a hyperbolic cylinder with $a=3,\\,b=4.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9840265f-02bf-58fa-94fd-77fb970af297" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces and Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121542ce-6356-51c9-90a7-86cd6ba2b5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone" :db/id 17592186401531} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d6c8c7-34c2-5007-b993-6a2477b8f4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperboloid" :db/id 17592186401532} {:answer/id #uuid "b98c944e-85b6-5534-ab35-0f7cf75123b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cylinder" :db/id 17592186401533} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8f1c2e-2ed2-5741-ad54-ce3d99d51afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic paraboloid" :db/id 17592186401534} {:answer/id #uuid "d521747d-2c8e-5cb8-b7c2-c2125829bd87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic cylinder" :db/id 17592186401535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d521747d-2c8e-5cb8-b7c2-c2125829bd87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic cylinder" :db/id 17592186401535} :answer-field/id #uuid "689c4fa8-7285-5e24-ac05-5212e0e90119" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "540afee8-a0c6-54b4-b0fc-ff72c9dd4c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-164481" :question/problem "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-(y-1)^{2}=1,\\,-\\infty <z<\\infty \n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of a hyperbolic cylinder, whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand a hyperbolic cylinder, whose axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ in the $xy$-plane, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is a hyperbolic cylinder with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(-2,1),\\,a=b=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186284506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89fbf42b-d171-5772-87ce-cf622b720963" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces and Cylinders With Alternative Orientations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd6eaa8-586c-5103-b0cc-92a8aaaf1a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis" :db/id 17592186281813} {:answer/id #uuid "2556d713-7643-5e10-b347-3ff594c56f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis" :db/id 17592186282020} {:answer/id #uuid "2b318cd8-3045-5788-9bb8-ab1134992947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis" :db/id 17592186282075} {:answer/id #uuid "56d2cf8c-c2ea-5087-9d96-d9a6dcd9d5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis" :db/id 17592186282432} {:answer/id #uuid "eafb0571-17a8-5784-a800-513c70030f56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis" :db/id 17592186283749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2556d713-7643-5e10-b347-3ff594c56f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis" :db/id 17592186282020} :answer-field/id #uuid "09284adb-1272-5cf4-81c3-aa20d058b0a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186283955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e28adc4e-c42a-55a7-ab38-db39626f07cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-164510" :question/problem "Identify the surface $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is an elliptic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis and $b=\\sqrt{2},\\,c=2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89fbf42b-d171-5772-87ce-cf622b720963" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces and Cylinders With Alternative Orientations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed669204-c56c-5225-aefc-956037ae0d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic cylinder whose axis is the $x$-axis" :db/id 17592186401538} {:answer/id #uuid "359f70ba-f3cf-563a-9d19-52e3ff5fe27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $x$-axis" :db/id 17592186401539} {:answer/id #uuid "d5285b0d-7ee4-5490-8561-775dea047606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $y$-axis" :db/id 17592186401540} {:answer/id #uuid "74313148-6534-5531-9101-2416622ea616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone whose axis is the $z$-axis" :db/id 17592186401541} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ce9479-a493-554f-88d6-f3b32cbf67e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbolic cylinder whose axis is the $y$-axis" :db/id 17592186401542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "359f70ba-f3cf-563a-9d19-52e3ff5fe27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $x$-axis" :db/id 17592186401539} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa665e14-2670-50c6-9aa4-6148466ac38d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50443007-c819-5285-a74f-1d6dfd323e6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164547" :question/problem "Identify the following surface:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=2z,\\,-\\infty <x<\\infty .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nThus, a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4pz.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $x$-axis and $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "755a881c-6b14-5e96-bd68-6af58d5ef813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $1$" :db/id 17592186401545} {:answer/id #uuid "5965e390-56a0-55ef-a7dc-eff34c09cc53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id 17592186401546} {:answer/id #uuid "1f629da2-c549-5180-8903-3a78b8767616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id 17592186401547} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1fc5c2-bdfa-52e9-a59e-c6227d171448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id 17592186401548} {:answer/id #uuid "47a16fa9-9d49-55d0-a143-c2b6cca5c4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id 17592186401549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f629da2-c549-5180-8903-3a78b8767616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id 17592186401547} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41dd28d-0ca0-5222-9cfe-700ddaa37f17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e791f5b6-5e18-534a-99e5-45cc16c1809f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16455" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=1$ and ${b}^{2}=8.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=1,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=8,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=1.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 1=2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549870ce-a18a-5a94-a9ea-f3de72aaac1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1243d3b-361e-5bdf-975f-049a3ec6fa01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone centered at $(-2,0,-1)$" :db/id 17592186401552} {:answer/id #uuid "fa487f89-3be7-5d40-961d-7546309e11de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone centered at $(-2,0,1)$" :db/id 17592186401553} {:answer/id #uuid "025044f2-a8e8-56c9-abcc-e56e1609a263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperboloid of two sheet centered at $(-2,0,1)$" :db/id 17592186401554} {:answer/id #uuid "1c63b753-83a9-5faf-9522-2b10200701ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperboloid of one sheet centered at $(-2,0,1)$" :db/id 17592186401555} {:answer/id #uuid "a76c934a-6a50-5b6b-97fe-08774387e03f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone centered at $(-2,1,1)$" :db/id 17592186401556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa487f89-3be7-5d40-961d-7546309e11de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone centered at $(-2,0,1)$" :db/id 17592186401553} :answer-field/id #uuid "fffbfe5a-e6b2-509f-a19b-67c3c7c49922" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "040e9acf-1af9-5813-86f6-e469cb0eecc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-164553" :question/problem "Identify the surface $5{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-5{z}^{2}+20x+10z+15=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the $x$ and $z$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-5{z}^{2}+20x+10z+15 & =0 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}+4x)-{y}^{2}-5({z}^{2}-2z)+15 & =0 \\\\ 5[(x+2)^{2}-4]-{y}^{2}-5[(z-1)^{2}-1]+15 & =0 \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}-20-{y}^{2}-5(z-1)^{2}+5+15 & =0 \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}-{y}^{2}-5(z-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ -5(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+5(z-1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of an elliptic cone centered at $(-2,0,1).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549870ce-a18a-5a94-a9ea-f3de72aaac1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8dd7e4-d04f-54e0-9c82-7503faa98408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipsoid centered at $(1,-2,0)$" :db/id 17592186401559} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee2009b-db2c-5ad5-a30f-47e82534faf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone centered at $(1,-2,0)$" :db/id 17592186401560} {:answer/id #uuid "0eacccc9-5134-5c35-b00f-adda5251c8bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An elliptic cone centered at $(1,2,1)$" :db/id 17592186401561} {:answer/id #uuid "1d5f9579-d5b4-564c-9ff2-31895a46959d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperboloid of one sheet centered at $(1,-2,0)$" :db/id 17592186401562} {:answer/id #uuid "c2873b29-87cc-5f23-93ab-b79ab215b691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipsoid centered at $(1,2,1)$" :db/id 17592186401563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8dd7e4-d04f-54e0-9c82-7503faa98408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipsoid centered at $(1,-2,0)$" :db/id 17592186401559} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d3a90f-543b-5602-bf42-4796008282b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84fd9eec-1cf5-594c-8917-00bf240277f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-164555" :question/problem "Identify the surface ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2x+20y+11=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the $x$ and $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2x+20y+11 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+5({y}^{2}+4y)+{z}^{2}+11 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+5([y+2)^{2}-4]+{z}^{2}+11 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+5(y+2)^{2}-20+{z}^{2}+11 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+5(y+2)^{2}+{z}^{2}-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by rearranging, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+5(y+2)^{2}+{z}^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+5(y+2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{10} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of an ellipsoid centered at $(1,-2,0).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8434a84-5ef1-505b-a2c7-d42809c26959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $4$" :db/id 17592186401566} {:answer/id #uuid "386a0708-44f8-5648-9834-907d2d7fef7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id 17592186401567} {:answer/id #uuid "f019f459-9c8d-5bca-ac2e-8bf08a5f03f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id 17592186401568} {:answer/id #uuid "1362416b-6b2e-5b41-8cce-b430bb96c908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id 17592186401569} {:answer/id #uuid "c489543f-9ba5-563a-8700-e139b5d4d02f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id 17592186401570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1362416b-6b2e-5b41-8cce-b430bb96c908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id 17592186401569} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fd9c31-b922-5bc4-b3d4-983574117b6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cb21ae0-f2f0-5250-97c3-5094275c61e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-16465" :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{20}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-1$ and $k=-3,$ so the center is located at $(-1,-3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=5$ and ${b}^{2}=20.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=5,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=20,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4f3d642-de3f-54d5-aede-eba21e11be92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id 17592186401573} {:answer/id #uuid "e9df0568-122c-5b6d-9b84-d5caa3cabe43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},0)" :db/id 17592186401574} {:answer/id #uuid "dc20f4f3-9c00-5d0b-920a-7a632bc6b622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id 17592186401575} {:answer/id #uuid "20209ee4-eb61-55e6-aa7a-3caa61c198e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id 17592186401576} {:answer/id #uuid "73f81d60-d0cf-5e03-8299-56a8163c5850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id 17592186401577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc20f4f3-9c00-5d0b-920a-7a632bc6b622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id 17592186401575} :answer-field/id #uuid "160e9ede-f9e3-593e-8295-6afb981450c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fca3c3f7-188d-5506-9818-be66c0607803" :question/math-academy-id "q-16642" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}+16 & =36 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(3-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)$ and $(3+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e455adf-7973-5ee4-bb3c-0bd83316580a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id 17592186401580} {:answer/id #uuid "d3568acd-90d7-5e24-95a2-7dddb6de4815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1\\pm \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id 17592186401581} {:answer/id #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id 17592186401582} {:answer/id #uuid "829db562-f4d5-5e31-b45c-b397742cb7e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\sqrt{5},0)" :db/id 17592186401583} {:answer/id #uuid "a69ed72a-53dd-5923-8c05-9efa0f54638b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id 17592186401584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id 17592186401582} :answer-field/id #uuid "d58b31e7-b6c4-5d90-9a58-b8914d59f384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba3205c4-c133-5ac7-861e-655b59a14526" :question/math-academy-id "q-16645" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{1}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =9\\cdot 25 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =225 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =200 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{200}{9} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{200}{9}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-1-\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)$ and $(-1+\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cd4c18b-e511-5b14-9f13-a5b4cb4f47f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and  ${f}_{max}=15$" :db/id 17592186401587} {:answer/id #uuid "2821ee27-e28a-5af2-b0bc-7da60bb40070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id 17592186401588} {:answer/id #uuid "77dac977-36a1-5c53-9361-88fca32b4abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id 17592186401589} {:answer/id #uuid "d746e3e5-aa5e-5fa1-88ee-69128529ad71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id 17592186401590} {:answer/id #uuid "18156a3d-068d-5e69-8ef7-8771427933c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id 17592186401591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2821ee27-e28a-5af2-b0bc-7da60bb40070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id 17592186401588} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc59060d-a9ed-5533-b31b-311227554f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e7d00266-1ded-5f4c-819a-7467528496c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167165" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2x,\\,2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50c91312-6d25-5d77-a03c-b9737dcd0907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186401594} {:answer/id #uuid "3070b0e6-2443-59e9-88ad-5d64b7dbe0c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186401595} {:answer/id #uuid "de9e4a77-4bdb-50ab-8c39-a254aabe63cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186401596} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed4ec19-9461-5c93-8e53-e713230511d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186401597} {:answer/id #uuid "6572c207-612f-5e17-bdb8-cc85be4bf3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186401598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de9e4a77-4bdb-50ab-8c39-a254aabe63cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186401596} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37c6cf2-31b1-549d-9c9e-1baf99bb11bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd6f7437-bc45-50cb-9400-bcd422c5b408" :question/math-academy-id "q-168921" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\nII. It is a type II region\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/bc/bc6b71d1fe69abd34dbd939dc62ede5201b75713e25456cf084e889bc44f449e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.4$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/4a/4a1fd7069abaa3b3fa4d9c286773d226798bf3ad565e85663cab44557fd635c1.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=-1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/26/260e8e959ca95a029a98bd4102106a66df418ea73c0640398c8fdebfdbf812f6.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186401599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a316d2cb-bab1-518a-9a6c-3956bfcbcd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id 17592186401601} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec09427-50e7-5ea6-a6c5-d462ec4a977e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id 17592186401602} {:answer/id #uuid "685faf06-be0e-5f6e-869b-353ff052fead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id 17592186401603} {:answer/id #uuid "ed368aed-25dd-5df6-b266-89719aec6636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+1" :db/id 17592186401604} {:answer/id #uuid "d50e40c4-e8c2-5ba6-ad0e-dc0b92fad230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}" :db/id 17592186401605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "685faf06-be0e-5f6e-869b-353ff052fead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id 17592186401603} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e70783-1025-5acd-82ba-24685ceb80f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b98dba6-8029-58a2-a81c-ae05e5ca1860" :question/math-academy-id "q-170347" :question/problem "![](images/71/71d9df0708a77639f3f7b2640ea2a03cebbfd72e6cfb3369c07d83a89eba6508.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Therefore, $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a53010-eddc-5115-8c70-0f32d0144384" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Hyperbola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "295a34e6-e842-585f-b03d-2b207719a6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of intersection" :db/id 17592186401608} {:answer/id #uuid "3eac3404-fcdd-511d-b564-54a6a260657f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 3" :db/id 17592186401609} {:answer/id #uuid "d3932dde-cf5e-5e48-9c49-40bd8d6b1366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186401610} {:answer/id #uuid "809ebb14-2ff5-5591-a2dc-a7839d212e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id 17592186401611} {:answer/id #uuid "dee838d7-2ccd-57a7-9289-a3238457885f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id 17592186401612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "295a34e6-e842-585f-b03d-2b207719a6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of intersection" :db/id 17592186401608} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c46fcc-9e01-5325-b5ea-b258368147e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed5c021d-75c0-5eb0-8447-cc8817c3eb31" :question/math-academy-id "q-170558" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1$ intersects the line $y=8x.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=8x,$ we first replace $y$ with $8x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{(8x)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{64{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ -3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions. So, the hyperbola does not intersect the line $y=8x.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186060112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe5bd766-d624-54e3-945e-7ae37b83886a" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Sentence Containing a Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc237d2e-e258-51aa-b02c-d3de373a0b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,|\\,18$ or $11$ is prime" :db/id 17592186401614} {:answer/id #uuid "95645e07-ad94-5f53-94f7-cca49c5ad899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18$ and $11$ is prime" :db/id 17592186401615} {:answer/id #uuid "d8dae576-92ac-54ed-a811-031ffd21fa2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,|\\,18$ and $11$ is prime" :db/id 17592186401616} {:answer/id #uuid "42432f20-0241-59fd-b7c8-f9f2bbb70103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,|\\,18$ and $11$ is not prime" :db/id 17592186401617} {:answer/id #uuid "4e68f1f1-4450-5751-bd58-9f125f0beedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18$ or $11$ is prime" :db/id 17592186401618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95645e07-ad94-5f53-94f7-cca49c5ad899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18$ and $11$ is prime" :db/id 17592186401615} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c5c17af-159f-5984-9b54-f720cf946df4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "711d64e0-f4d6-5c58-a54c-d6327695bb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-171016" :question/problem "$\\,$ $3\\,|\\,18$ or $11$ is not prime.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(3\\,|\\,18\\text{ or }11\\text{ is not prime}) & ≡¬((3\\,|\\,18)∨(11\\text{ is not prime})) \\\\  & ≡¬(3\\,|\\,18)∧¬(11\\text{ is not prime}) \\\\  & ≡(3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18)∧(11\\text{ is prime}) \\\\  & ≡3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18\\text{ and }11\\text{ is prime}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93249d2-e5f0-5767-98da-c33b837b41c0" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfbc4514-da7a-5d32-bb0c-75cb3c1af14e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a rational number whose triple is $2.$" :db/id 17592186401621} {:answer/id #uuid "00b16d7a-3879-5b16-a22b-05c9128f9304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists no rational number whose triple is $2.$" :db/id 17592186401622} {:answer/id #uuid "445c2f89-43cc-540c-a4c7-441ca2f3d468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a rational number whose triple is a multiple of $2.$" :db/id 17592186401623} {:answer/id #uuid "db52d8b1-5884-5600-886d-f29af9a8111f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Triple every rational number is not $2.$" :db/id 17592186401624} {:answer/id #uuid "33760338-3c64-54bf-8af4-8c4133bc6f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Triple every rational number is $2.$" :db/id 17592186401625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfbc4514-da7a-5d32-bb0c-75cb3c1af14e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a rational number whose triple is $2.$" :db/id 17592186401621} :answer-field/id #uuid "516df518-0f30-5fe7-9381-34b1cbb3ff17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fa1ebd9-8d46-5a04-b414-ffbaef5b882d" :question/math-academy-id "q-171998" :question/problem "$\\,$ $∃q\\in Q,\\,3q=2.$\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?" :question/worked-solution "The existential quantifier \"$∃$\" means \"there exists.\" So, our quantified statement reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $q\\in Q$ **such that** $3q=2.$ \n\nThis can be further simplified as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a rational number whose triple is $2.$\n\n**Note:** This statement is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186401626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "875d7f2a-43ec-5635-8e72-48ae83d0339b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe9a0e2-c6d2-56ac-b91e-b2347fd649b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a number between $0$ and $1$ that is greater than its square." :db/id 17592186401628} {:answer/id #uuid "286a7a4d-b364-5fd1-bc1c-cdcc0f25f9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Consider the square of every number between $0$ and $1.$" :db/id 17592186401629} {:answer/id #uuid "676c24b5-3446-5b45-8adc-2f8128e722d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Every number is greater than its square." :db/id 17592186401630} {:answer/id #uuid "db1e01e2-6988-5261-bb19-4bde540dddf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a number whose square is between $0$ and $1.$" :db/id 17592186401631} {:answer/id #uuid "9830dd3d-604d-5712-a275-601b3e2f9f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Every number between $0$ and $1$ is greater than its square." :db/id 17592186401632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9830dd3d-604d-5712-a275-601b3e2f9f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Every number between $0$ and $1$ is greater than its square." :db/id 17592186401632} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f005b5-9729-5d5f-b912-f7b0246c77fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "698421f5-96c5-5307-a8fb-d2ac4a250cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-171999" :question/problem "$\\,$ $∀x\\in (0,1),\\,x>{x}^{2}$\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?" :question/worked-solution "The universal quantifier \"$∀$\" means \"for all.\" So, our quantified statement reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $x\\in (0,1),\\,x>{x}^{2}.$\n\nThis can be further simplified as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Every number between $0$ and $1$ is greater than its square.\n\n**Note:** This statement is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186401633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce3c28-857b-5538-b362-cf52526e3688" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0beb1ecb-7649-51d5-8923-8085f1d146bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id 17592186401635} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5ec049-b8d0-5c17-a28c-05813122674f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id 17592186401636} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d87b1b-17a5-523c-a973-f5e0ad287cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186401637} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4f4094-0cfd-5ae4-8308-88f0b83b2c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id 17592186401638} {:answer/id #uuid "946c0adc-baba-5359-a975-69b7a89f4838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186401639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa5ec049-b8d0-5c17-a28c-05813122674f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id 17592186401636} :answer-field/id #uuid "5088efea-7f9e-57d5-8f9c-14c688a0e06b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "104d7814-9b21-55a2-864d-c61ec56f5c8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-17392" :question/problem "Given that $y=2{x}^{e}-ln⁡x$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{e}-ln⁡x) \\\\  & =2e{x}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2e{x}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2e\\cdot {1}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =2e-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "391bb8ae-88ba-520c-a5cf-7e2fa5474788" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Predicate Expressed in Plain English"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4342a2-aa37-5d96-b2bd-53d063965b9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-x$ is not prime" :db/id 17592186401642} {:answer/id #uuid "15a35b0c-6fdf-55ef-951e-82f3c0ef6382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ is not prime" :db/id 17592186401643} {:answer/id #uuid "ed204714-bf39-5405-9311-45ac92c2d622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is not prime" :db/id 17592186401644} {:answer/id #uuid "ca80c410-622d-5f0f-975e-2b344e560b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is prime" :db/id 17592186401645} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b2b009-d901-5f51-ba56-c279e930d4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ is prime" :db/id 17592186401646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca80c410-622d-5f0f-975e-2b344e560b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is prime" :db/id 17592186401645} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6698acc-7b29-5b47-adfc-142f7b407f15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "813f8661-6028-5444-9eb0-c5477e6d2450" :question/math-academy-id "q-175404" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\nP(x):x\\text{ is }\\text{not}\\text{ prime},\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following represents $¬P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n¬P(x):\\text{It is not true that }x\\text{ is }\\text{not}\\text{ prime},\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, $x$ is prime. Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$$\n¬P(x):x\\text{ is prime}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "391bb8ae-88ba-520c-a5cf-7e2fa5474788" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Predicate Expressed in Plain English"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa68b75b-8128-5a25-b790-f2663b29914e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is not a member of the set of real numbers" :db/id 17592186401649} {:answer/id #uuid "9cec5a45-da56-5be0-a4fe-d31742e4edcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is a member of the set of integer numbers" :db/id 17592186401650} {:answer/id #uuid "83772d67-29e7-568c-95eb-2014c2e04d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is a member of the set of real numbers" :db/id 17592186401651} {:answer/id #uuid "b37340ba-173b-5278-8d7d-6e463d3106c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is not a member of the set of rational numbers" :db/id 17592186401652} {:answer/id #uuid "428328e2-4ee1-58bf-86ab-1eac59b08650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is not a member of the set of integer numbers" :db/id 17592186401653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "428328e2-4ee1-58bf-86ab-1eac59b08650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is not a member of the set of integer numbers" :db/id 17592186401653} :answer-field/id #uuid "6588a6a5-4237-5a1f-91da-7b37766d0fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fa22fb7-c16d-5dc4-beee-381645b3cb56" :question/math-academy-id "q-175419" :question/problem "$\\,$ $x$ is a member of the set of integer numbers.\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ is a member of the set of integer numbers.\n\nIn other words, $x$ is *not* a member of the set of integer numbers. Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is **not** a member of the set of integer numbers."}] [{:db/id 17592186101253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78a314f5-c68e-5618-9913-6599483e5664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{C-{x}^{3}}" :db/id 17592186401655} {:answer/id #uuid "3ccfcafe-cef5-50d0-a01d-4b0b97482678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{C-3{x}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186401656} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb9aead-e399-5915-a838-fa945173baeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{C-3x}" :db/id 17592186401657} {:answer/id #uuid "930d61d6-ef38-5e4c-8a33-08fa25e7168c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id 17592186401658} {:answer/id #uuid "447f6dfe-e7b5-5176-98bb-437118ba132e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{C-x}" :db/id 17592186401659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "930d61d6-ef38-5e4c-8a33-08fa25e7168c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id 17592186401658} :answer-field/id #uuid "948f7240-5694-5bc6-95db-83a3f04326f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffe3c5eb-c32b-557c-96a7-7ea03bab3fa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-17544" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{z}^{-1}+2{x}^{2}{z}^{-2}}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{xz}+\\frac{2x}{{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-2x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-2x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-2{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186101258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8181ba88-9e06-5281-b3b3-c20c7b029122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x-6}{x-2}" :db/id 17592186401661} {:answer/id #uuid "0333c509-36d0-531c-b1e8-c0d5eb1a0e56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x+7}{1+x}" :db/id 17592186401662} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed26247-cff8-52e1-b593-ea1aea2edbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x+4}{4x-3}" :db/id 17592186401663} {:answer/id #uuid "cf88b680-b1c2-5eb0-a109-817c0a9bd2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{5x-4}" :db/id 17592186401664} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a50594-0018-55c3-84f5-62e1bd92ad8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{x+1}" :db/id 17592186401665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf88b680-b1c2-5eb0-a109-817c0a9bd2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{5x-4}" :db/id 17592186401664} :answer-field/id #uuid "e799e599-a900-5eac-8575-dfee31f5aa3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ccfb013-777e-5e8a-8a81-f7a1a7ca50df" :question/math-academy-id "q-17559" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y(y-1)}{x}=0,\\,y(1)=5,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{x+C}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{x+C}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition  $y(1)=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =\\frac{1}{1+C}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{(x-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{(5x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "637030d2-d58f-56af-bf16-f33f755b65f9" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Plain English Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15927e61-d24a-5e9e-8234-3a83c7489a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ is not smaller than $3.$" :db/id 17592186401668} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6ec547-f205-5bfa-8b82-3ce3fb9cc66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ is not smaller than $5.$" :db/id 17592186401669} {:answer/id #uuid "60c6346a-02a6-5963-9190-7f70562c480e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ is smaller than $3.$" :db/id 17592186401670} {:answer/id #uuid "68d51cc8-d409-5837-9199-4a007d311037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ is smaller than $5.$" :db/id 17592186401671} {:answer/id #uuid "a3306971-ca93-5299-ab72-c4d506a635f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ is equal to $5.$" :db/id 17592186401672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf6ec547-f205-5bfa-8b82-3ce3fb9cc66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ is not smaller than $5.$" :db/id 17592186401669} :answer-field/id #uuid "3836e3be-ceaa-5182-be58-3dca86925d41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd8d390f-c42d-56c3-b2d4-b1b0454599e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-176051" :question/problem "Given the statement\n\n$$\nP:3\\text{ is smaller than }5\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following represents $¬P?$" :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n¬P:\\text{It is not true that }3\\text{ is smaller than }5.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, $3$ is *not* smaller than $5.$ Therefore, $¬P$ means the following:\n\n$$\n¬P:3\\text{ is }\\text{not}\\text{ smaller than }5.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that $P$ is **true,** while $¬P$ is **false.**"}] [{:db/id 17592186401673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "637030d2-d58f-56af-bf16-f33f755b65f9" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Plain English Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d826904-96a2-59ce-b5fc-60b5526a1ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  is not greater than  $0.1.$" :db/id 17592186401675} {:answer/id #uuid "390a4ec3-5b8a-5b98-bc89-d674abef5a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.1$  is not greater than $0.$" :db/id 17592186401676} {:answer/id #uuid "b53fd129-b8e8-5685-a1f6-07230f89a1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  is equal to $0.1.$" :db/id 17592186401677} {:answer/id #uuid "252daeb4-b00c-5b00-87cc-5e54a6b731b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.1$  is greater than $0.$" :db/id 17592186401678} {:answer/id #uuid "778d6c76-109a-5c81-a30f-11950a9705cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  is greater than  $0.1.$" :db/id 17592186401679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "778d6c76-109a-5c81-a30f-11950a9705cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  is greater than  $0.1.$" :db/id 17592186401679} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4cf313f-d1b6-5abf-b5ce-f86b65e36b0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0f8781c-d297-5aca-886f-46748c33ed7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-176859" :question/problem "Given the statement\n\n$$\nP:0\\text{ is }\\text{not}\\text{ greater than }0.1\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following represents $¬P?$" :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n¬P:\\text{It is }\\text{not}\\text{ true that }0\\text{ is }\\text{not}\\text{ greater than }0.1,\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, $0$ is greater than $0.1.$ Therefore, $¬P$ means the following:\n\n$$\n¬P:0\\text{ is greater than }0.1.\n$$\n\nNote that $P$ is **true,** while $¬P$ is **false.**"}] [{:db/id 17592186401680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f674fcf-7f3e-53c0-8759-6f83b0820160" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Conjunction and Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce66c39b-76e7-547b-8560-3f8586f07d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is rational or less than $2$" :db/id 17592186401682} {:answer/id #uuid "15d749ae-772a-5eae-be65-5432afc6b914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is neither rational nor less than $2$" :db/id 17592186401683} {:answer/id #uuid "4d40f6cc-c5d5-5763-a712-bf7b4204ab5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is rational" :db/id 17592186401684} {:answer/id #uuid "bbda7dd0-e8b9-50bc-83b4-ebc0a9a52b4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is rational but not less than $2$" :db/id 17592186401685} {:answer/id #uuid "d69d874a-89d2-5882-9667-c0ab124288a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is rational and less than $2$" :db/id 17592186401686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d69d874a-89d2-5882-9667-c0ab124288a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is rational and less than $2$" :db/id 17592186401686} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbf0b017-304e-58e5-9a9f-333116d74094" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c55e65c-37f4-5fad-8b00-23ee3e7e21f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-177758" :question/problem "Identify the sentence that is equivalent to the following: \"$x$ is rational, and $x$ is less than $2$ and it is rational.\"" :question/worked-solution "Using the commutative law, we can replace the phrase \"$x$ is less than $2$ and it is rational\" with the phrase \"$x$ is rational and it is less than $2.$\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$x$ is rational, and $x$ is rational and it is less than $2.$\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"$x$ is rational and $x$ is rational, and it is less than $2.$\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"$x$ is rational and $x$ is rational\" to just \"$x$ is rational.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$x$ is rational, and it is less than $2.$\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"$x$ is rational and less than $2.$\""}] [{:db/id 17592186401687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f674fcf-7f3e-53c0-8759-6f83b0820160" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Conjunction and Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af6d814d-d90e-5c43-8ac7-1f04325df5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is even and it divides $11$" :db/id 17592186401689} {:answer/id #uuid "be275bd8-9162-5a47-b7ff-a74c4eba977a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is even" :db/id 17592186401690} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac2210d-f557-57b4-a31d-f558e998356a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is even or it divides $11$ but not both" :db/id 17592186401691} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d46acf-4fcc-5090-b717-8a09704de071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ divides $11$" :db/id 17592186401692} {:answer/id #uuid "47d58c80-0a73-52c8-8da1-4b57f78ac610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is even or it divides $11$" :db/id 17592186401693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47d58c80-0a73-52c8-8da1-4b57f78ac610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is even or it divides $11$" :db/id 17592186401693} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d67412-b89b-5efb-ae9d-97880811f38f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb50cec6-7c0e-563f-899c-48d469d6d9ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-177760" :question/problem "Simplify the following sentence: $x$ is even and it is an even number, or $x$ is even or it divides $11.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the phrase \"$x$ is even and it is an even number\" to just \"$x$ is even.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$x$ is even, or $x$ is even or it divides $11.$\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma in the sentence:\n\n\"$x$ is even or $x$ is even, or it divides $11.$\"\n\nNow, we simplify the phrase \"$x$ is even or $x$ is even\" to just \"$x$ is even.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$x$ is even, or it divides $11.$\"\n\nFinally, we simplify the sentence to\n\n\"$x$ is even or it divides $11.$\""}] [{:db/id 17592186401694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa65a73-e750-5052-9249-7c096297ae51" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Minor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b9eaaa1-9a11-5e97-894b-033fdcba3183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-1$ and $k=-2$ only" :db/id 17592186401696} {:answer/id #uuid "13957928-5c88-587e-8787-1039aba9a145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=1$ only" :db/id 17592186401697} {:answer/id #uuid "23eb6ae8-6643-5983-b5dd-d782a53c5d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-2$ only" :db/id 17592186401698} {:answer/id #uuid "daaad303-eb94-54dc-b583-e74bb50bf666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=0$ and $k=-1$ only" :db/id 17592186401699} {:answer/id #uuid "01dc8965-c2ae-5ff0-b5e5-19a4d4675483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=10$ and $k=-3$ only" :db/id 17592186401700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b9eaaa1-9a11-5e97-894b-033fdcba3183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-1$ and $k=-2$ only" :db/id 17592186401696} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6e33e7-dd66-569c-9ddd-b087772632c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a62d5fa-3b38-507f-b4fe-dfd19836563a" :question/math-academy-id "q-18244" :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${a}_{22}$ is $-70$, find all the possible values of $k$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2k & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 11 & 9 & k+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and second column:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2k & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 5 & ∗ \\\\ 11 & ∗ & k+3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${a}_{22}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}2k & 6 \\\\ 11 & k+3\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}2k & 6 \\\\ 11 & k+3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2k\\cdot (k+3)-11\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${a}_{22}$ is $-70$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+6k-66 & =-70 \\\\ 2{k}^{2}+6k+4 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2}+3k+2 & =0 \\\\ (k+1)(k+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-1$ and $k=-2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9de08e2-7bf6-5ab5-bb90-7f2896dda1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Sentence Containing a Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "938c3ee2-cabf-5d4b-a82f-51aa90a79cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|=0$ and $x$ is rational" :db/id 17592186401703} {:answer/id #uuid "21140988-0342-5bc8-9482-5fe6d5552b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|\\ne 0$ and $x$ is not rational" :db/id 17592186401704} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a791d9-ddce-54ec-9234-65d0e6facd4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|=0$ and $x$ is not rational" :db/id 17592186401705} {:answer/id #uuid "03742fb6-2d4b-59f2-bd41-766fe48f2e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|=0$ or $x$ is not rational" :db/id 17592186401706} {:answer/id #uuid "5eaf2475-ccba-521f-83c3-2e8cf83dcaf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|\\ne 0$ or $x$ is not rational" :db/id 17592186401707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03742fb6-2d4b-59f2-bd41-766fe48f2e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|=0$ or $x$ is not rational" :db/id 17592186401706} :answer-field/id #uuid "f122c311-1c07-5ab1-ab5b-dbf45c9499a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "770c39e2-d9cc-5e78-8420-5f83d2044c16" :question/math-academy-id "q-182445" :question/problem "$\\,$ $|{1,x}|\\ne 0$ and $x$ is rational\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's law posits that the negation of a conjunction of two statements is equivalent to the disjunction of the negated individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(|{1,x}|\\ne 0\\text{ and }x\\text{ is rational}) & ≡¬((|{1,x}|\\ne 0)∧(x\\text{ is rational})) \\\\  & ≡¬(|{1,x}|\\ne 0)∨¬(x\\text{ is rational}) \\\\  & ≡(|{1,x}|=0)∨(x\\text{ is not rational}) \\\\  & ≡|{1,x}|=0\\text{ or }x\\text{ is not rational}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9de08e2-7bf6-5ab5-bb90-7f2896dda1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Sentence Containing a Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48c90be5-6c78-54a6-bb74-ebd1f944ea8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is not a rhombus or $Q$ is not a rectangle" :db/id 17592186401710} {:answer/id #uuid "82f495e5-01e7-5682-b038-4ef3a1fbc031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is a rhombus or $Q$ is a rectangle" :db/id 17592186401711} {:answer/id #uuid "096ba793-2074-5a71-9df9-d671228efdc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is a rhombus and $Q$ is a rectangle" :db/id 17592186401712} {:answer/id #uuid "1453d588-2b2c-5246-9c90-dbcc5f01a18d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is a rhombus or $Q$ is not a rectangle" :db/id 17592186401713} {:answer/id #uuid "b753b5df-38f2-5a86-8a5e-993702bfa764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is a rhombus and $Q$ is not a rectangle" :db/id 17592186401714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82f495e5-01e7-5682-b038-4ef3a1fbc031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is a rhombus or $Q$ is a rectangle" :db/id 17592186401711} :answer-field/id #uuid "a74c8461-811a-581d-b088-83dfb56b4446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20f8df14-2c7c-56d3-bb1c-84da27501882" :question/math-academy-id "q-182446" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P$ is not a rhombus and $Q$ is not a rectangle.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's law posits that the negation of a conjunction of two statements is equivalent to the disjunction of the negated individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,¬(P\\text{ is not a rhombus}\\text{ and }Q\\text{ is not a rectangle}) & ≡¬((P\\text{ is not a rhombus})∧(Q\\text{ is not a rectangle})) \\\\  & ≡¬(P\\text{ is not a rhombus})∨¬(Q\\text{ is not a rectangle}) \\\\  & ≡(P\\text{ is a rhombus})∨(Q\\text{ is a rectangle}) \\\\  & ≡P\\text{ is a rhombus}\\text{ or }Q\\text{ is a rectangle}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe5bd766-d624-54e3-945e-7ae37b83886a" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Sentence Containing a Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e98d0ca-b7a2-5170-9381-24b76f0454f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is not prime  or $2\\in Q$" :db/id 17592186401717} {:answer/id #uuid "b8773b7b-5555-5c11-b984-7a58534070f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is prime  and $2\\in Q$" :db/id 17592186401718} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a7643a-1d6b-5354-9347-6407878484a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is not prime  and $2\\in Q$" :db/id 17592186401719} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd83d82-9ddb-5f59-a77a-384e64a87e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is prime  or $2\\notin Q$" :db/id 17592186401720} {:answer/id #uuid "713e5e52-69ac-5388-8ad3-e0e16ad097ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is prime  or $2\\in Q$" :db/id 17592186401721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8773b7b-5555-5c11-b984-7a58534070f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is prime  and $2\\in Q$" :db/id 17592186401718} :answer-field/id #uuid "afaa16c5-b2b9-5a55-a11e-ac5052152f4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a692cde6-8afa-5365-a9a4-cb2917556510" :question/math-academy-id "q-182450" :question/problem "$\\,$ $2$ is not prime or $2\\notin Q.$\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(2\\text{ is not prime}\\text{ or }2\\notin Q) & ≡¬((2\\text{ is not prime})∨(2\\notin Q)) \\\\  & ≡¬(2\\text{ is not prime})∧¬(2\\notin Q) \\\\  & ≡(2\\text{ is prime})∧(2\\in Q) \\\\  & ≡2\\text{ is prime}\\text{ and }2\\in Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186060147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa65a73-e750-5052-9249-7c096297ae51" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Minor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205c22e6-8533-526d-adfc-b7173bb287ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=-4$ only" :db/id 17592186401723} {:answer/id #uuid "78173574-1dd2-594f-94a9-485be0dead83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=4$ and $t=5$ only" :db/id 17592186401724} {:answer/id #uuid "f493d638-8bce-58ac-912a-5338bcaaef9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=-5$ and $t=-4$ only" :db/id 17592186401725} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9478e1-7ffc-5682-8c4f-871cb83d3a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=-5$ and $t=4$ only" :db/id 17592186401726} {:answer/id #uuid "98c916e5-ba01-54de-ae08-6a77aed9f1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=5$ only" :db/id 17592186401727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78173574-1dd2-594f-94a9-485be0dead83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=4$ and $t=5$ only" :db/id 17592186401724} :answer-field/id #uuid "5695dc0f-925d-5609-ad66-b2dab30bc5b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c6851eb-a487-5113-99b9-8f1c04863168" :question/math-academy-id "q-18246" :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${p}_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =t\\cdot 9-(-8)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =9t-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9t-8 & ={t}^{2}+12 \\\\ {t}^{2}-9t+20 & =0 \\\\ (t-5)(t-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=4$ and $t=5.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56a88b2d-31e2-5c81-b7af-10adf58dcfe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't exercise regularly, and you won't improve your health." :db/id 17592186401730} {:answer/id #uuid "eeda8eae-691d-538c-893d-edf580428ade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you exercise regularly, or you'll improve your health." :db/id 17592186401731} {:answer/id #uuid "f9371ba2-5ccf-57d1-a125-df93ceb1efdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you do not exercise regularly, or you'll improve your health." :db/id 17592186401732} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5a4b12-3038-5307-aecf-3fc8e7806ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't exercise regularly, or you won't improve your health." :db/id 17592186401733} {:answer/id #uuid "beb760f6-e95b-5af4-8271-f33e3673d4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you exercise regularly, or you won't improve your health." :db/id 17592186401734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9371ba2-5ccf-57d1-a125-df93ceb1efdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you do not exercise regularly, or you'll improve your health." :db/id 17592186401732} :answer-field/id #uuid "1369236f-0d85-59b3-8f09-5b7bf8330423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3eb0922-de2b-5aa7-8232-87f927107273" :question/math-academy-id "q-182507" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you exercise regularly, then you'll improve your health.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You exercise regularly.\n\n- $B:$ You will improve your health.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You do not exercise regularly.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You do not exercise regularly, or you will improve your health.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you do not exercise regularly, or you'll improve your health.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true."}] [{:db/id 17592186401735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a0563d-13f2-5256-b18a-47d8b5f64041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert." :db/id 17592186401737} {:answer/id #uuid "f6731b6b-f0cf-55d7-87cc-7c66343c06ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, and you cannot enter the concert." :db/id 17592186401738} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b9de9c-dfe1-5e38-920b-ffe3d2c1e6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't buy a ticket, or you can enter the concert." :db/id 17592186401739} {:answer/id #uuid "62a4e17c-f6e3-5d49-ae77-c163f6da3e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't buy a ticket, and you cannot enter the concert." :db/id 17592186401740} {:answer/id #uuid "20b3068d-8214-5c62-ae9b-55f280ed1b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, or you can enter the concert." :db/id 17592186401741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34a0563d-13f2-5256-b18a-47d8b5f64041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert." :db/id 17592186401737} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f6ea998-1510-5ac2-a229-6d71f403c27f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc2aafac-bd5b-5b06-b1c9-744a025e7988" :question/math-academy-id "q-182570" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you don't buy a ticket, then you cannot enter the concert.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't buy a ticket.\n\n- $B:$ You cannot enter the concert.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You buy a ticket.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert."}] [{:db/id 17592186401742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048fd58d-6161-542c-aca0-9aa3830ec64a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Wear a jacket, and you won't get cold." :db/id 17592186401744} {:answer/id #uuid "2c28a06a-e16c-5c23-a019-7042d53b30bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you wear a jacket, then you will get cold." :db/id 17592186401745} {:answer/id #uuid "1568255d-6e74-51c6-b213-0baccfa15504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don’t wear a jacket, then you won't get cold." :db/id 17592186401746} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1a52bd-b4c3-59f6-aa7e-00e77fc5e146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don’t wear a jacket, then you will get cold." :db/id 17592186401747} {:answer/id #uuid "7703df10-5599-5c0e-b2b9-a7aaaab873cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you wear a jacket, then you won't get cold." :db/id 17592186401748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f1a52bd-b4c3-59f6-aa7e-00e77fc5e146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don’t wear a jacket, then you will get cold." :db/id 17592186401747} :answer-field/id #uuid "970b9ff4-c62f-56de-9540-47a07451659c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98ecba05-28ef-5ea8-9d45-86a0357f6077" :question/math-academy-id "q-182592" :question/problem "$\\,$ Wear a jacket, or you will get cold.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Wear a jacket.\n\n- $B:$ You will get cold.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't wear a jacket.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't wear a jacket, then you will get cold."}] [{:db/id 17592186060156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202c8237-3888-53f9-b30f-02e259152607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you water the plants, they will grow." :db/id 17592186401750} {:answer/id #uuid "118d90f3-6d32-5afe-8e02-cbf41ba95880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't water the plants, they will grow." :db/id 17592186401751} {:answer/id #uuid "89772d8b-75f5-5288-933b-dbba82a0628b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't water the plants, they won't grow." :db/id 17592186401752} {:answer/id #uuid "a723dd49-b3ce-5d3b-8bbe-d9d5caf0b650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you water the plants, they won't grow." :db/id 17592186401753} {:answer/id #uuid "69265ef0-339f-5ed1-8244-d0a8e9f42458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Water the plants, and they will grow." :db/id 17592186401754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "202c8237-3888-53f9-b30f-02e259152607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you water the plants, they will grow." :db/id 17592186401750} :answer-field/id #uuid "95a3fcff-39ee-51ea-961b-901f1fa75b71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c37bd0c3-9779-5d09-a1bb-d6c4e0fab0c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-182595" :question/problem "$\\,$ Either you do not water the plants, or they will grow.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not water the plants.\n\n- $B:$ The plants will grow.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You water the plants.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, then they will grow.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, they will grow.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true."}] [{:db/id 17592186401755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c0b4ea8-c305-52fb-b738-39b52cd65224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\notin N,$ or $x$ is not positive." :db/id 17592186401757} {:answer/id #uuid "0f89fefa-c3c4-5941-854f-f243eb5a7db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\in N,$ and $x$ is positive." :db/id 17592186401758} {:answer/id #uuid "fa092300-5642-58f4-a90f-6ea2281b0263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\in N,$ or $x$ is positive." :db/id 17592186401759} {:answer/id #uuid "064a345b-f5c1-5e19-82b0-253407d0787b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\notin N,$ and $x$ is positive." :db/id 17592186401760} {:answer/id #uuid "54036453-852b-5e55-ab90-eafbae198c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\notin N,$ or $x$ is positive." :db/id 17592186401761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54036453-852b-5e55-ab90-eafbae198c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\notin N,$ or $x$ is positive." :db/id 17592186401761} :answer-field/id #uuid "75c6802b-9187-56f8-8a12-dde53fdaa099" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d8f44ce-e231-5920-980e-3c7663e37fa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-183349" :question/problem "$\\,$ If $x\\in N,$ then  $x$ is positive.\n\nWhich of the following is logically equivalent to the conditional predicate above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x\\in N.$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is positive.\n\nThen, our initial sentence can be written as $P(x)⇒Q(x),$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬P(x)∨Q(x).\n$$\n\nThe negation of $P(x)$ can be written as\n\n- $¬P(x):$ $x\\notin N.$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\notin N,$ or $x$ is positive."}] [{:db/id 17592186401762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f58690c6-735d-5579-a228-58b5fdedcdab" :knowledge-point/title "Negating Universal Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81cd1e65-4f9b-54b2-b6b0-e90a5052ee66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $t\\in Q$ such that $t<1.$" :db/id 17592186401764} {:answer/id #uuid "58924096-406f-5237-89e1-91e281ef716f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $t\\in Q$ such that $t\\ge 1.$" :db/id 17592186401765} {:answer/id #uuid "8d041320-296d-5ba1-9fc8-c4862754bf6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186401766} {:answer/id #uuid "bf93f7c8-8f44-5fe2-86fb-e73b3d7712a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $t\\in Q,$ $t<1.$" :db/id 17592186401767} {:answer/id #uuid "ce711d41-3a67-5509-82bc-c7e212d267b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $t\\in Q,$ $t\\ge 1.$" :db/id 17592186401768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58924096-406f-5237-89e1-91e281ef716f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $t\\in Q$ such that $t\\ge 1.$" :db/id 17592186401765} :answer-field/id #uuid "57eaefa3-f6a7-50a6-968d-23bf7fb1f0aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "030c3e29-d504-53d7-8a01-ca59114f9c38" :question/math-academy-id "q-183452" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ For all $t\\in Q,$ $t<1.$" :question/worked-solution "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(t):t<1,\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀t\\in Q,\\,P(t).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n¬(∀t\\in Q,\\,P(t))≡∃t\\in Q,\\,¬P(t).\n$$\n\nFinally, $∃t\\in Q,\\,¬P(t)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $t\\in Q$ such that $t\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f58690c6-735d-5579-a228-58b5fdedcdab" :knowledge-point/title "Negating Universal Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9706d3-878e-5f5a-8d71-1f8c29f93104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186401771} {:answer/id #uuid "9d92cd18-3e84-5f45-ac52-3e75689e1424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some prime numbers are divisible by $5.$" :db/id 17592186401772} {:answer/id #uuid "102af750-9b07-5a1d-88e3-59388e6a3dcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some prime numbers are not divisible by $5.$" :db/id 17592186401773} {:answer/id #uuid "511f29ba-6dd5-5d0f-881a-33e6cf9c983e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All prime numbers are not divisible by $5.$" :db/id 17592186401774} {:answer/id #uuid "ffff6c41-3a54-56aa-b5dd-2fff15e337fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All prime numbers are divisible by $5.$" :db/id 17592186401775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d92cd18-3e84-5f45-ac52-3e75689e1424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some prime numbers are divisible by $5.$" :db/id 17592186401772} :answer-field/id #uuid "0352b364-ca54-5ac4-b327-306591f5283f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2d99ed4b-1e64-50ce-9a89-e8a256193ea9" :question/math-academy-id "q-183463" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ All prime numbers are not divisible by $5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $P$ be the set of prime numbers. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all $x\\in P,$ $x$ is not divisible by $5.$\n\nIntroducing the universal quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\,$ $∀x\\in P,\\,x\\text{ is not divisible by }5.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀x\\in P,\\,x\\text{ is not divisible by }5.) & ≡∃x\\in P,\\,¬(x\\text{ is not divisible by }5.) \\\\  & ≡∃x\\in P,\\,x\\text{ is divisible by }5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\,$ There exists $x\\in P$ such that $x$ is divisible by $5.$\n\nor simply\n\n$\\,$ Some prime numbers are divisible by $5.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb609a7e-467e-5003-b4c4-2a1637977443" :knowledge-point/title "Negating Existential Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57d31c79-99cb-527d-9b34-2a7dcf7d4269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $n\\in N$ such that $\\frac{n}{10}\\in N.$" :db/id 17592186401778} {:answer/id #uuid "d1430d2c-821a-5588-9653-ab0e459b688b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $n\\in N,$ $\\frac{n}{10}\\notin N.$" :db/id 17592186401779} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d17dc4-c4c3-507c-a879-cb1df4136b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $n\\in N$ such that $\\frac{n}{10}\\notin N.$" :db/id 17592186401780} {:answer/id #uuid "647dcb52-a68e-5b8f-9cfc-7730d5fdb6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186401781} {:answer/id #uuid "aea50b23-830d-5f10-9d25-173f5ad0d82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $n\\in N,$ $\\frac{n}{10}\\in N.$" :db/id 17592186401782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1430d2c-821a-5588-9653-ab0e459b688b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $n\\in N,$ $\\frac{n}{10}\\notin N.$" :db/id 17592186401779} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed16752d-c309-52b1-87e1-9ddbf2957675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58bb6ca7-db5e-5a82-931b-128a7693b5ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-183467" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $n\\in N$ such that $\\frac{n}{10}\\in N.$" :question/worked-solution "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(n):\\frac{n}{10}\\in N,\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n¬(∃n\\in N,\\,P(n))≡∀n\\in N,\\,¬P(n).\n$$\n\nFinally, $∀n\\in N,\\,¬P(n)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $n\\in N,$ $\\frac{n}{10}\\notin N.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93249d2-e5f0-5767-98da-c33b837b41c0" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92013b7a-990a-5205-8cac-7e0776136a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $x\\in Q$ such that ${x}^{2}=3.$" :db/id 17592186401785} {:answer/id #uuid "950fa403-afb4-5ef8-b103-f21d430d61d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $x\\in Q,$ ${x}^{2}=3.$" :db/id 17592186401786} {:answer/id #uuid "abd7aa3d-2334-5e0e-9c0b-fe55f310ada0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $x\\in Q,$ ${x}^{2}\\ne 3.$" :db/id 17592186401787} {:answer/id #uuid "8081b467-0c02-5669-af98-e691fb189d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For some  $x\\in Q,$ ${x}^{2}\\ne 3.$" :db/id 17592186401788} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3c28fc-2e79-5ffc-919d-ed38b081ed6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $x\\in (-3,3)$ such that $x\\in Q.$" :db/id 17592186401789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92013b7a-990a-5205-8cac-7e0776136a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $x\\in Q$ such that ${x}^{2}=3.$" :db/id 17592186401785} :answer-field/id #uuid "44d896ba-9353-5e53-bd26-80e136208868" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8238e2be-16fc-5278-bef2-5c7c9ad7976d" :question/math-academy-id "q-183515" :question/problem "$\\,$ $S(x):$ ${x}^{2}=3$\n\nGiven the predicate above, which of the following expresses the statement $∃x\\in Q,\\,S(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The existential quantifier \"$∃$\" means \"there exists.\" So, the statement \n$∃x\\in Q,\\,S(x)$ \nreads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $x\\in Q$ **such that** ${x}^{2}=3.$\n\n**Note:** This statement is false."}] [{:db/id 17592186401790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d463a0dd-784d-55b6-a598-60819f5330d7" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Nested Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63028878-7dd8-5666-a703-3a2244219cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a rational number $x$ such that for all $ϵ>0,$ $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id 17592186401792} {:answer/id #uuid "e32e5fd4-f7df-549f-91bc-ddfd82e7ec10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a number $x$ such that for all $ϵ,$ $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id 17592186401793} {:answer/id #uuid "b112f284-46d7-52f3-b2ff-ee95f01c9a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Given any rational number $ϵ>0$ there exists a real number $x$ such that $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id 17592186401794} {:answer/id #uuid "6c332157-7d64-5ef0-b617-8736954ebfc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a positive real number $ϵ$ such that for all rational numbers $x,$ $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id 17592186401795} {:answer/id #uuid "9407de98-85a3-5b7e-8d73-44f5d91469ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Given any real number $ϵ>0$ there exists a rational number $x$ such that $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id 17592186401796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9407de98-85a3-5b7e-8d73-44f5d91469ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Given any real number $ϵ>0$ there exists a rational number $x$ such that $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id 17592186401796} :answer-field/id #uuid "55efd161-25e4-5edd-a99d-363937149d24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "090f259e-6817-5d8c-bd8b-c0774c7e3270" :question/math-academy-id "q-183573" :question/problem "$\\,$ $∀\\,ϵ\\in {R}^{+},\\,∃\\,x\\in Q,\\,-ϵ<x<ϵ.$\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?" :question/worked-solution "Our quantified statement reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $ϵ\\in {R}^{+},$ **there exists** $x\\in Q$ **such that** $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$ \n\nThis can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Given any real number $ϵ>0$ there exists a rational number $x$ such that $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d463a0dd-784d-55b6-a598-60819f5330d7" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Nested Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6790e841-0c6a-511d-9596-104d0fba6854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a real number $x$ such that $x+y\\ne 0$ for any real number $y.$" :db/id 17592186401799} {:answer/id #uuid "87a788ca-e694-5ab2-b2c8-99b2516244ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all real numbers $x$ and $y,$ $x+y\\ne 0.$" :db/id 17592186401800} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecf925c-30cf-53ea-8ea6-eb003db73991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a real number $x$ such that $x+y=0$ for any real number $y.$" :db/id 17592186401801} {:answer/id #uuid "24715df5-3648-57cf-a0c2-7c8757e47346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all real number $x$ there is another real number $y$ such that $x+y=0.$" :db/id 17592186401802} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8dff61-a1b8-558a-96c5-06cbb8e259d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are real numbers $x$ and $y$ such that $x+y=0.$" :db/id 17592186401803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ecf925c-30cf-53ea-8ea6-eb003db73991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a real number $x$ such that $x+y=0$ for any real number $y.$" :db/id 17592186401801} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e399198-cdcc-5a53-af6f-9602b7c93771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9724aea-cc2a-5bda-9ffa-87b9cd25835a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183599" :question/problem "$\\,$ $∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,x+y=0$\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?" :question/worked-solution "Our quantified statement reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $x\\in R$ **such that**, **for all** $y\\in R,\\,x+y=0$ \n\nThis can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a real number $x$ such that $x+y=0$ for any real number $y.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7578c48-cc9e-5170-8ffe-5ebee6580677" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Decimals in One Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c397f249-716c-58ab-a9bf-91bfc3f7d868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0.5,\\,b=-1.4" :db/id 17592186401806} {:answer/id #uuid "2dec0c3e-7449-5041-96b3-12d8ef936e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.5,\\,b=2.4" :db/id 17592186401807} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc082a1-ed8d-5fbd-b12d-2a2df6063e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.2,\\,b=1.4" :db/id 17592186401808} {:answer/id #uuid "3da6187f-1bd3-5425-a6fa-9cc0e764d299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-1.2,\\,b=0.4" :db/id 17592186401809} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8550aa-5225-5217-b55a-8b4d6c0d09f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0.2,\\,b=1.7" :db/id 17592186401810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cc082a1-ed8d-5fbd-b12d-2a2df6063e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.2,\\,b=1.4" :db/id 17592186401808} :answer-field/id #uuid "4994c6c6-b044-5fa6-ae9d-9a350ba14b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8748ba37-2d9a-5451-b497-621aa22378b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-18688" :question/problem "Find the solution of the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 \\\\ 1.5a+0.5b=2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 &  \\\\ 1.5a+0.5b=2.5 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 \\\\ 15a+5b=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=2a-1\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $a,$ we can substitute $b=2a-1$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15a+5(2a-1) & =25 \\\\ 15a+10a-5 & =25 \\\\ 25a & =30 \\\\ a & =\\frac{30}{25} \\\\ a & =1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $a=1.2$ into the  first equation to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =2(1.2)-1 \\\\  & =2.4-1 \\\\  & =1.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $a=1.2$ and $b=1.4.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9699341b-c4e2-547f-8d98-bcda30567272" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a34f86b-faa2-5844-9680-3f6951dc4ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x+8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id 17592186401813} {:answer/id #uuid "63214331-a811-5bf2-8cb7-4da5c8c1d678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{cot}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}csc⁡x" :db/id 17592186401814} {:answer/id #uuid "2285f6af-f131-5a62-954e-164127014717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id 17592186401815} {:answer/id #uuid "5a35c415-50b4-5005-8173-6121abbd643e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}csc⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id 17592186401816} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8e226f-a28c-5b1a-b7df-6f312bfd06a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x+8{x}^{3}tan⁡x" :db/id 17592186401817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2285f6af-f131-5a62-954e-164127014717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id 17592186401815} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7aeb284-4f80-510f-8935-07a7e23c626b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee4e083b-fcc9-5eb5-9a27-ac52f9a97afd" :question/math-academy-id "q-1913" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=-2{x}^{4}cot⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{4}cot⁡x) \\\\  & =(-8{x}^{3})(cot⁡x)+(-2{x}^{4})(-{csc}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x+2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1ec1379-ce50-53c7-b04d-3380676ebeb9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e41ae0ea-e83b-581f-a124-c29c127545ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.46" :db/id 17592186401820} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddc81bc-6b74-5b86-9aa6-68b7985e21ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4.94" :db/id 17592186401821} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2d52e0-5f90-57ca-a8a9-c0f4aaadbdfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=10.03" :db/id 17592186401822} {:answer/id #uuid "e34561e5-c74f-5726-99b2-cd744a8924ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.24" :db/id 17592186401823} {:answer/id #uuid "eea394a2-6e5a-5394-a910-d19a50a2530e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=8.36" :db/id 17592186401824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e41ae0ea-e83b-581f-a124-c29c127545ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.46" :db/id 17592186401820} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab640647-5232-553a-be5a-08e8539221b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59ea1544-758d-5928-bf3f-4995dd8b61f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-19299" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=4{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{2}-2t+1,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=2.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{2}-2t+1) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-t}+6t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =-4{e}^{-2}+6\\cdot 2-2 \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-2}+10 \\\\  & =9.458\\,658… \\\\  & ≈9.46\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1ec1379-ce50-53c7-b04d-3380676ebeb9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9733cf69-5d70-514e-a80d-eadad1d41489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=6.67" :db/id 17592186401827} {:answer/id #uuid "85286b89-c2a3-56c9-a420-e9d18189a069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=8.05" :db/id 17592186401828} {:answer/id #uuid "259c571e-8927-56b2-95c0-5bb1688deec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4.33" :db/id 17592186401829} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebd6114-b8d6-5140-a703-c4dd01c13274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=3.75" :db/id 17592186401830} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2eab8c-d466-5781-91a5-1f6a93c6b3d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=0.22" :db/id 17592186401831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "259c571e-8927-56b2-95c0-5bb1688deec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4.33" :db/id 17592186401829} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc005a4-0a0a-5369-9aa9-e0229fc91626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3df15b1d-5c6a-5a31-88c3-94f27234c33f" :question/math-academy-id "q-19302" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}-2t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=3.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t-2+\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=3,$ we substitute $t=3$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =2(3)-2+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =6-2+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\  & =4.333\\,333… \\\\  & ≈4.33\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1d3691b-a1a5-5e81-bfe2-760939975d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id 17592186401834} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebae067-1d98-5398-842f-52e5beff87e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id 17592186401835} {:answer/id #uuid "1270a921-f7ad-5051-be4e-3a8d359de937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id 17592186401836} {:answer/id #uuid "83d51c96-b38e-5a00-a86c-b8ab59d290d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id 17592186401837} {:answer/id #uuid "dcada4a4-8b8c-5274-9b06-9d7f2171686e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}" :db/id 17592186401838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcada4a4-8b8c-5274-9b06-9d7f2171686e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}" :db/id 17592186401838} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c042d42-f6d6-52fa-992c-350b4e969301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2001916-7ed2-504f-9a8e-d02fda09253a" :question/math-academy-id "q-19493" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${x}^{1/3}$ about $x=8$ to approximate the value of ${7}^{1/3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=8.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/3} \\\\ f(8) & ={8}^{1/3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{-2/3}=\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/3} & =2+\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}(x-8)+⋯ \\\\  & =2+\\frac{x-8}{12}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ into the above to approximate ${7}^{1/3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{1/3} & ≈2+\\frac{7-8}{12} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7c8f4da-c782-51e0-ac51-87a89f31860d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the System of Equations Associated With an Augmented Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ddbc91f-a2bc-5c20-979a-ce7e906dc06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z=1 \\\\ 3x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+4z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401841} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1394d8-a03f-5e29-aaba-bb0176d8f59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ 3x-2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+4z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401842} {:answer/id #uuid "9761f4c8-5981-5fd5-88ce-d6d56601691c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ 3x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+4z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401843} {:answer/id #uuid "13919b67-24a3-5f25-90aa-a16013b5b7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401844} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7be1b8-6206-50f4-9b88-856c8d8e718a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ 3x+y+3z=-2 \\\\ x+3y+4z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9761f4c8-5981-5fd5-88ce-d6d56601691c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ 3x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+4z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401843} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8af118b-89c1-56df-aee8-e8ea929b9eb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5cb76d6-8596-56c0-bf91-5764390c6dbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-20362" :question/problem "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix consists of two parts: the coefficient matrix and the column of constants.\n\nThe coefficient matrix represents the coefficients in the linear system, while the column of constants represents the constants on the right-hand sides of the equations.\n\nUsing the variables $x$, $y$, and $z,$ then, the corresponding system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ 3x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+4z=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id 17592186401848} {:answer/id #uuid "7b15bc55-e6d8-5c21-b55e-568e588591b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186401849} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8c26cd-3204-5045-8920-160928c0068e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186401850} {:answer/id #uuid "7828179c-427e-5c33-a7ae-bd0edbe05cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id 17592186401851} {:answer/id #uuid "db0ee922-0122-505b-abc7-21b2699ea220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id 17592186401852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id 17592186401848} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec6f4c2-cef2-5136-bda0-046075ea56a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a11f695c-004e-57f4-b1f5-cd09434f69e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-20851" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{t},\\,y=12t+1,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{12}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}}=12\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =12\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =12\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186106155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f742e7-89c6-543b-b43a-f41c27f35abf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Singular 3x3 System of Equations with One Pivot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b345fefc-1a1b-5902-8dd5-899abe1effb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id 17592186401854} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5fc700-54a5-5fd4-baab-ddc94fdb3fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id 17592186401855} {:answer/id #uuid "85a5ea74-3dfe-53d7-a75c-1f909a7f8bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id 17592186401856} {:answer/id #uuid "c137c596-06bd-536e-868a-22fa042d4332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id 17592186401857} {:answer/id #uuid "01f0000f-6b48-549c-8c07-55de9522db7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id 17592186401858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c137c596-06bd-536e-868a-22fa042d4332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id 17592186401857} :answer-field/id #uuid "81971273-5ed1-5672-ab54-cbe5f79a2ee1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93d7b7ec-0149-5969-9f8f-fedef6cf0475" :question/math-academy-id "q-20966" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+4z=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx-y+4z=-1\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=-1+y-4z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+y-4z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$, $z$ (free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+y-4z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "886ea0dd-2f3b-5230-a912-ca494bbb228c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id 17592186401861} {:answer/id #uuid "1b58ab2c-a7ae-5296-9ea9-aaaf06b0d86c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186401862} {:answer/id #uuid "3480faea-4f30-5410-8c55-291a77967982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id 17592186401863} {:answer/id #uuid "1933a3b0-751e-5adc-a9a1-355a8352674b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id 17592186401864} {:answer/id #uuid "f9dd7c11-42ae-55e1-8e09-7e1133cc18c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id 17592186401865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9dd7c11-42ae-55e1-8e09-7e1133cc18c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id 17592186401865} :answer-field/id #uuid "532bfa22-570e-5521-a58b-09bf6df9181b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c14e172-0d42-58ef-86b2-05fb93de9caa" :question/math-academy-id "q-20990" :question/problem "![](images/58/5855e92b04ffbf8447d32c7edb190cd16046a13e8808e6ffaade1f59e414c869.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=2t{e}^{t},\\,y={t}^{2}+2t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(t+1),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t+2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t+2}{2{e}^{t}(t+1)}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186083619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177fc0fe-e31a-5f41-9aab-356a0006fb3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ only" :db/id 17592186401867} {:answer/id #uuid "10683790-7483-51aa-9bcd-7831b9d3d041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$" :db/id 17592186401868} {:answer/id #uuid "1843433f-102c-52fd-970b-a78c3d6b3a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ only" :db/id 17592186401869} {:answer/id #uuid "50097778-e660-56b2-929d-8baced007acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$" :db/id 17592186401870} {:answer/id #uuid "835e0688-6998-5bbb-863e-83fa6322632b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ only" :db/id 17592186401871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10683790-7483-51aa-9bcd-7831b9d3d041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$" :db/id 17592186401868} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9d95bd4-f167-53f1-8c45-da39995abe7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbd878d8-e318-5109-beb9-89ecefca7f9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-21081" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a58988e-9e60-53e1-9ccc-6195ac2bdc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401874} {:answer/id #uuid "c4824b8b-2ed7-5c3c-ae44-9e7e39c2adaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401875} {:answer/id #uuid "8e755153-eb7c-5c80-ac13-6bd2f55ebc06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401876} {:answer/id #uuid "8dadc22b-b156-5944-b045-f3c498f81787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401877} {:answer/id #uuid "89771be0-5923-5fe1-8923-30d66256f236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89771be0-5923-5fe1-8923-30d66256f236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id 17592186401878} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3d97d84-3165-5293-95f8-8ad9ae1b81ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a60afb71-a1f2-5589-86e8-84aaf38f221a" :question/math-academy-id "q-21353" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4aa2bca8faab077c47fb968882579233babe7d323ba3120088e8ea5599fbd570.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={e}^{t},\\,y=t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=2$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "875d7f2a-43ec-5635-8e72-48ae83d0339b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ba36cad-3d3b-52fe-8da9-534ffd79e6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $x\\in (1,\\infty ),\\,\\,{x}^{2}>x.$" :db/id 17592186401881} {:answer/id #uuid "d6110e32-9a48-58f5-a9da-5ed70f94bf07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For some $x\\in (1,\\infty ),\\,\\,{x}^{2}>x.$" :db/id 17592186401882} {:answer/id #uuid "738c391b-269d-5c3a-8b74-cd075c624a45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists at least one number $x\\in (1,\\infty )$ such that ${x}^{2}>x.$" :db/id 17592186401883} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d4a42d-e83d-55f4-8466-098995f56994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If ${x}^{2}>x,$ then $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$" :db/id 17592186401884} {:answer/id #uuid "07c9511c-28dc-5f7a-9d27-cc053b0e17fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all real numbers $x,$ either $x\\in (1,\\infty )$ or ${x}^{2}>x.$" :db/id 17592186401885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ba36cad-3d3b-52fe-8da9-534ffd79e6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $x\\in (1,\\infty ),\\,\\,{x}^{2}>x.$" :db/id 17592186401881} :answer-field/id #uuid "da2f987f-545e-5531-8f85-0b57a2cd730e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6336cc3e-143c-5d52-83b4-52b2733f351b" :question/math-academy-id "q-216463" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):{x}^{2}>x$\n\nGiven the predicate above, which of the following expresses the statement $∀x\\in (1,\\infty ),\\,P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The universal quantifier \"$∀$\" means \"for all.\" So, the statement \n$∀x\\in (1,\\infty ),\\,P(x)$ \nreads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $x\\in (1,\\infty ),$ ${x}^{2}>x.$\n\n**Note:** This statement is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186401886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb609a7e-467e-5003-b4c4-2a1637977443" :knowledge-point/title "Negating Existential Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5119e50-bd14-5fee-9b82-2344f54225fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $y\\in R,$ $\\frac{y}{2}$ is not real." :db/id 17592186401888} {:answer/id #uuid "36b80171-403c-5326-9454-7cb78c2f2f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $y\\in R,$ $\\frac{y}{2}$ is real." :db/id 17592186401889} {:answer/id #uuid "45708669-67c8-542c-9a91-dd315dbb834d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed." :db/id 17592186401890} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e7f6e8-1e52-5cc5-98e3-04a7bbd84474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $y\\in R$ such that $\\frac{y}{2}$ is real." :db/id 17592186401891} {:answer/id #uuid "45896d61-d63d-552c-858f-190cb028d8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $y\\in R$ such that $\\frac{y}{2}$ is not real." :db/id 17592186401892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36b80171-403c-5326-9454-7cb78c2f2f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $y\\in R,$ $\\frac{y}{2}$ is real." :db/id 17592186401889} :answer-field/id #uuid "24925563-239f-5c15-a72e-dbb52acac1ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "804b67f2-c953-5690-bdac-d7da623929f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-216689" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $y\\in R$ such that $\\frac{y}{2}$ is not real." :question/worked-solution "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(y):\\frac{y}{2}\\text{ is real},\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∃y\\in R,\\,¬P(y).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∃y\\in R,\\,¬P(y)) & ≡∀y\\in R,\\,¬¬P(y) \\\\  & ≡∀y\\in R,\\,P(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $∀y\\in R,\\,P(y)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $y\\in R,$ $\\frac{y}{2}$ is real."}] [{:db/id 17592186401893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41b7d83f-3b9b-556c-9e49-13df9c3b597e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f423be97-7cf1-5892-a889-cd6b8dad197b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡x|}+C" :db/id 17592186401895} {:answer/id #uuid "a1be16c0-5889-5190-9b94-52c61fcd25d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡x|+C" :db/id 17592186401896} {:answer/id #uuid "e01f3245-4ea5-537d-ba0e-1dda5e71ae4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2})+C" :db/id 17592186401897} {:answer/id #uuid "0802bc99-bb18-5591-b8e0-f242435e7f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡x|+C" :db/id 17592186401898} {:answer/id #uuid "99159f55-3655-56b7-986e-53df7b0da90a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡x|}+C" :db/id 17592186401899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0802bc99-bb18-5591-b8e0-f242435e7f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡x|+C" :db/id 17592186401898} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff01c23-3bf9-54e8-b3ae-815e53dd5059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6e0472f6-3d3e-5a0e-bbc2-09db07e6e756" :question/math-academy-id "q-2194" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "793fda82-4b8f-5357-8355-7eb714c2f363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id 17592186401902} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a3c59d-ed02-5238-a8c6-fd2024482e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-t" :db/id 17592186401903} {:answer/id #uuid "f6cd580d-4800-5b0c-92e0-e1600068f58e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id 17592186401904} {:answer/id #uuid "b627bbcd-b10a-5d39-a559-125ce37018ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+t" :db/id 17592186401905} {:answer/id #uuid "ee753101-7bb5-5dbb-a4a6-c54e0820154f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186401906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6a3c59d-ed02-5238-a8c6-fd2024482e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-t" :db/id 17592186401903} :answer-field/id #uuid "618ade45-7715-5c2b-8e23-5edac27f8883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6706b3e3-ba1f-5e48-a0c6-87c0de600ccd" :question/math-academy-id "q-225023" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v(2-u),\\,y=u{v}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $1\\ge u\\ge 0$ and $v=1.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v(2-u),\\,u{v}^{3}) \\\\  & =(1(2-u),\\,u(1)^{3}) \\\\  & =(2-u,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2-u,\\,y=u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d42c473-b845-56f6-91ed-2f7569cdadf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from   $(1,1)$ to $(0,2).$" :db/id 17592186401909} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd13488-86ae-53ef-94b1-8445c40feae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,2)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id 17592186401910} {:answer/id #uuid "2eaf5793-614c-59c2-8266-69fcc04e0964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$" :db/id 17592186401911} {:answer/id #uuid "1cea9769-6304-556d-88e7-17fabaf6e8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$" :db/id 17592186401912} {:answer/id #uuid "132e7131-949c-5374-87b4-fafc2b9fee75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,2)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id 17592186401913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2eaf5793-614c-59c2-8266-69fcc04e0964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$" :db/id 17592186401911} :answer-field/id #uuid "47132c57-ee0e-5ec3-b95e-67a15913786d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bd99021-9f7b-5102-9e9a-342e79f39167" :question/math-academy-id "q-225228" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v-1}{u+1},\\,y=(u+v-1)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(\\frac{v-1}{u+1},\\,(u+v-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{v-1}{0+1},\\,(0+v-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,(v-1)^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=(v-1)^{2}+1,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=t-1,\\,y=(t-1)^{2}+1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (\\frac{1-1}{0+1},(0+1-1)^{2}+1)=(0,1) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (\\frac{0-1}{0+1},(0+0-1)^{2}+1)=(-1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$\n\n![](images/69/69a2a8ab136360c01d1a17a58dc2fc4864ae2089330a551df913b200d923f087.png)"}] [{:db/id 17592186401914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc62d8df-450f-5f7a-b647-b53c051f8339" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Decimals in Both Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c29c57bf-e265-52a1-bca7-e9f5dad2f3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{4}{3},$ $y=-2$" :db/id 17592186401916} {:answer/id #uuid "211a2e7b-9745-58a0-94ae-5560db869797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1,$ $y=-3$" :db/id 17592186401917} {:answer/id #uuid "862aa3fb-5e83-5622-8209-8caba2d722df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{7}{3},$ $y=1$" :db/id 17592186401918} {:answer/id #uuid "30d0a7a2-ddd3-5355-ab33-3b6c7d057ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2,$ $y=0$" :db/id 17592186401919} {:answer/id #uuid "0284c37f-87d7-5181-9665-5eb50a2bc860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{5}{3},$ $y=-1$" :db/id 17592186401920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0284c37f-87d7-5181-9665-5eb50a2bc860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{5}{3},$ $y=-1$" :db/id 17592186401920} :answer-field/id #uuid "e422abe5-7836-5aa6-bfb2-34896476a4d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d7c59ff-df86-5863-a05a-c20fb84b2ead" :question/math-academy-id "q-26223" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.3x-0.1y=0.6 \\\\ 0.6x+0.8y=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply both equations by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.3x-0.1y=0.6 & |\\,\\times 10 \\\\ 0.6x+0.8y=0.2 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=6 \\\\ 6x+8y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we divide the second equation by $(-2),$ we will get a $-3x$ term in the second equation that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=6 &  \\\\ 6x+8y=2 & |\\,÷(-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=6 \\\\ -3x-4y=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3x}-y & =6 \\\\ -\\cancel{3x}-4y & =-1 \\\\ (-y-4y) & =(6-1) \\\\ -5y & =5 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-1$ into the equation $3x-y=6$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y & =6 \\\\ 3x-(-1) & =6 \\\\ 3x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ and $y=-1.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c3666f3-ba98-51a9-b678-f25e043d3a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+3.5" :db/id 17592186401923} {:answer/id #uuid "e8039e0c-f293-5ce4-b395-a4064a0685bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}-2.5" :db/id 17592186401924} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f24666-89d0-5332-bb2a-21f966094be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}-3.5" :db/id 17592186401925} {:answer/id #uuid "a479a80c-f0e1-5b4f-9f67-06b99cd086bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+1.5" :db/id 17592186401926} {:answer/id #uuid "266f8f59-63e6-5980-a4b9-749b4fb8201c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+2.5" :db/id 17592186401927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "266f8f59-63e6-5980-a4b9-749b4fb8201c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+2.5" :db/id 17592186401927} :answer-field/id #uuid "828a7f8a-1289-571e-8aa4-3a396be14d44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6948e752-4360-51fe-b06f-c6885f4f282f" :question/math-academy-id "q-27482" :question/problem "![](images/c0/c091fc5d742ddca3090725974f00ff7da77b1530d601bb82e288518b27970b0a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.5.$ Therefore, $c=2.5.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186115711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e366677-1acc-548b-9d8a-043caa67d076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186401929} {:answer/id #uuid "34c4cf88-6a77-5dc4-9298-de12464e93a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186401930} {:answer/id #uuid "fc388d3c-f7de-526c-bae0-44297aa5c1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186401931} {:answer/id #uuid "8e52e4ac-f6da-59c1-9b6c-569b5c0f46d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186401932} {:answer/id #uuid "87f49733-d7a3-52db-ba58-c47681214494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186401933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc388d3c-f7de-526c-bae0-44297aa5c1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186401931} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae9fdfce-f210-5819-a39f-6d6ef14bee25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcb31a9b-9679-5d24-8bf7-7929bd2e8a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-29163" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=1$\nII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$\nIII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id 17592186401934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a258070e-5c3c-595e-81d6-3e0f9b9a5daf" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Mixed Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c28cb971-d849-577e-9682-a8890d4ae773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401936} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c91422-9ef9-5a21-a3cd-6168971db223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401937} {:answer/id #uuid "928ea34b-f94c-5914-8894-15d7edbe4d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401938} {:answer/id #uuid "129beb51-d7c5-5a45-86b1-53f54c6e7926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge x+2 \\\\ y<-x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401939} {:answer/id #uuid "1f5ea53c-6d9f-56de-99b0-c8f54f45ac23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y<-x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "928ea34b-f94c-5914-8894-15d7edbe4d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186401938} :answer-field/id #uuid "210acf01-110f-53da-bf3c-8725abe1a7d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cb8229e-2a6a-582b-a6c3-627b88cf6816" :question/math-academy-id "q-29384" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a740aafbb9f74e2681506d3027f848fa92e7eea2bac3e08ccaf3fc7e0acf8fa1.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown in the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge -x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074992e8-e1fa-5890-859b-ab91c898befa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61\\text{ km/h}" :db/id 17592186401943} {:answer/id #uuid "95aa0b04-7251-5fbf-b2eb-66bafc65e559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65.5\\text{ km/h}" :db/id 17592186401944} {:answer/id #uuid "336d2d1d-872d-56d4-a5f4-5bec78ef5250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\text{ km/h}" :db/id 17592186401945} {:answer/id #uuid "35306f0e-0e84-5e47-a2f4-679a063c0acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\text{ km/h}" :db/id 17592186401946} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f8d307-81e4-50be-9385-c254d6f22bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75.2\\text{ km/h}" :db/id 17592186401947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35306f0e-0e84-5e47-a2f4-679a063c0acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\text{ km/h}" :db/id 17592186401946} :answer-field/id #uuid "6827bd64-2698-5152-a010-779141f5c62e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "507fbe2b-2f95-5bfa-be32-3403810daadf" :question/math-academy-id "q-2939" :question/problem "![](images/92/9283bcc77ac8e63c1ba00f9a81e659bcbcf41bc98b94a1a0617b02feab71cf0a.png)\n\nCar $B$ is being driven due south toward Caltech at a speed of $60\\,\\text{km/h},$ while car $A$ is being driven due east away from Caltech. When car $B$ is $0.6\\,\\text{km}$ away from Caltech, car $A$ is $0.8\\,\\text{km}$ from Caltech. If the distance between the two vehicles is increasing at a rate of $20\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is the speed of car $A?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/6e/6e7ef0d4665710a31c1c601048498f20ad6b3099769c1d140320074805964ec7.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Caltech and car $A$,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Caltech and car $B$,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two cars.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=0.8\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=0.6\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {0.8}^{2}+{0.6}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =1\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-60\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=20\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0.8)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(0.6)(-60) & =2(1)(20) \\\\ 1.6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =40+72 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{112}{1.6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car $A$ is $70\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186064711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e61c15b-79c7-5166-b178-3747e17866dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id 17592186401949} {:answer/id #uuid "b14cf7b0-2299-5e5f-b5c6-d5c85c148877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,250\\text{km/h}" :db/id 17592186401950} {:answer/id #uuid "b8011803-44bb-5708-a342-7bae100244be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,350\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id 17592186401951} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f10350-a4ef-5fe3-aef8-799220547f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "975\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id 17592186401952} {:answer/id #uuid "062254bd-0f6e-5f77-8e3b-5a1990d9eb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "750\\,\\text{m/h}" :db/id 17592186401953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b14cf7b0-2299-5e5f-b5c6-d5c85c148877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,250\\text{km/h}" :db/id 17592186401950} :answer-field/id #uuid "81478755-4be2-5ae2-9535-e8dd0fd9f860" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "216f383a-6fa0-5f0b-b2c6-22682df9e591" :question/math-academy-id "q-2941" :question/problem "A rocket that is launched vertically is tracked by a radar station located on\nthe ground $3\\,\\text{km}$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the rocket at the instant when its distance from the radar station is $5\\,\\text{km}$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $1\\,000\\,\\text{km/h}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/7a/7af033f137fd76e5e3003926a598e695f4e0852b11dc27dfa84d58040144972b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the radar station and the launch point,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the launch point and the rocket, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the radar station and the rocket.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $y$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,000\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the radar station and the launch point ($x$) remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)(0)+2(4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(5)(1\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{10\\,000}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,250\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical speed of the rocket is $1\\,250\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b53db17c-c360-5669-8f88-e93f4bdbd8fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186401956} {:answer/id #uuid "25dcd0ff-82e2-5596-97c0-808147eeda5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186401957} {:answer/id #uuid "c06a013e-9247-5fe8-9462-4797376bce04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186401958} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5c203d-08c8-5beb-ba1e-76f154f20aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186401959} {:answer/id #uuid "12121f61-eccf-5393-b245-c6404140579b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186401960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25dcd0ff-82e2-5596-97c0-808147eeda5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186401957} :answer-field/id #uuid "8895f00a-eea4-5d81-b69d-a26ada8cb409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c11092b-9e53-51e1-b299-c5287bf677e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-29477" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3$\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions."}] [{:db/id 17592186069223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d78ba1c-5297-5540-afdc-d419d33e0cf7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76d7ebef-b52d-5f30-ac74-d49866c8f6a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id 17592186401962} {:answer/id #uuid "d00f8b18-cb37-5c31-961a-3438330ad683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6" :db/id 17592186401963} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0444d5-a369-5e49-89bd-c5ebc8c0237a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-11\\,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id 17592186401964} {:answer/id #uuid "44f7e400-b932-5f1e-a0ab-bdb9a22e3846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4" :db/id 17592186401965} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8857d6-881c-5549-bde0-3f1a4f61d2d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=9\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id 17592186401966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76d7ebef-b52d-5f30-ac74-d49866c8f6a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id 17592186401962} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5fa6494-6274-595a-88b0-e033638de4a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c02e0e4e-c511-5b9e-9632-6db46dfe1bf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-30052" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-4x+4=9$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2})-4 & =5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-2,q=9,$ and $p+q=7.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2+3=5 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2-3=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=5.$ Thus, the sum is $4.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d78ba1c-5297-5540-afdc-d419d33e0cf7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f24ab63d-b4d8-50ae-8389-5045b51319bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=0\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id 17592186401969} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1507f7-b1b3-56e3-a38c-1b563d660137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1$" :db/id 17592186401970} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d7f47d-c746-5735-9e9f-835593e46fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id 17592186401971} {:answer/id #uuid "aa922213-ad7d-513f-ad50-36362f5e8cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id 17592186401972} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d50863-24e6-52af-928c-18cf82d1862f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1$" :db/id 17592186401973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d7f47d-c746-5735-9e9f-835593e46fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id 17592186401971} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f724395-4532-5650-9bbb-1b5a58b225fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85741874-6828-5db4-a1ab-e8bb9b524df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-30055" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-2x+1=1$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,pq\\,,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot x\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=-1$ and $q=1.$ Thus, $pq=-1.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\ |x-1| & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =1\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-1=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186097224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a3b2c4a-a63b-5807-aacc-5245a5e6b02f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=4$" :db/id 17592186401975} {:answer/id #uuid "46b9e8a3-14f5-5f69-831f-e7e0d797a1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id 17592186401976} {:answer/id #uuid "c082a9b0-d023-52a6-be52-56d8828805a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$" :db/id 17592186401977} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3248ac-1443-5057-98d3-f07c503f35c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id 17592186401978} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0e922d-f9cb-5465-99c6-9d8aecf68d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$" :db/id 17592186401979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46b9e8a3-14f5-5f69-831f-e7e0d797a1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id 17592186401976} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ff50305-88e5-5409-b5ae-8fb7fea82cf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05af1cea-c88c-5fcc-8838-0055bd7f29aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-31213" :question/problem "![](images/19/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=0$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f18ae8c-d034-5c1c-af41-b32f52e9728d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186401982} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2ac919-d0f0-55be-b66b-077be2492a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$" :db/id 17592186401983} {:answer/id #uuid "b6faa5c0-12ea-54ed-86d4-4c1d75cbd567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186401984} {:answer/id #uuid "96d32a85-ec32-5353-9868-3cb1ac090a40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id 17592186401985} {:answer/id #uuid "a41d1397-7de6-584c-b009-e1eca0c2916b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6,\\,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186401986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f18ae8c-d034-5c1c-af41-b32f52e9728d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=2$" :db/id 17592186401982} :answer-field/id #uuid "2def65a7-d460-55e9-9537-f22df65257ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98bca65a-5215-5c3c-9419-26f83fa4001a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31219" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d776a61d1039639d91e2c667c68965d3e2c901cdc926e8c54e6356cf1d87c2e.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$ \n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "491969ed-8b76-5f12-8e92-e2c07f40a5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186401989} {:answer/id #uuid "47e753ff-b001-5c13-a3e5-fa0b1469d565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186401990} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0a1d9a-26c8-5f3e-8675-24c9ff948a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186401991} {:answer/id #uuid "345a93b2-c8e2-542e-9732-fd3c08c01fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186401992} {:answer/id #uuid "80637310-e8ef-5a17-b8ea-c4cf7c2aacb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186401993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47e753ff-b001-5c13-a3e5-fa0b1469d565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186401990} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08b9f41-8719-5f37-ae27-999f6560f9f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ac0783b-5b73-5c8c-959b-6029e5d56c68" :question/math-academy-id "q-31872" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $J$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $J$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $J$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[J\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${J}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{J}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{6} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186401994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26bd5bb7-0a8a-5edb-975d-4fd5f046599e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186401996} {:answer/id #uuid "08992256-6bf5-5ba6-a198-d2f970ef2ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186401997} {:answer/id #uuid "52bbf42f-38cf-5d5c-af4e-ee00925fa061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186401998} {:answer/id #uuid "61cc3875-ca37-5eff-9f67-f6c05f654183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186401999} {:answer/id #uuid "5af54dbb-f852-501b-bb4f-1e25a922f13c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186402000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08992256-6bf5-5ba6-a198-d2f970ef2ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186401997} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3cd4133-bfb2-57ca-9d13-24b0af1c763d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "561c404c-366e-5e58-b3aa-1094d48ff6e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-31919" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $det(A)\\ne 0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, then $A$ is $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nI. invertible\nII. **not ** invertible\nIII. singular" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($det(A)\\ne 0$), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option I gives a true statement:\n\n- *If $det(A)\\ne 0$ , then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4eea07e-f2f3-5991-9f8c-d1a3d485cb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186402003} {:answer/id #uuid "b42745fe-be44-51aa-b6af-8f31a15a57a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186402004} {:answer/id #uuid "428db092-3f21-5636-9fa7-75f2fd6123d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186402005} {:answer/id #uuid "8f69e7f4-02bf-5a3c-99ee-ec0db4ec6abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186402006} {:answer/id #uuid "3e91bc4e-4104-5e61-9a68-1d8607d11d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186402007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e91bc4e-4104-5e61-9a68-1d8607d11d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id 17592186402007} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2cc2c12-ff60-5e41-a718-fde30b82b390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8e64088-2308-5369-8285-5d893b0dfcdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-31945" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $K$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $K$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $K$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${K}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4683baa-7e31-565a-90dd-81bc97617456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402010} {:answer/id #uuid "e66e39bf-af21-54ee-9c01-1e665e79afcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402011} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb3bda1-f364-5e06-b3f9-1ec1294cdafe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402012} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae71a29-b56a-5e44-b3f7-481f0faef4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402013} {:answer/id #uuid "e81114b5-b5d4-5ed1-9aad-cfda69edeb8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4683baa-7e31-565a-90dd-81bc97617456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402010} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce793d72-97c8-5680-9b23-e9cde7d661bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fb0889d-6fed-5243-aaf7-929089854f2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-31954" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $D$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${D}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $D$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $D$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${D}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3ccae38-2121-52d3-adbe-774101358f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id 17592186402017} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1763ae-5fcd-5d4b-b18a-af3a643749d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id 17592186402018} {:answer/id #uuid "300bc792-b634-5ba3-8561-858fdf7d8d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-8{x}_{3} \\\\ 6-6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id 17592186402019} {:answer/id #uuid "55911223-4948-5180-95bf-96b38f95603a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-6{x}_{3} \\\\ 10+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id 17592186402020} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a39045-c764-56cd-a094-6caa0b024ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id 17592186402021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3ccae38-2121-52d3-adbe-774101358f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id 17592186402017} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d6271a-24bd-58c0-9553-83b2dc10742e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f65d67ed-b94b-50cc-87d4-9aa7130f7547" :question/math-academy-id "q-32247" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+10{x}_{3}=-8 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}=6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8d3a4ec-7531-5a58-8454-d19869fb21eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Continuity of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f63e48-7661-5659-ba85-f776e6101ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only" :db/id 17592186402024} {:answer/id #uuid "c5afdbb2-d8b4-53b7-a14e-dc8bce9f0e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/id 17592186402025} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd1d4d3-2d1e-5b75-ba7e-8c3f063e1ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the functions are continuous at $t=0$" :db/id 17592186402026} {:answer/id #uuid "5a43738f-363a-5de1-9bad-40504d08726c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/id 17592186402027} {:answer/id #uuid "1dedb7fb-0e93-57f7-a7d5-b9044d3f5144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(t)$ only" :db/id 17592186402028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbd1d4d3-2d1e-5b75-ba7e-8c3f063e1ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the functions are continuous at $t=0$" :db/id 17592186402026} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2d80644-598d-52f6-81f1-3f59a94eb57e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95526ab5-adbc-57eb-9fe3-cf21f5b51d6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-32373" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous at $t=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,7t+6,\\,3-{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨1,\\,{t}^{2/3},\\,t⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt[3]{t},\\,2t+3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous at a point if and only if each of its components is continuous at that point. \n\nLet's consider the functions one-by-one. \n\n- The function $f(t)$ is continuous at $t=0$ because each of its components ${t}^{2}$, $7t+6$ and $3-{t}^{3}$are continuous at $t=0.$\n\n- The function $g(t)$ is continuous at $t=0$ because each of its components $1$,  ${t}^{2/3}$ and $t$  are continuous at $t=0.$\n\n- The function $h(t)$ is continuous at $t=0$ because each of its components ${t}^{2}$, $\\sqrt[3]{t}$ and $2t+3$ are continuous at $t=0.$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ All the functions are continuous at $t=0$"}] [{:db/id 17592186129586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cfc838b-2e21-5021-8afc-000d12acdf39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a872eb-6635-5701-9ed5-8eb0a33709e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402030} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb3d6c2-db2b-5faa-b000-9d6b331c8070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402031} {:answer/id #uuid "abb39b52-dd18-5df8-a5af-f84fac501c06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402032} {:answer/id #uuid "55dfb5d2-b647-54c5-af77-52b2a5d5ea07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402033} {:answer/id #uuid "dfba25c3-fa45-56a2-a736-7025ac326b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55dfb5d2-b647-54c5-af77-52b2a5d5ea07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402033} :answer-field/id #uuid "f95e5646-83c9-5ea4-84fa-6be151721bec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b78e718-c7bd-590c-b65a-fabb1ffe4981" :question/math-academy-id "q-324453" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186129587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a962d5e-5cc9-530e-9f95-85c399af7e4b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Decoupling Substitution of a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a797ac7-33c4-512f-90f8-59eb16783f56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-2{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=3{u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186402036} {:answer/id #uuid "54d062ef-f5a7-554f-9aec-32b3e4d45203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=-{u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186402037} {:answer/id #uuid "a9817443-5f2f-5697-b9b4-facc30ec893f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186402038} {:answer/id #uuid "bb06d79b-0719-5c04-a4f5-69705749bb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)=2{u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=2{u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186402039} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f2d3e6-cd5c-5ab7-a220-8d6563dfe260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)+{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186402040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9817443-5f2f-5697-b9b4-facc30ec893f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186402038} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c339bd-cf81-5ede-a22f-a63cc71054fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c347aa2-fe8e-5437-94e5-a44fd44640d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-324455" :question/problem "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d476e061-e62e-516d-a210-085f525da0f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402043} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6e6397-9397-52a6-ae26-dbbab796036e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402044} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9ea285-612d-5a27-90ce-9aac6b9d11bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402045} {:answer/id #uuid "683b97cc-4e24-5dae-871f-704c3c57f91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402046} {:answer/id #uuid "936ed705-fd9f-5e57-9360-5bc6b86acdee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd6e6397-9397-52a6-ae26-dbbab796036e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402044} :answer-field/id #uuid "10f01866-5785-5c51-bd4f-76009cfa054f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df166e56-b183-5016-9f58-5d60ce55786e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32546" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186115776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b06f36c-5690-5392-a40a-f670e7419923" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Differentiability of a Vector Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ba69f7-a434-5ff4-969c-fa8fa69d9be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only" :db/id 17592186402049} {:answer/id #uuid "3264961f-a437-5289-bec4-efa2d5756979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/id 17592186402050} {:answer/id #uuid "f87b02d9-9e85-582c-84c5-09942dae9dd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of them are differentiable on $(0,\\pi )$" :db/id 17592186402051} {:answer/id #uuid "fae54900-ccf7-5a50-91e3-eb3eccd28c4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All of them are differentiable on $(0,\\pi )$" :db/id 17592186402052} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0a0f63-eca8-57ec-a462-c2093a71fe9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/id 17592186402053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15ba69f7-a434-5ff4-969c-fa8fa69d9be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only" :db/id 17592186402049} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d70eb46-6632-54ee-b92a-5875a0ef49c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11a6774d-821b-5df3-83c4-601fbb258758" :question/math-academy-id "q-32568" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (0,\\pi )?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨1+{t}^{2},\\,-t+2,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all $t\\in (0,\\pi ).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $(0,\\pi )$ if and only if each of \n its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (0,\\pi )$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$  to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all $(0,\\pi )$, we have that   $g(t)$ is also differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second component of this vector, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$, is undefined at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$,  we conclude that the function $h(t)$ is not differentiable on $(0,\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only"}] [{:db/id 17592186120196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7abb7a-38b8-51e1-aca2-0d2b5391e4f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402055} {:answer/id #uuid "1602c8f1-24ac-5bc4-886f-c23bd7016636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402056} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc39e06-17c8-5b75-b657-4b4e1555cf14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402057} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f664b1-8db7-5e54-b116-e1469468f127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402058} {:answer/id #uuid "a602201c-06fb-5598-8925-dcc674ce2b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a602201c-06fb-5598-8925-dcc674ce2b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402059} :answer-field/id #uuid "b453da57-6eef-5d7a-9c79-162c447ab3b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10430503-ea48-51a6-b4f8-6c1fb5c683b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-32569" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eab33316-9a2a-5e12-b51c-b02a826d1b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402062} {:answer/id #uuid "1bad7312-2e66-5700-9aae-eb7c85b9c383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186402063} {:answer/id #uuid "7f572362-82aa-5062-a6d6-241eb9bbaaef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 18 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402064} {:answer/id #uuid "a48759ab-5bd7-525d-8ee8-1aa323ee45c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402065} {:answer/id #uuid "55aa56c5-1a06-51f3-9787-5f99d9d8585f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55aa56c5-1a06-51f3-9787-5f99d9d8585f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402066} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f4ecb30-087b-5664-9448-53b7642eb521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7e3bf80-d71d-502c-8977-ed540b710cc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-32608" :question/problem "Given that  $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot D \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186120212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c159d750-ab1f-538b-b8cc-ae66ef1ae414" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Numbers in the Matrix U of an LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24743710-ccae-5ce6-b9bd-ec3e3a56e15a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,-2,\\,3" :db/id 17592186402068} {:answer/id #uuid "d50d3f79-94d2-5665-9a7d-33432a11e564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,-5,\\,3" :db/id 17592186402069} {:answer/id #uuid "aa28f05d-2582-5b23-a7d7-5ac2da1040ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,-5,\\,1" :db/id 17592186402070} {:answer/id #uuid "7167e25c-1100-59e0-8e73-47c13073ebe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,-5,\\,4" :db/id 17592186402071} {:answer/id #uuid "4746c90f-6d0d-57b3-b8bf-7604a4ec6128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,-2,\\,4" :db/id 17592186402072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7167e25c-1100-59e0-8e73-47c13073ebe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,-5,\\,4" :db/id 17592186402071} :answer-field/id #uuid "a81fb830-b280-555b-983a-34f12dd2e101" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce5c7307-c449-5fe7-8928-1317176c796c" :question/math-academy-id "q-32772" :question/problem "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. From left to right, what are the missing elements from the second row of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{21}$ of the matrix $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the elements from the second row of $U$ are $0,-5,$ and $4.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cfc838b-2e21-5021-8afc-000d12acdf39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa35b3d1-b162-5941-885d-8db182d4c937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402075} {:answer/id #uuid "47bb5c03-56ca-5b3a-9ba9-d6c4be7af812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402076} {:answer/id #uuid "72f7434b-6af7-53ee-bdbc-e38c8f39837c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402077} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0e91d3-d561-5c05-80db-683f07d1c4f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402078} {:answer/id #uuid "93e87a9b-599c-5a66-8fb7-bd8983873641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47bb5c03-56ca-5b3a-9ba9-d6c4be7af812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402076} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe2e4cc-de53-5a7a-8325-103564fb6a3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cca77f3c-b8b3-5463-80d6-aae528e0d55f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327976" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cfc838b-2e21-5021-8afc-000d12acdf39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d02b41e1-8e36-5518-81f1-e2f6825869f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402082} {:answer/id #uuid "cd48207e-6122-5485-be51-43956eb3e0ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402083} {:answer/id #uuid "5032c363-ccdd-5824-884a-40d99378e4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402084} {:answer/id #uuid "112f3fd7-e749-5dda-8050-204e1edb598f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402085} {:answer/id #uuid "88dceeef-1df2-5cb0-92dd-bbf3d61e6940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5032c363-ccdd-5824-884a-40d99378e4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id 17592186402084} :answer-field/id #uuid "3737eb4c-9c7f-5503-aca6-74894a835bb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94d96b96-98bb-5063-b4f0-15839c9585ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-327989" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186120222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "754f30f6-05c6-5575-a6bd-d8228629df28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,2)$ and $(8,e,2e)$" :db/id 17592186402088} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf74d58-2c01-59d6-a721-6e7f621cea0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,1)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2})$" :db/id 17592186402089} {:answer/id #uuid "31f3d5ba-15f4-5f33-b534-c97e0bf58653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2})$" :db/id 17592186402090} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbd3fdc-1447-52b4-83b7-2d0d7c8672c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2})$" :db/id 17592186402091} {:answer/id #uuid "78a43baf-f3b1-5839-b974-cb8da7856e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,1)$ and $(4,e,2e)$" :db/id 17592186402092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fbd3fdc-1447-52b4-83b7-2d0d7c8672c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2})$" :db/id 17592186402091} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c2ccd35-074d-50ff-bf17-a0b3922ccfb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e918f3b-d930-5ea5-9b21-8651bb576465" :question/math-academy-id "q-32918" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =c\\,⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =⟨4ct,\\,c{e}^{t},\\,2c{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}=4ct \\\\ {e}^{t}=c{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}=2c{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} & =1\\cdot 4t \\\\ {t}^{2} & =2t \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$ and $2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{2},\\,{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2(2)^{2},\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2}).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186120245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf29808-4551-56a0-ac86-a4587a06fc0f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Line and a Plane Given in Other Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d74ada3-9243-5f7c-ade2-62caa4b9c021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,1,-4)" :db/id 17592186402094} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddeb712-2499-5385-8cf5-8d5841c638be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-4,7)" :db/id 17592186402095} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a42789-8ed3-50da-bd89-5ed82656d5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,1,1)" :db/id 17592186402096} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5b4306-4e52-5f8f-a6c5-d9a8c474f65b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id 17592186402097} {:answer/id #uuid "b73612ac-9159-5b03-82b8-04dc3dbeb325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-4,1)" :db/id 17592186402098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73612ac-9159-5b03-82b8-04dc3dbeb325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-4,1)" :db/id 17592186402098} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ba93ced-d091-5536-afc3-ce47413fdf3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49e4fa98-401d-5c2d-a49c-1997a8550033" :question/math-academy-id "q-33203" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨-5,0,1⟩+t⟨-3,1,0⟩,\\,Π:-3x+y-2z+27=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨-5-3t,t,1⟩.\n$$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+y-2z+27 & =0 \\\\ -3x+y-2z & =-27 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩ & =-27 \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩ & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨-5-3t,t,1⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩ & =-27 \\\\ ⟨-5-3t,t,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩ & =-27 \\\\ -3(-5-3t)+1\\cdot t-2\\cdot 1 & =-27 \\\\ 15+9t+t-2 & =-27 \\\\ 10t & =-40 \\\\ t & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=-4$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨-5-3(-4),-4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(7,-4,1).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf29808-4551-56a0-ac86-a4587a06fc0f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Line and a Plane Given in Other Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb5e385e-d48b-552d-92db-b2c42b730f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,8,14)" :db/id 17592186402101} {:answer/id #uuid "2cec0929-bdd8-5d7b-8ece-52cdde4c6f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-2,6)" :db/id 17592186402102} {:answer/id #uuid "c23c18d5-4a2a-5884-8422-592e07e7f5b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id 17592186402103} {:answer/id #uuid "50770341-c200-5dd2-a57c-f88a32fa60ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4,8)" :db/id 17592186402104} {:answer/id #uuid "b695496a-1b9b-5846-a379-f0e3c0d96851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(14,8,3)" :db/id 17592186402105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb5e385e-d48b-552d-92db-b2c42b730f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,8,14)" :db/id 17592186402101} :answer-field/id #uuid "0878a66a-d92b-5043-a146-6c379d55810e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaab1f60-24fb-5572-8cfb-474f418bf09f" :question/math-academy-id "q-33205" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨-1,-2,6⟩+t⟨2,5,4⟩,\\,Π:x+2y+z-33=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨-1+2t,-2+5t,6+4t⟩.\n$$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z-33 & =0 \\\\ x+2y+z & =33 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ & =33 \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨-1+2t,-2+5t,6+4t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ & =33 \\\\ ⟨-1+2t,-2+5t,6+4t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ & =33 \\\\ -1+2t+2(-2+5t)+6+4t & =33 \\\\ -1+2t-4+10t+6+4t & =33 \\\\ 16t & =32 \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨-1+2(2),-2+5(2),6+4(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,8,14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(3,8,14).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbeddd1b-c3dd-54cd-a00c-bfa7f8bb9029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $9$ horizontally." :db/id 17592186402108} {:answer/id #uuid "20dad633-ddf9-5439-b4ea-d46bf03e30dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $9$ units to the right." :db/id 17592186402109} {:answer/id #uuid "502d4257-08f1-572c-b4f7-44ca7819cd6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $9$ units to the left." :db/id 17592186402110} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2964df-d6ec-5d47-adc1-edddefca6e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id 17592186402111} {:answer/id #uuid "e92b73b6-6083-5681-b869-7b2bec74f5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $9$ vertically." :db/id 17592186402112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "502d4257-08f1-572c-b4f7-44ca7819cd6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $9$ units to the left." :db/id 17592186402110} :answer-field/id #uuid "8decbbab-7268-5304-9585-788574a6793b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8dd5186-15e7-5cde-a17c-1b124d1cef2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332064" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{-9t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{-9t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $9$ units to the left."}] [{:db/id 17592186221966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a983ec-1fbc-5d7a-a42b-c402efab9bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402114} {:answer/id #uuid "3c101c73-2373-5f19-95b7-38cf875588f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402115} {:answer/id #uuid "2db689f9-cf9d-528f-953d-bb9eb442f63a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186402116} {:answer/id #uuid "c464b411-fc87-5f11-bfa9-a3c55b9869f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402117} {:answer/id #uuid "54a49d44-5d72-5536-8b82-96f937946071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186402118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92a983ec-1fbc-5d7a-a42b-c402efab9bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402114} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff1d45ca-54f7-5849-8957-95bea1964db0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eda104b-fb43-5560-b213-0b2f03bde928" :question/math-academy-id "q-333360" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ 2x-y-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3y-3=0\\,⇒\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(-1)+1=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5bc609b-dc5b-52e9-9f0e-206f32bb829b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Homogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa2879fd-b388-54d3-91a6-9889b3cc921c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id 17592186402121} {:answer/id #uuid "7f291e69-4dda-53a6-8a62-f0061587ecc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id 17592186402122} {:answer/id #uuid "8c43d172-267c-5edc-a00c-eb37c3afc069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id 17592186402123} {:answer/id #uuid "6160111a-8bd8-53b3-8c8a-0ec9830b724b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id 17592186402124} {:answer/id #uuid "a82130d9-20ec-5094-9c98-83b539f3238e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id 17592186402125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c43d172-267c-5edc-a00c-eb37c3afc069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id 17592186402123} :answer-field/id #uuid "d370b658-9d56-5718-913a-a06cb231e944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c065292-5916-5335-a2ce-48232ddb8e4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333856" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186229675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5bc609b-dc5b-52e9-9f0e-206f32bb829b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Homogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be54f80-3e00-5c60-8d2c-859b73f1b04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}" :db/id 17592186402127} {:answer/id #uuid "a489112a-2e25-5139-8701-0da71be4eaed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}" :db/id 17592186402128} {:answer/id #uuid "f40839a9-1ee4-555c-b0e0-836cd8ef8a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402129} {:answer/id #uuid "d13024ed-e948-5192-b456-f8fc65a79957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402130} {:answer/id #uuid "2d239718-30d6-534b-aa0f-8cec27c7ffe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d239718-30d6-534b-aa0f-8cec27c7ffe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186402131} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbee732a-a84c-5f81-aebe-d7513334919d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "783e954f-0aef-53d9-a6ee-3a8685affb71" :question/math-academy-id "q-334772" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb88ef39-e68a-5400-8035-7c504e8a0052" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Falling Body Model With Quadratic Drag"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32063b22-d699-5f63-a71d-869c73971f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=2g-kv" :db/id 17592186402134} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc50789-5421-50d2-b60e-67a3bd2e10f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=2g" :db/id 17592186402135} {:answer/id #uuid "491ac020-a720-59c1-ba10-c392f69847b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=2g-k{v}^{2}" :db/id 17592186402136} {:answer/id #uuid "4948e46f-d047-5768-bf2d-e94a4743506f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}{v}^{2}" :db/id 17592186402137} {:answer/id #uuid "d34ed5ad-0c4e-5b97-a8e7-e19f41dfe4f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}v" :db/id 17592186402138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4948e46f-d047-5768-bf2d-e94a4743506f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}{v}^{2}" :db/id 17592186402137} :answer-field/id #uuid "8915c372-42a6-5420-8c91-5f069925b152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28461e48-9c29-58e8-b99d-2d64a173813c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335312" :question/problem "A rock of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the rock's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the rock is proportional to the square of the rock's velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, the acceleration due to gravity is $g,$ and the only forces acting on the rock are the weight due to gravity and drag." :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the rock:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the rock, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the rock (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since drag, in $\\text{N}=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and squared velocity, in $(\\text{m/s})^{2}={\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\text{kg/m}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the rock is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=2g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the rock, we get\n\n$$\n2g-k{v}^{2}=2\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}{v}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ days" :db/id 17592186402141} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe81525-8780-55b2-9e79-394a40fd9a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$50$ days" :db/id 17592186402142} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9dd8e3-5764-58b2-9ff2-4b88c32898f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$40$ days" :db/id 17592186402143} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5c6a61-12a4-5352-8fcc-e3319bbc18bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ days" :db/id 17592186402144} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb667f2-6fca-5729-a7fd-9dfe18862602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$28$ days" :db/id 17592186402145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ days" :db/id 17592186402141} :answer-field/id #uuid "63531a29-5d3e-5f5f-97ce-ef01b7d03202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4fd0915-73cc-575a-b3cc-4a156a96dd49" :question/math-academy-id "q-335542" :question/problem "A wildlife reserve can support a total of $800$ deer.\nAt the beginning of a disease study in the reserve, a parasitic infection has affected $80$ deer. After $10$ days, the number of deer affected by the parasite increases to $300.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the infected deer population to reach $600$ animals?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of deer affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the deer population affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total deer population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total deer population in the reserve, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial deer population affected by the parasite was $80$ animals. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=80,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =80 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{0} & =80 \\\\ 800-K & =80 \\\\ K & =720\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-720{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the deer population affected by the parasite was $300$ animals. So, applying the condition $P(10)=300,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =300 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-10k} & =300 \\\\ 500 & =720{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{500}{720}=\\frac{25}{36} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $600$ deer are affected by the parasite. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =600 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =600 \\\\ 200 & =720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{200}{720}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $600$ deer will be affected by the parasite after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})}=35.128…≈35\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id 17592186402146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a62be6e9-5300-5837-bb21-616f9afabd11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$570$ loaves" :db/id 17592186402148} {:answer/id #uuid "52f73195-6ad4-5063-9185-1d0ce506d0fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$560$ loaves" :db/id 17592186402149} {:answer/id #uuid "487b0c65-385a-5896-a74a-7185c8309375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$577$ loaves" :db/id 17592186402150} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb7ad78-9e83-5378-a008-fb209e8c0c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$550$ loaves" :db/id 17592186402151} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3362f7-e0ab-59c9-813b-230e3fd73524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$590$ loaves" :db/id 17592186402152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "487b0c65-385a-5896-a74a-7185c8309375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$577$ loaves" :db/id 17592186402150} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e99660a-207e-56cc-af0c-7810e8c04e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee0a87d3-d3e9-539a-9074-68a374d85d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335590" :question/problem "A small bakery produces loaves of bread. The owner knows that the bakery can produce a maximum of $600$ loaves a day; however, current production is below this maximum. Since she started monitoring, the bakers produced $480$ loaves and $500$ loaves on days $4$ and $8,$ respectively. How many loaves do they produce on day $40?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the bread production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the bread production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible bread production and its current bread production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the bakery, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ loaves, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(600-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{600-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{600-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $4,$ the bread production was $480$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(4)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(4) & =480 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $8,$ the bread production was $500$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(8)=500,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(8) & =500 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-8k} & =500 \\\\ K{e}^{-8k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-8k}} & =\\frac{120}{100} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} & =120 \\\\ K & =120{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} \\\\ K & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=600-144{e}^{-(t/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of loaves baked on day $t=40$ is\n\n$$\nE(40)=600-144{e}^{-(40/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}≈576.743…≈577\\,\\text{loaves},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id 17592186402153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67f7ba4e-e115-562d-8f0b-e2fb3c50999d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ day" :db/id 17592186402155} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fa0d9-3d48-5ce5-bfa8-99fbc7aafb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id 17592186402156} {:answer/id #uuid "3c36fd19-5696-5a1e-9dc9-b04f43bf5c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12\\text{th}$ day" :db/id 17592186402157} {:answer/id #uuid "31324eeb-37b1-5843-9dfa-024be7517c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20\\text{th}$ day" :db/id 17592186402158} {:answer/id #uuid "11e8a292-e74d-5f79-93dd-d159881ed802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10\\text{th}$ day" :db/id 17592186402159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fa0d9-3d48-5ce5-bfa8-99fbc7aafb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id 17592186402156} :answer-field/id #uuid "450a3a23-c54b-5ed0-90a2-deef088dea63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44cded13-6bc7-52ea-8fcc-d6fe583c3d07" :question/math-academy-id "q-335619" :question/problem "A national park is monitoring the number of people who have downloaded a new park app. The system can support a maximum of $800$ users, but the total is still below this limit. Since the park began tracking downloads, the number of users reached $620$ on day $3$ and $720$ on day $9.$ On what day will the number of users reach $760?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of users, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of app users $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of users the system can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ users, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the number of users was $620.$ So, applying the condition $P(3)=620,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =620 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-3k} & =620 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the number of users was $720.$ So, applying the condition $P(9)=720,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =720 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-9k} & =720 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{180}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} & =180 \\\\ K & =180{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} \\\\ K & =180\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ K & =180\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-270{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$  be the day when the app reaches $760$ users. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =760 \\\\ 800-270{e}^{-kd} & =760 \\\\ 40 & =270{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{40}{270} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{4}{27} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the number of users will be $760$ on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})}=14.1285…≈14,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id 17592186402160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c9870a1-f792-53e1-9097-5a43d6035fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402162} {:answer/id #uuid "a89a0be5-8768-5590-83e4-bcbf4ea9fb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402163} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bb60e5-5192-5666-b667-43311399f89e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402164} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba59cf7-16b9-58fc-997b-8944f2b75bc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186402165} {:answer/id #uuid "56b2f656-8a92-58eb-ab9c-786f826b15f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186402166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c9870a1-f792-53e1-9097-5a43d6035fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402162} :answer-field/id #uuid "151341a6-4b50-5d9d-ac35-c33852fb4d04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb0dc9ee-4198-5fbc-84d6-225dac676eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-335639" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 3x-y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-7y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85c43a78-188e-5ac6-8e6c-a2db1d4000a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402169} {:answer/id #uuid "239ae03e-f563-561f-b322-55220d576cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402170} {:answer/id #uuid "3af6e3d8-10db-531f-982e-4b5b3d01053f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402171} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8ac901-6746-5ade-a6a7-36a65ab2324b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186402172} {:answer/id #uuid "b232e07f-b421-55de-842f-8f073fd45fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186402173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "239ae03e-f563-561f-b322-55220d576cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id 17592186402170} :answer-field/id #uuid "02478602-b3c1-5c9d-81bf-ab4c5f0d8364" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53ed55d4-8280-5930-99c5-60558f8b8c67" :question/math-academy-id "q-335642" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=0 \\\\ 2x+5y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-6y+5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b87aeba5-eb67-5dc0-9c25-d9c1cefdb6e2" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Modified Lotka-Volterra Model: Predator Extinction Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfea3f1f-0f04-5f62-9442-10012cf69aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186402176} {:answer/id #uuid "aa401dab-3f4c-5cd0-89a0-8b6651eab85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186402177} {:answer/id #uuid "9a325d2f-f957-525b-b0fe-8fe7914320a5" :answer/type {:db/ident