d 17592186402209} {:answer/id #uuid "f86c81e4-5ffc-590a-bee6-c31a2b4d7942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186402210} {:answer/id #uuid "26b4b7f1-4d1e-5d04-a085-a90adb893d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186402211} {:answer/id #uuid "88cb0c2c-4eb3-51aa-922a-e0d82d33d145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186402212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f86c81e4-5ffc-590a-bee6-c31a2b4d7942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186402210} :answer-field/id #uuid "b25976c6-d6cc-526b-8713-ff3cdf0d728b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186402207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbc98d60-8975-59af-8c09-070d79da0e68" :question/math-academy-id "q-335673" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n$$J(x^{\\ast})=\\begin{bmatrix}{{field-1}}&{{field-2}}\\\\{{field-3}}&{{field-4}}\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-y+xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-2x)-xy)=2-8x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+xy)=-1+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-8x-y & -x \\\\ y & -1+x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-8(0)-0 & -0 \\\\ 0 & -1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fd99f64-1a0e-5d84-8e13-95caae8d936d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186402215} {:answer/id #uuid "8b10290b-80cb-5b29-9f79-1f195229c212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186402216} {:answer/id #uuid "05e09efb-9144-55ca-b467-bbf73a07f532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186402217} {:answer/id #uuid "49d1e9f7-e09c-5fb0-9b7e-39e20b2c166e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186402218} {:answer/id #uuid "9983cf80-2f24-55d7-be17-9eb6b4c84e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186402219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9983cf80-2f24-55d7-be17-9eb6b4c84e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186402219} :answer-field/id #uuid "acf1e061-7a98-5801-95c8-22217b716f6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a42fad0a-c170-522e-8f97-0f501aa9bc73" :question/math-academy-id "q-338587" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\nI. $4{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,y(0)=1$\nII. ${y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x},\\,y(1)=0,\\,y(3)=2$\nIII. ${y}^{″}={x}^{3},\\,y(2)=5$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=3$).\n\n- The third system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition."}] [{:db/id 17592186402220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8c898fc-6802-5296-9866-c844f9ed0349" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Boundary Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51963deb-494b-5038-a567-d52bbae853d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Other" :db/id 17592186402222} {:answer/id #uuid "ca70af21-75b9-54e1-a733-f7fe2f7fa12e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id 17592186402223} {:answer/id #uuid "320aef74-4e39-5907-aac3-4ae1dafcfabd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id 17592186402224} {:answer/id #uuid "99d38800-e0a0-56b4-b188-2bf47e62f659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Periodic" :db/id 17592186402225} {:answer/id #uuid "b97382cb-a37a-52dd-8bc5-72cf2224232a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id 17592186402226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca70af21-75b9-54e1-a733-f7fe2f7fa12e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id 17592186402223} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd10be9b-3b17-5633-861a-6fa42ef0e0d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc494029-58f4-51d4-95fe-9c9b2384d2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-338623" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)-{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=0,\\,3y(3)+{y}^{′}(3)=6,\\,1\\le x\\le 3\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=1$ and $x=b=3.$\n\n$$\ny(1)=0,\\,3y(3)+{y}^{′}(3)=6\n$$\n\nThese are Robin (mixed) boundary conditions."}] [{:db/id 17592186402227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ea69de7-f96b-5949-9aec-584cb4e2f214" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Unknown Time Given an Exponential Growth or Decay Model With an Unknown Rate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "386007b3-91ce-5982-8534-d31233322fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.3$ weeks" :db/id 17592186402229} {:answer/id #uuid "0a01f341-43f3-528c-b811-0f8051a23c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ weeks" :db/id 17592186402230} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac92552-dc2b-5d29-986a-281a4e64330d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.4$ weeks" :db/id 17592186402231} {:answer/id #uuid "c1738319-0cbe-5aa8-beb9-61d8c99fe62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ weeks" :db/id 17592186402232} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2f0725-683a-50fc-855c-66c7d9ba756f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.2$ weeks" :db/id 17592186402233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "386007b3-91ce-5982-8534-d31233322fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.3$ weeks" :db/id 17592186402229} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ae4df5-40ba-5454-aa69-0f62becda821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b562af2b-8343-5000-806c-d01887adea11" :question/math-academy-id "q-339222" :question/problem "The number of subscribers $N(t)$ to an e-learning platform varies according to the differential equation ${N}^{′}(t)=rN(t),$ where $t$ is the time since the platform was launched, measured in weeks, and $r>0.$ It is known that the number of subscribers doubles after the first four weeks. How long does it take for the number of subscribers to triple after the platform is launched? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the number of subscribers when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of subscribers equals $3{N}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N({t}_{1}) & =3{N}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{N}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=3{N}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3{N}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{N}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3\\cancel{{N}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the number of subscribers doubles after the first four weeks the platform is launched. Therefore,\n\n$$\nN(4)=2{N}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{N}_{0}{e}^{4r}=2{N}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}_{0}{e}^{4r} & =2{N}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{N}_{0}}{e}^{4r} & =2\\cancel{{N}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{4r} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈6.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id 17592186402234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ea69de7-f96b-5949-9aec-584cb4e2f214" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Unknown Time Given an Exponential Growth or Decay Model With an Unknown Rate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10efe48b-a9f4-5aa8-8700-8513aef294db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.4$ years" :db/id 17592186402236} {:answer/id #uuid "9483f82e-0758-5787-8ce2-48f26c64a23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ years" :db/id 17592186402237} {:answer/id #uuid "85698021-f6eb-5674-af9e-26f88bf35ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.3$ years" :db/id 17592186402238} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0b804f-53f8-5360-9835-166b54051187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.1$ years" :db/id 17592186402239} {:answer/id #uuid "85d74bd3-72ef-5c17-a201-f97592fcc512" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ years" :db/id 17592186402240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85698021-f6eb-5674-af9e-26f88bf35ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.3$ years" :db/id 17592186402238} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa46205e-ab90-5810-a697-993aab9f4c54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8375c335-8cf2-5c82-b2c9-770890e7d65a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339226" :question/problem "The value $V(t)$ of a tech device varies according to the differential equation ${V}^{′}(t)=-rV(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in years, and $r>0.$ It is known that the value reduces by one-half after the first year. How long does it take for the value to reduce to one-fifth of its original value? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nV(t)={V}_{0}{e}^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${V}_{0}$ is the value when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the value equals $\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the value reduces by one-half after the first year. Therefore,\n\n$$\nV(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r}=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -r & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈2.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id 17592186402241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aeeaf85-0cbd-58ab-91d6-93448ff54b4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Initial Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98555ddd-6e37-50dd-95fe-cea932afb900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$31.13$" :db/id 17592186402243} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf3ab14-e63c-5a07-8f00-c6624545c11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$116.01$" :db/id 17592186402244} {:answer/id #uuid "e23ede75-277e-5685-b92b-dcc0d6291e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$122.03$" :db/id 17592186402245} {:answer/id #uuid "145dc5fb-065b-57b6-ab94-00b00d6949c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$113.25$" :db/id 17592186402246} {:answer/id #uuid "41ca3324-7989-5875-90e2-784c63f6f17b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$114.93$" :db/id 17592186402247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41ca3324-7989-5875-90e2-784c63f6f17b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$114.93$" :db/id 17592186402247} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6cbfe2e-3815-5b36-b83f-63a2f8eb905b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1d42a16-3984-5dae-bf8b-539545f414f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-339248" :question/problem "The rate of increase ${E}^{′}(t)$ of the average monthly cost of electricity in a particular region varies according to the differential equation ${E}^{′}(t)=0.02E(t),$ where $t$ is the time, in years, since the year $2017.$ In $2021,$ the average monthly cost was $$124.50.$ What was the average monthly cost of electricity in $2017?$" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nE(t)={E}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.02t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${E}_{0}$ is the average monthly cost of electricity in $2017.$\n\nWe are given that the monthly residential electricity cost was $$124.50$ in $2021,$ so after $t=4$ years. Hence, substituting $t=4$ and $E(4)=124.50$ into our general solution and solving for ${E}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(4) & ={E}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.02\\cdot 4} \\\\ 124.50 & ={E}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.08} \\\\ {E}_{0} & =\\frac{124.50}{{e}^{0.08}} \\\\  & ≈114.93\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest cent.\n\nTherefore, the average monthly cost in $2017$ was $$114.93.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aababf37-9c33-5d0f-9433-1d0cf555f97b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186402250} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa28dc5-5323-59c1-89c5-ec291f51cc7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186402251} {:answer/id #uuid "fe799d91-4567-55b5-9d6d-065742893e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186402252} {:answer/id #uuid "0560bd9f-1114-574e-9bb5-08d3e454f4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186402253} {:answer/id #uuid "f1484f26-148e-5676-bd10-764ffcb28ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186402254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fa28dc5-5323-59c1-89c5-ec291f51cc7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186402251} :answer-field/id #uuid "de781819-e735-5446-8359-21f70d676ce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c104c7c7-be1b-5f7c-9495-548d3d354240" :question/math-academy-id "q-339251" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nI. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,y(4)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=-1$\nII. $x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=9$\nIII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-8x{y}^{′}+16y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=3$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=4$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=2$)."}] [{:db/id 17592186402255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8892f2f5-e370-5028-8460-a0e49e8ec9cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186402257} {:answer/id #uuid "88eda162-b9e7-53c5-aaf0-8ab22fd5c91a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186402258} {:answer/id #uuid "42ef59eb-cad5-5519-b0d9-e972100a8fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186402259} {:answer/id #uuid "eddbd1d4-bb8e-5d05-a1f6-e513d4b1a8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186402260} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ad3aa9-ab9b-50bd-8028-70bf56ae664a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186402261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ef59eb-cad5-5519-b0d9-e972100a8fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186402259} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e74f1dd-50b5-55df-99cd-c5f3fc2522c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7b3292e-ae09-5cdf-bbb6-9d5f24a711b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-339256" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nI. $x{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+2y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1$\nII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=5x$\nIII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=2$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=4$). This is a **boundary value problem.**"}] [{:db/id 17592186402262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf29808-4551-56a0-ac86-a4587a06fc0f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Line and a Plane Given in Other Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee24fdf4-e1ee-5f88-bc78-1cd69107c3af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4,2)" :db/id 17592186402264} {:answer/id #uuid "3cff9213-8332-5106-9a38-96d7ec4a4378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9,5,3)" :db/id 17592186402265} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7ebdfb-49aa-56f4-a0ad-f4d48aa68b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,5,9)" :db/id 17592186402266} {:answer/id #uuid "6adfa3c5-c5cd-56cd-841d-1fd0fc016077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-1,6)" :db/id 17592186402267} {:answer/id #uuid "cd22fb61-25ce-5f17-bad1-fbd4ce2f0694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id 17592186402268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd22fb61-25ce-5f17-bad1-fbd4ce2f0694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id 17592186402268} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e05243-350c-5a1d-ad1e-5c71c37318cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbfd2820-3a30-53a2-832d-407032fe3cf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-33987" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨3,5,9⟩+t⟨4,-1,6⟩,\\,Π:-2x+4y+2z-16=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨3+4t,5-t,9+6t⟩.\n$$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+4y+2z-16 & =0 \\\\ -2x+4y+2z & =16 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,4,2⟩ & =16 \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,4,2⟩ & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨3+4t,5-t,9+6t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-2,4,2⟩ & =16 \\\\ ⟨3+4t,5-t,9+6t⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,4,2⟩ & =16 \\\\ -2(3+4t)+4(5-t)+2(9+6t) & =16 \\\\ -6-8t+20-4t+18+12t & =16 \\\\ 32 & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has no solution. Therefore, we conclude that the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ do not intersect."}] [{:db/id 17592186402269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e01d04-6c13-52eb-a4b6-ad9a5cde8134" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56be28be-e705-54a4-a778-9f6ec8ce939a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id 17592186402271} {:answer/id #uuid "6aed1c1d-e07b-56b5-8813-9bc524e3b177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}))" :db/id 17592186402272} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f5bf53-f4d6-5d6a-980c-1b00b877e968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id 17592186402273} {:answer/id #uuid "413d3322-907e-5aaf-a499-45064137e05b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}))" :db/id 17592186402274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6aed1c1d-e07b-56b5-8813-9bc524e3b177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}))" :db/id 17592186402272} :answer-field/id #uuid "6520f82c-2711-5a08-aef8-d05fa583c5ad" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186402270} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0840c9bd-29c8-51d1-97c9-4c4a62931e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10})" :db/id 17592186402276} {:answer/id #uuid "6426dfc7-965f-5694-8849-76b5475c6032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id 17592186402277} {:answer/id #uuid "af28147c-5f72-550f-b281-517b8bce6252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10})" :db/id 17592186402278} {:answer/id #uuid "08df2de5-a148-5277-89dc-2f4b6e34694a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id 17592186402279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08df2de5-a148-5277-89dc-2f4b6e34694a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})" :db/id 17592186402279} :answer-field/id #uuid "27113094-5e40-5dac-9419-bf117be37919" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186402275} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff1a95df-b525-52a3-a9f3-14f8763557db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id 17592186402281} {:answer/id #uuid "0965069c-ec53-552f-94e8-f41e248346e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id 17592186402282} {:answer/id #uuid "374b6057-dfd1-5f85-921b-54b469594c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id 17592186402283} {:answer/id #uuid "8f17fad2-853c-5665-b126-6ced9d2c13cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id 17592186402284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "374b6057-dfd1-5f85-921b-54b469594c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id 17592186402283} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a3e9d85-8afb-532c-bc98-02a869ca1ca9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186402280} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08cfe6b5-a35c-56e3-a8b6-b208ff4d131b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id 17592186402286} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6b6d9b-8a8b-5425-9396-e4c78607c4b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id 17592186402287} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f34d3a-cfa0-5e02-8320-e125baa30770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{50(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id 17592186402288} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7ac352-d46a-51ed-a992-d68e07a53b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{250(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id 17592186402289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a7ac352-d46a-51ed-a992-d68e07a53b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{250(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id 17592186402289} :answer-field/id #uuid "c69873a9-e21f-55cc-ba65-c7ed6f009b57" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186402285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de25ec60-135e-5b0f-93ec-2a89a92381de" :question/math-academy-id "q-343341" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $10$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=5x$ defined on $[-5,5).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $10$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n5x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-5,5).$ \nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}5t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ {[\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{5}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{5})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{250(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}))\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186402290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3cb7656-1ce6-5b6a-9e88-5ba24a943c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Infinite Sum Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8731841-813d-5c15-9844-bc300aded01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id 17592186402292} {:answer/id #uuid "7d76bc51-9148-5c68-8eea-0c61f6eca229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186402293} {:answer/id #uuid "4b83a754-c4bd-58d7-824f-3b576edd0dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186402294}