heta -3" :db/id 17592186404014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae0179db-bc3d-59ac-a7ab-f6e35d77cdda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id 17592186404011} :answer-field/id #uuid "6729ae77-9d58-5d6c-9396-7d1f8b6ef681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9082f86b-7c0f-5dd7-b074-5c0e7d067128" :question/math-academy-id "q-79287" :question/problem "![](images/07/07ec13485a828c8f5cd99ae2fbc3f7444135ba3c1cdf790762edb01b9726317e.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,2),$ $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0a50ab1-4ada-57e2-8605-70a502bab6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id 17592186404017} {:answer/id #uuid "32f648c8-3d4d-551c-a0fe-e98c1f6d2eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id 17592186404018} {:answer/id #uuid "89052cbe-3422-56ea-9b9a-942801acf80e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id 17592186404019} {:answer/id #uuid "8b13ccd7-add3-5ba4-bf95-5440c51f18c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id 17592186404020} {:answer/id #uuid "1c440c18-9297-5a46-a77e-a81bd6a4fec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id 17592186404021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0a50ab1-4ada-57e2-8605-70a502bab6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id 17592186404017} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9041eb-bfd0-524e-8602-d57198b3ea25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a6a1e67-ac65-5715-8684-1ea8d5d95e68" :question/math-academy-id "q-79292" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d45616d089e8f5d078ff48c949b26d0920074ab4b611ec71b491a0e18fd293c.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,1)$ with horizontal radius $1$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=1$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5102bd-f838-51a2-a43d-a2fd2a78f6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $3$" :db/id 17592186404024} {:answer/id #uuid "72c55eb1-bc22-5e56-b296-0190d78ec97b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $2$" :db/id 17592186404025} {:answer/id #uuid "46477b31-f16d-5120-a625-74e00bf51669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $3$" :db/id 17592186404026} {:answer/id #uuid "eb064b01-f667-53e4-8106-1aaf96595200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $2$" :db/id 17592186404027} {:answer/id #uuid "23bd81ec-466e-5b37-9878-ddbec03eee54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $4$" :db/id 17592186404028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46477b31-f16d-5120-a625-74e00bf51669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $3$" :db/id 17592186404026} :answer-field/id #uuid "6704063a-555e-57cc-88b9-c16e0fa9f2fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc9954e3-432a-5a64-87f7-a9d25a9f95d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-79301" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3bb8c00ab2e0ef3e7c1bc21726458e718e67897f28093321884bd1c67ca768f.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-4,-1)$ and passes through the point $(5,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-4,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}=4p(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,5),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4(0+4) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ y & =-5,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-5$ and $3.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967d9b2c-1fda-53bc-ae0d-e10db710fdf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k{T}^{3},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186404031} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ea423a-8ac0-584d-a9cf-f4c2ccea48eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt[3]{T},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186404032} {:answer/id #uuid "021d55c8-03a2-563f-ad3a-db919a1e2246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{3}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186404033} {:answer/id #uuid "e4736ffb-9caa-5548-a49e-35adb9ed2dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k{T}^{3},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186404034} {:answer/id #uuid "313b345a-3675-5490-b55d-49f946a9d2d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{\\sqrt[3]{T}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186404035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "021d55c8-03a2-563f-ad3a-db919a1e2246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{3}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id 17592186404033} :answer-field/id #uuid "54fb041f-00b7-53a7-b2c7-5721abae068d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e640b0e6-4f4b-5358-84ca-251a991cee48" :question/math-academy-id "q-79306" :question/problem "In a controlled environment, the temperature $T$ of a hot drink decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the cube of the temperature. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the temperature of the drink $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*In a controlled environment, the **temperature** $T$ of a hot drink **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **cube of the temperature** $T.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *temperature* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *cube of the temperature.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{T}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the temperature *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{3}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f97501c-2fa0-5b11-84b4-86faae530ce4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Combined Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72faae25-5e2c-5641-a31e-198f13b85833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s-1}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id 17592186404038} {:answer/id #uuid "60fd44b5-40fc-5e2e-ba3b-d25072a0023b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+2}{{s}^{2}+2}" :db/id 17592186404039} {:answer/id #uuid "d44ae010-dbbe-5f4d-b0e4-19ecdfbf0caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-3}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id 17592186404040} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d0ddbf-ea24-5c90-92e4-c9ae79a6aded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-1}{{s}^{2}+2}" :db/id 17592186404041} {:answer/id #uuid "c8338925-abcb-5816-8940-b12198bb3d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s+2}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id 17592186404042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8338925-abcb-5816-8940-b12198bb3d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s+2}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id 17592186404042} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd0d8007-7775-5b7a-a407-ec01a5ac1b20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9e7904b-a424-5faa-b622-0f16d31ff921" :question/math-academy-id "q-79438" :question/problem "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$ for $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}+L{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}+3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}+3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}+3\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3s+2}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3d1d5f5-b46e-5640-b0b5-5d564a04aafd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c29ea0e-f357-5dc3-99ca-2bae8aec29b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $2$" :db/id 17592186404045} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ea6ef9-6b75-5257-b121-1b523e9b915b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $1$" :db/id 17592186404046} {:answer/id #uuid "666c638e-5bf2-5c76-ab61-9718d548bf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $3$" :db/id 17592186404047} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7dd162-1ea2-5c31-abfa-4c05e5a985ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $4$" :db/id 17592186404048} {:answer/id #uuid "ac02fad1-6d75-529b-b2da-c5e1cd09aae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not homogeneous" :db/id 17592186404049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "666c638e-5bf2-5c76-ab61-9718d548bf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $3$" :db/id 17592186404047} :answer-field/id #uuid "040589f2-2b68-5371-a3aa-d45a057e519d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26800737-1d20-5fc8-93e8-d8bcbcdef142" :question/math-academy-id "q-79577" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x,y,z)=2xyz-{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}z$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}f(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =2(tx)(ty)(tz)-(ty)^{3}+(ty)^{2}(tz) \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}xyz-{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{y}^{2}z \\\\  & ={t}^{3}(2xyz-{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}z) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y,z)$ is $3.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dec0bd00-6c95-5ad2-9a20-e6fa27f6b7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186404052} {:answer/id #uuid "4027ffef-21bf-5026-a35d-cacbaeea7718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186404053} {:answer/id #uuid "377531b4-668f-592e-8418-7d547f543a49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186404054} {:answer/id #uuid "37f0ffe2-851a-513c-8df5-26662dc4ef64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186404055} {:answer/id #uuid "0646923e-bb24-5a58-bdd6-f6f8d6b30748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186404056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0646923e-bb24-5a58-bdd6-f6f8d6b30748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186404056} :answer-field/id #uuid "a78239aa-6f5c-55b1-bce4-31c264920ad6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25cb2680-651e-5a50-81aa-a03a420f17ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-79609" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nI. ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y$\nII. ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-xy$\nIII. ${D}_{3}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{1}]+b[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+bx\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-ax{y}_{1}-bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{1}]+b[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\na{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})=a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee4f807-4aa5-5a16-8059-65d96394945a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{({x}^{4}+C)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id 17592186404059} {:answer/id #uuid "223caf6f-af28-5ef9-9a4f-1a520db495e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{8}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186404060} {:answer/id #uuid "337814ed-5fcc-59cf-8f04-ff8ec9f234ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186404061} {:answer/id #uuid "7a73c3ff-fb2c-5b83-a30f-1c7130a2b411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186404062} {:answer/id #uuid "41ee67ee-bf37-531f-ae68-93bf2d23f87f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{8}+C}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id 17592186404063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ee4f807-4aa5-5a16-8059-65d96394945a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{({x}^{4}+C)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id 17592186404059} :answer-field/id #uuid "8853e332-7fcd-583c-9b4c-e2dadf8be7ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "144b4980-16bd-56a0-bd2d-6e2904dde295" :question/math-academy-id "q-79784" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=8x{y}^{1/2}-\\frac{4}{x}y\\,,\\,y>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{1/2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1/2}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}=\\frac{1}{2{y}^{1/2}}\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}=2z\\frac{dz}{dx}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =8xz-\\frac{4}{x}{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-\\frac{2}{x}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-\\frac{2}{x}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=4x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (4x) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =4{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & ={x}^{4}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{({x}^{4}+C)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfbba14c-2f05-5726-a785-65e6001ed044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186404066} {:answer/id #uuid "4c881cec-e1cd-5edf-9137-3cff2e33554f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186404067} {:answer/id #uuid "641040d1-65b8-5f54-96f4-8c708a40416c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186404068} {:answer/id #uuid "3591323b-6891-5cae-8d4e-c948451b3786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186404069} {:answer/id #uuid "f99f217b-b9d6-5f00-901a-4222c7e159a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186404070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c881cec-e1cd-5edf-9137-3cff2e33554f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186404067} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae248aa2-3ead-50b7-9e6b-de77efc79d9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "742f0d7e-0be6-5200-afaf-b211266d03e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-79799" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nI. $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}$\nII. $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$\nIII. $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}=\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{e}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{e},{c}_{2}=0.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because ${e}^{-4t}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a3eb10-2e29-538b-8e42-ae99c94ac32f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}" :db/id 17592186404073} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb2d24f-0cf5-5987-8f28-2e07f5e68a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x)" :db/id 17592186404074} {:answer/id #uuid "a90d16a2-31d8-5954-91c9-74ac65bc33c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id 17592186404075} {:answer/id #uuid "cd772fdb-05d3-5e4f-8029-e1013c88e05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x-1)^{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id 17592186404076} {:answer/id #uuid "845d8cbd-0461-59c4-ad0c-0c173f3eec19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x-1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id 17592186404077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a90d16a2-31d8-5954-91c9-74ac65bc33c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id 17592186404075} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5400590-876e-51ce-80d8-59c049ac6615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2213d2c-5fa6-5310-a52f-41ff5bbd4ec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79847" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n(x+1)\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{y}^{1/2}(x+1)^{3}+2y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{4},\\,x>-1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z=(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{1/2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "The substitution $z={y}^{1/2}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z=(x+1)^{2},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition\n\n$$\nz(0)=\\sqrt{y(0)}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1},\\,Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\int 1/(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)^{-1}} \\\\  & =(x+1)^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>-1.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=(x+1)^{-1}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z) & =\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,z & =x+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z) & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ z & =(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the value of $C$ that satisfies the initial condition $z(0)=\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(0) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (0+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{0}^{2}+0+C) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have \n\n$$\nz=(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2} & =(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f407cd0-11c1-59c6-a7c5-25e4be37e2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Series Solution to a First-Order Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6afa0a96-1e18-57e5-96cd-8e8e36f2fa90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404080} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7b5a8b-e6fb-5d55-8fd3-3aca05a0f8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404081} {:answer/id #uuid "56264eb4-a51b-5aab-ba16-b250b94b35ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404082} {:answer/id #uuid "44cffaf9-c852-530e-9f05-aa73eb3e2216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404083} {:answer/id #uuid "db0e845a-af2d-57ff-ad0f-ce6c7a19a615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56264eb4-a51b-5aab-ba16-b250b94b35ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404082} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a9402c-24fc-5626-9974-b4187437af29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bddd3bab-9ed7-5695-ab3a-94854e4c62de" :question/math-academy-id "q-79950" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=yx+{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=0,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =y(0)(0)+{e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)={y}^{′}x+y+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & ={y}^{′}(0)(0)+y(0)+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)={y}^{″}x+{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}={y}^{″}x+2{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{″}(0)(0)+2{y}^{′}(0)+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+2(1)+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =0+x\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot 3+⋯ \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f407cd0-11c1-59c6-a7c5-25e4be37e2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Series Solution to a First-Order Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "848ad199-1e25-5c94-a266-2a096cb64c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+x-{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404087} {:answer/id #uuid "3c14f5c5-8ea4-5bf2-8134-553a460b1e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404088} {:answer/id #uuid "2328a7d3-d4ee-5a30-ad0f-222ab3e91a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+2x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404089} {:answer/id #uuid "c15ca29a-9669-5876-aeb2-1a4d68c21031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404090} {:answer/id #uuid "48088ea4-10b5-580c-ba68-2cb63181cdf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c15ca29a-9669-5876-aeb2-1a4d68c21031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404090} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b2114bc-ceff-5608-a240-0cb6adf8c6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13dde15f-53dc-52e9-8781-7f7c083b83f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79951" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2(0)-y(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=2-{y}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =2-{y}^{′}(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+y(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =2-(1)(1)+(-1)(0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=-{y}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-{y}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =-{y}^{″}(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2{y}^{′}(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+y(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =-(1)(1)+2(1)(0)+(-1)(1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-2)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7df3b7c7-8ab5-56e8-8b9c-3e3f6b261779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=7,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id 17592186404094} {:answer/id #uuid "bb986d69-47f8-539e-8e78-570956df6719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=7,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id 17592186404095} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7bfd3a-84c3-5bd7-86de-43e46a13b773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=7$" :db/id 17592186404096} {:answer/id #uuid "72889b64-0d8f-5cf5-a078-304cd0270229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=7$" :db/id 17592186404097} {:answer/id #uuid "d85b9e14-5285-547f-8225-bac68ffb2c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=1$" :db/id 17592186404098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d85b9e14-5285-547f-8225-bac68ffb2c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=1$" :db/id 17592186404098} :answer-field/id #uuid "9085b031-ef66-5fae-9905-de9a5b2e5bed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e995591-79aa-5800-bd07-e99e51f5d9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-80373" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $H$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb0eb10f-8358-5f70-9dcf-5a12623a7ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404101} {:answer/id #uuid "1df643a8-80e1-5748-9179-fabd113dde47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404102} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd75f60-e366-51aa-9704-98e0a480bbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404103} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9540b0-0bdb-50eb-99c1-9744cc3a5660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404104} {:answer/id #uuid "f62ab074-e099-5956-af5c-d32875cc38df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa9540b0-0bdb-50eb-99c1-9744cc3a5660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404104} :answer-field/id #uuid "76990587-1106-5838-a7d3-5abb529f309f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fb53181-29d9-5f89-8598-3c491720e6bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80389" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "639780ae-1398-50d7-b815-e2a195a0e73d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id 17592186404108} {:answer/id #uuid "c73ad559-0eae-5f15-bdf7-739e613b69ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id 17592186404109} {:answer/id #uuid "d0038efe-a96a-5281-ad36-c4af7f9c03a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id 17592186404110} {:answer/id #uuid "c0406221-3155-5859-8236-e58b9f481700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id 17592186404111} {:answer/id #uuid "d939f8e6-1f46-548c-8adc-229c62fd7d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=5$" :db/id 17592186404112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "639780ae-1398-50d7-b815-e2a195a0e73d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id 17592186404108} :answer-field/id #uuid "b352245b-eaa4-5fde-aea9-5afac2d00b93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84388249-87e9-543c-a81f-fd99783cee24" :question/math-academy-id "q-80392" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15560ba1-c0e7-57a3-bd24-dc19e7e9f52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404115} {:answer/id #uuid "4a752dce-688f-5fd3-becb-69a53449004c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404116} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bff80f-44fe-590e-825f-851b2661193c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404117} {:answer/id #uuid "2042fd4c-3a1d-5b77-a6af-7aa6566dcc2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404118} {:answer/id #uuid "8b20c357-a80f-5d68-9f17-47dfa214c55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2042fd4c-3a1d-5b77-a6af-7aa6566dcc2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404118} :answer-field/id #uuid "958185d9-bfb3-5c99-9580-566f3546885e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7be4462e-3d0b-5745-b83e-db8e5d74f57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80493" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20692b0c-ecf3-5857-b371-5e00dd088456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404122} {:answer/id #uuid "8297858c-6a75-5e90-8004-887fd5f4fe6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id 17592186404123} {:answer/id #uuid "21bdb776-d042-5bc3-8def-4d142fb67170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404124} {:answer/id #uuid "9a56eb51-adbb-529e-b66c-37695fb34a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404125} {:answer/id #uuid "3093f458-0d1e-55d2-af6e-2473cd7616a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3093f458-0d1e-55d2-af6e-2473cd7616a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404126} :answer-field/id #uuid "5324a7ce-971d-53cb-b107-19a127a08e1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f53e1d4c-a53d-5c3e-8ae8-0e080a8e331f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80496" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-3\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$  Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Constants in First-Order Partial Derivative Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37daf35b-b051-5de5-be26-c9ede321ab36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1$, $b=2$" :db/id 17592186404129} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb42258-31a2-56c5-aa5a-940f6339e1ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1$, $b=2$" :db/id 17592186404130} {:answer/id #uuid "92a398de-810f-54f4-a742-9f17711093b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2$, $b=e$" :db/id 17592186404131} {:answer/id #uuid "36f61f3a-a59d-517f-b778-17ee028f7808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-e$, $b=2$" :db/id 17592186404132} {:answer/id #uuid "7d026b63-b9dd-5d7f-972a-f02973c06d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2$, $b=1$" :db/id 17592186404133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d026b63-b9dd-5d7f-972a-f02973c06d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2$, $b=1$" :db/id 17592186404133} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa7f024f-dcae-5a46-aab4-36686da92a80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f63374b4-82d5-5092-bcef-8ec87e149ab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80766" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}=b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $(1+x){e}^{x}$ and $x{y}^{2},$ respectively, we see that $b=1$ and $a=-2.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5abea3ab-76a7-5598-8ede-b4cf88dd47d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues of a General 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d3a6e5c-6b63-525d-b4d4-f7578f8a390f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {1,-6}" :db/id 17592186404136} {:answer/id #uuid "be0e2c11-87fe-59eb-9698-32afe60aafab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-1,2,-3}" :db/id 17592186404137} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd9bbcf-ec11-5649-bcb8-ae71202883ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {2,-3}" :db/id 17592186404138} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdcb0b4-6e56-57cc-bb23-2e4c99b0b161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-1,-2,3}" :db/id 17592186404139} {:answer/id #uuid "415b6c2c-e8ea-529a-83eb-176e95a19f4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-1,2}" :db/id 17592186404140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bdcb0b4-6e56-57cc-bb23-2e4c99b0b161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-1,-2,3}" :db/id 17592186404139} :answer-field/id #uuid "135ac5a3-acd1-57d7-85af-ac59d7bb8214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba3b9cc6-841d-5636-9777-0bc1e8c24e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80825" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}+6\\lambda +5)-(-1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-7\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $6:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 6\n$$\n\n- Let's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}-7(1)-6 & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Let's pick $x=-1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{3}-7(-1)-6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =-1$ is a root of the equation.\n\nSo, we can factor $(\\lambda +1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}-7\\lambda -6$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +1)({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f185774-4d55-515c-9032-8f6832c6999a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Truth Value of a Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ac2392-a6e5-591f-a5a1-17941b5ea7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Possible" :db/id 17592186404143} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc0e3a4-8b74-5fcf-b175-8552a9860e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Maybe" :db/id 17592186404144} {:answer/id #uuid "2b59ca4a-6fb7-5fb3-abb1-7608d84f6622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id 17592186404145} {:answer/id #uuid "93659c74-5338-5ca4-a3f8-18b1cd2f5221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Sometimes" :db/id 17592186404146} {:answer/id #uuid "e029fc14-4c44-508f-af5e-dbb44d816ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id 17592186404147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e029fc14-4c44-508f-af5e-dbb44d816ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id 17592186404147} :answer-field/id #uuid "5139bddd-0e23-5ba6-baed-c7f7bf6d512d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2aeaa42-0925-5203-ab6b-c6edd9263eda" :question/math-academy-id "q-80999" :question/problem "What is the truth value of the following mathematical statement?\n\n$\\,$ A square has exactly $3$ sides." :question/worked-solution "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nThis statement is false because a square has exactly $4$ sides."}] [{:db/id 17592186404148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d098974e-26ed-5d31-b1cd-a3dbc06e5513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id 17592186404150} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a92768-6f32-5589-8651-0b25ea1a112b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id 17592186404151} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd5d749-fe1e-50cd-8a40-2be69a0ab8d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id 17592186404152} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fb75dc-1ab6-5d98-9700-9f4e9f0bef00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186404153} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5c324b-f42d-5122-b7c3-fe01bb4516cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id 17592186404154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd5d749-fe1e-50cd-8a40-2be69a0ab8d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id 17592186404152} :answer-field/id #uuid "235aadc5-963f-5fda-85cc-68d107673c61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "225d5964-b5f6-5f9c-9606-7856be911138" :question/math-academy-id "q-81202" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0+4-2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =4-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id 17592186404155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f64437bc-258f-57d6-8252-86408694ed54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404157} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fce00d-d8dc-5d30-b228-f50bd9f70fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404158} {:answer/id #uuid "ea65c7ad-6c2b-58e5-8658-7ac9d1dfed34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404159} {:answer/id #uuid "06abf95b-dbcf-5d63-a7e2-6b9ef8e25083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404160} {:answer/id #uuid "163ea62e-033a-5464-9c63-6deea7d57755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "163ea62e-033a-5464-9c63-6deea7d57755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404161} :answer-field/id #uuid "630df7f3-d3b0-52b1-a138-2b8c01a3e3ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "811972db-6534-593a-aa1e-100258f3cf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81236" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\nT[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a499ca4-07df-5c8d-b83c-a9910cc2fe6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=v$ and $u=-v.$" :db/id 17592186404164} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf255f1-3d51-5826-98c1-93901517d605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $u=v.$" :db/id 17592186404165} {:answer/id #uuid "5da5054c-581f-5f31-9886-dad4f588043d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $u={v}^{2}.$" :db/id 17592186404166} {:answer/id #uuid "6060e83f-92e8-5d62-986a-7a4aa49b2792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $u=-v.$" :db/id 17592186404167} {:answer/id #uuid "1669bc78-1efd-58f9-91e9-a08a66025cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0$ and on the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$" :db/id 17592186404168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1669bc78-1efd-58f9-91e9-a08a66025cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0$ and on the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$" :db/id 17592186404168} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd706ed-a6fa-5777-8fba-b9aa9ded06db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82fb995e-103e-58b4-a8e9-4c6b2963c1a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81273" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}\\,\\,v \\\\ u+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}\\,\\,v$ and $y(u,v)=u+{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={u}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2uv & {u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2uv\\cdot 2v-{u}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4u{v}^{2}-{u}^{2} \\\\  & =u(4{v}^{2}-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ u(4{v}^{2}-u) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equation $u(4{v}^{2}-u)=0$ defines a pair of intersecting curves in the $uv$-plane: the line $u=0$ and the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dd60f7-0060-5908-af95-4c108c3b1718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :db/id 17592186404171} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2acdfc-59b5-5eb0-aa0e-5c6158877fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $2u+3v=0.$" :db/id 17592186404172} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd5de86-0281-5ef0-9bac-c48c24b2a6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/id 17592186404173} {:answer/id #uuid "90292b65-61da-58e0-bd75-eda1b58d756e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id 17592186404174} {:answer/id #uuid "22f6e316-1aa7-5c12-abf8-9b28b8e93f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id 17592186404175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca2acdfc-59b5-5eb0-aa0e-5c6158877fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $2u+3v=0.$" :db/id 17592186404172} :answer-field/id #uuid "e87074f0-9d22-553d-826e-78e5774760d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59e9bc67-c967-5c58-a234-d26582651fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81279" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+3{v}^{2} \\\\ u-2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+3{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u-2v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 6v \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2)-6v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u+3v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $2u+3v=0$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id 17592186404176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cbca2a4-5302-5621-923e-847f18587e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id 17592186404178} {:answer/id #uuid "686c83f8-b2ff-5446-a69b-a6dca4ee631b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id 17592186404179} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e87b1e-635e-59bb-a8b0-e23b7a0b0aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id 17592186404180} {:answer/id #uuid "1c87caa4-7f94-5398-b1e3-967d57492e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id 17592186404181} {:answer/id #uuid "2280b79f-4cae-53e8-ad6b-44654f7eae72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id 17592186404182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c87caa4-7f94-5398-b1e3-967d57492e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id 17592186404181} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2dd7953-8b90-52cd-be66-71f8f16406ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5883242f-b44f-5ff7-9fa7-30bcd180241a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81324" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of ${R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=\\frac{1}{2}i$ and $T(j)=j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $i$ and  $j.$\n\n![](images/34/34c82826da26290fc890671734c354fbfd9c253f1cfaca38de1ea615434e6766.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=\\frac{1}{2}i$ and $T(j)=j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](images/02/02470083dea4808af1854e9ed88ab496828a429c593af780209d8a6d4b15a6b1.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction."}] [{:db/id 17592186404183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5009b51a-3311-5349-a0d4-9263093c408c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" :db/id 17592186404185} {:answer/id #uuid "15868a01-ce63-5e6b-b375-fec48a6e6ad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id 17592186404186} {:answer/id #uuid "55de43a8-0397-50c5-867b-d34348940323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id 17592186404187} {:answer/id #uuid "5c165536-c143-5156-8063-ecfdc1329b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id 17592186404188} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1de765-7e1f-51a4-a847-e7a575a67eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ only" :db/id 17592186404189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5009b51a-3311-5349-a0d4-9263093c408c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" :db/id 17592186404185} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e82b63e-4a76-52fe-b5a0-9529b5e4e1a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fe978ee-e242-5740-b3cc-178c387af89b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81386" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+4y \\\\ y+z \\\\ x-4z\\end{aligned}]$ and the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the vectors that belong to the kernel of $T$ are ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd566f7-15e2-59d9-bf6a-1fa3598399b9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Composite Function Using a Standard Maclaurin Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f99b133-4a61-5da9-8229-2d15c1976133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{720}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404192} {:answer/id #uuid "9040cf5a-c20f-5c3d-8188-6973a3292336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{240}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404193} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc4cf98-68bd-567f-b621-955fa5da11dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{240}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404194} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbcf19d-8dfb-53a4-baf5-a9db5210ac2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{720}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404195} {:answer/id #uuid "4c08336c-5cbd-5687-910d-5e791a42e28f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{240}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f99b133-4a61-5da9-8229-2d15c1976133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{720}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404192} :answer-field/id #uuid "48887a96-5fe7-5b45-98ea-8163f74623cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0508a683-ff80-5adf-84b7-14d7f45257c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-8171" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $\\sqrt{x}$ in the above expansion and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x} & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{4}}{4!}-\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{6}}{6!}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{720}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc0be03-9369-508e-9765-8c88871ec20b" :knowledge-point/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b7885b-ae95-575e-97f6-8a71e1b57ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186404199} {:answer/id #uuid "65627c21-66cb-5f02-8ba6-0c37010c9bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id 17592186404200} {:answer/id #uuid "dea138f5-3204-5f95-9262-ff365ea22b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186404201} {:answer/id #uuid "3f211c15-8d28-53f3-8403-b6b4d9ef7e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no maximum value" :db/id 17592186404202} {:answer/id #uuid "21d62bc4-f68d-55ab-b0ca-9dee218d1167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186404203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f211c15-8d28-53f3-8403-b6b4d9ef7e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no maximum value" :db/id 17592186404202} :answer-field/id #uuid "64bfa454-0c70-5d48-80bc-d4490bd1867f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b338b5cf-801c-50c4-9622-d9ab0fa4da90" :question/math-academy-id "q-81863" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-3)+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no maximum value."}] [{:db/id 17592186060420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58ec96-53b3-5317-9801-c730728f32c6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Contrapositive of a Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59ef73e-2be3-53b3-ad02-9dc50f3be05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is not warm outside, then you don't need a jacket." :db/id 17592186404205} {:answer/id #uuid "066a4a63-27d3-54e5-b1e8-2493f211b5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you need a jacket, then it is warm outside." :db/id 17592186404206} {:answer/id #uuid "55062177-0b75-5d06-a6a5-cdf3aa66a762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't need a jacket, then it is warm outside." :db/id 17592186404207} {:answer/id #uuid "25b3e2ab-6ad3-5e7c-a3e4-9a4c09ebf969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is warm outside, then you don't need a jacket." :db/id 17592186404208} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8aa02d-eb4a-599b-b592-da512b965916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you need a jacket, then it is not warm outside." :db/id 17592186404209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55062177-0b75-5d06-a6a5-cdf3aa66a762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't need a jacket, then it is warm outside." :db/id 17592186404207} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d552753-26be-5a35-820e-a8e846bf8657" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "929e0321-87ad-5ccc-a7b5-9aeb909c70ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-81985" :question/problem "Find the contrapositive of the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ **If** it is not warm outside, **then** you need a jacket." :question/worked-solution "In our conditional statement: \n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ It is not warm outside.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ You need a jacket.\n\nThe contrapositive of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\n¬B⇒¬A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the negations of $A$ and $B:$\n\n- $¬A:$ It is warm outside.\n\n- $¬B:$ You don't need a jacket.\n\nTherefore, the contrapositive is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If you don't need a jacket, then it is warm outside."}] [{:db/id 17592186404210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec5acb94-b33a-5921-b761-6f78db3f25f7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Converse of a Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7d619e0-9c27-57b7-88a2-a8566007c2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is not winter, then it is not snowing." :db/id 17592186404212} {:answer/id #uuid "4327cf36-0d54-568f-9b23-fb28f90fac91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is snowing, then it is winter." :db/id 17592186404213} {:answer/id #uuid "63c8f492-71bb-5a22-aa16-d6742bb59a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is winter, then it is not snowing." :db/id 17592186404214} {:answer/id #uuid "3b39a0d4-fe13-5d8d-8219-7578a6fcf9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is not snowing, then it is winter." :db/id 17592186404215} {:answer/id #uuid "72312945-3764-584f-9205-240825178c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is winter, then it is snowing." :db/id 17592186404216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7d619e0-9c27-57b7-88a2-a8566007c2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is not winter, then it is not snowing." :db/id 17592186404212} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b467a52-98b6-5550-9876-540b62223358" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "370105fd-4191-5aeb-88c5-e95f00d93193" :question/math-academy-id "q-82627" :question/problem "Find the converse of the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ **If** it is not snowing, **then** it is not winter." :question/worked-solution "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ It is not snowing.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ It is not winter.\n\nThe converse of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\nB⇒A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the converse is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If it is not winter, then it is not snowing."}] [{:db/id 17592186404217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec5acb94-b33a-5921-b761-6f78db3f25f7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Converse of a Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23228fce-70f0-5dba-9000-708acac135b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are watching TV, then you are listening to music." :db/id 17592186404219} {:answer/id #uuid "5857c6c1-9129-5867-b73b-a4a5c5d0d9cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are listening to music, then you are not watching TV." :db/id 17592186404220} {:answer/id #uuid "9063fc79-ede2-57e3-a9f9-32c94704548c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are listening to music, then you are watching TV." :db/id 17592186404221} {:answer/id #uuid "59e3af6f-b80c-5ecf-960d-802915fd17be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are not listening to music, then you are watching TV." :db/id 17592186404222} {:answer/id #uuid "c78db225-a5cc-51a0-a9b1-3f7a16c6e468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are watching TV, then you are not listening to music." :db/id 17592186404223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5857c6c1-9129-5867-b73b-a4a5c5d0d9cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are listening to music, then you are not watching TV." :db/id 17592186404220} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e370986-ac15-5412-8ce9-6f960a44aba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89a8d37a-6c72-523d-8fc0-d006e8d4daf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-82630" :question/problem "Find the converse of the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ **If** you are not watching TV, **then** you are listening to music." :question/worked-solution "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ You are not watching TV.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ You are listening to music.\n\nThe converse of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\nB⇒A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the converse is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If you are listening to music, then you are not watching TV."}] [{:db/id 17592186157828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "857d696d-6a3f-5fff-a5e1-b05148f28f55" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Sentences Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25b3b537-e2d4-5af5-abe1-7fe27d620072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is blue." :db/id 17592186404225} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8926ca-6ebf-5aa1-80b8-915743a3f1a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is red." :db/id 17592186404226} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1aeba2-f7a1-5ed9-8109-e70dd146aca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is blue or red." :db/id 17592186404227} {:answer/id #uuid "2b83dfed-9c07-56fa-b16a-12c95687af8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is blue and red." :db/id 17592186404228} {:answer/id #uuid "924f8062-92c3-57c2-a169-650f347777d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is blue or red but not both." :db/id 17592186404229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f1aeba2-f7a1-5ed9-8109-e70dd146aca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is blue or red." :db/id 17592186404227} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca5578dd-0a57-5cc2-809c-152390f18874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ae3ad46-5289-52fe-9fde-b40fc7aa7003" :question/math-academy-id "q-82720" :question/problem "Simplify the following sentence: \"The pen is blue and it is a blue pen, or the pen is blue or it is a red pen.\"" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the phrase \"the pen is blue and it is a blue pen\" to just \"the pen is blue.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The pen is blue, or the pen is blue or it is a red pen.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma in the sentence:\n\n\"The pen is blue or the pen is blue, or it is a red pen.\"\n\nNow, we simplify the phrase \"the pen is blue or the pen is blue\" to just \"the pen is blue.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The pen is blue, or it is a red pen.\"\n\nFinally, we simplify the sentence to\n\n\"The pen is blue or red.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186404230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149709f4-f9d8-529f-87af-a022ec1d46a8" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Sentences Using the Commutative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b32a31f4-569d-51f0-9177-a1505090b425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is new but not red" :db/id 17592186404232} {:answer/id #uuid "03d7b53f-1cda-5f14-aa6d-87acf47842ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is red but not new" :db/id 17592186404233} {:answer/id #uuid "a997887d-6b8a-5033-a7c7-39403e21d00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is new and red" :db/id 17592186404234} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0f91ed-949b-5609-8a80-2ea003d3abbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is red" :db/id 17592186404235} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4804d0-20da-5a86-b01a-abf2739587c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is new or red" :db/id 17592186404236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a997887d-6b8a-5033-a7c7-39403e21d00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is new and red" :db/id 17592186404234} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1094ea0-4165-5ef2-8aaf-f67fca2d0314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edbfe110-6f37-5a51-8f96-227364b973f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83001" :question/problem "Identify the sentence that is equivalent to the following: \"The cup is red and it is new.\"" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"The cup is red and new.\"\n\nUsing the commutative law, we can swap the order of the words \"red\" and \"new.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The cup is new and red.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186404237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa22294c-97df-59d3-a2ef-bb723379bd93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-1}" :db/id 17592186404239} {:answer/id #uuid "05c76659-9ee2-5ea1-9921-4c61064bc47c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2)/3}" :db/id 17592186404240} {:answer/id #uuid "d957c339-7d86-5b49-bfb4-f34772448576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-1)/3}" :db/id 17592186404241} {:answer/id #uuid "59acccda-8ad0-5671-8d3b-1eba77285637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-5}" :db/id 17592186404242} {:answer/id #uuid "08886f8e-1110-502c-b6b0-62359e9dd0d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4)/3}" :db/id 17592186404243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08886f8e-1110-502c-b6b0-62359e9dd0d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4)/3}" :db/id 17592186404243} :answer-field/id #uuid "5399864b-fdb6-560a-bb1c-eadfa2e8485b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "177e97c5-b0fe-5fdf-abe1-80b42d3faf53" :question/math-academy-id "q-8306" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2yx+y{x}^{2}$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2yx+y{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(2x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=1$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =1 \\\\ K{e}^{{1}^{2}+{1}^{3}/3} & =1 \\\\ K{e}^{4/3} & =1 \\\\ K & ={e}^{-4/3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2yx+y{x}^{2}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4/3}\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3}={e}^{({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4)/3}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01db7ff6-6460-5d8d-ade8-71a8e02c2a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{19}{54}" :db/id 17592186404246} {:answer/id #uuid "d8373422-756d-5371-80d9-489591080546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{17}{81}" :db/id 17592186404247} {:answer/id #uuid "635cb5d7-7f18-5bb2-a15b-2282c9accf79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{8}{27}" :db/id 17592186404248} {:answer/id #uuid "4da8621d-de59-5cf2-8a08-783a60173651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id 17592186404249} {:answer/id #uuid "064bd6fd-ada7-5cba-9a3e-93099a992f58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id 17592186404250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01db7ff6-6460-5d8d-ade8-71a8e02c2a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{19}{54}" :db/id 17592186404246} :answer-field/id #uuid "62f9d6d4-3621-5ba8-8abe-069970462f3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f750b509-3c63-5fc0-8993-9bde234b6a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83217" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${x}^{-1/2}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of ${8}^{-1/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\ f(9) & ={9}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(9)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1/2} & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 27}(x-9)+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{x-9}{54}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=8$ into the above to approximate ${8}^{-1/2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{-1/2} & ≈\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{8-9}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{54}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de71fcb6-36ca-581a-92ea-47c1d0c6bf10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Product of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6684cb3-66f7-5670-a618-240321438de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{120}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404253} {:answer/id #uuid "d8eb2141-deaf-5745-8f39-c0df69c4795f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{12}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{60}+⋯" :db/id 17592186404254} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f058db-10d3-5127-a407-810a87d6f495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}-⋯" :db/id 17592186404255} {:answer/id #uuid "6537e3c8-b8ab-55e5-9855-26f836f4373a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{24}+\\frac{{x}^{9}}{96}-⋯" :db/id 17592186404256} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccc3d10-6b2b-5fed-b615-462c22afdb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{120}-⋯" :db/id 17592186404257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ccc3d10-6b2b-5fed-b615-462c22afdb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{120}-⋯" :db/id 17592186404257} :answer-field/id #uuid "38f38032-483e-5ebe-a2ba-4fc05febd614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83cee5be-d033-528b-aa1a-c2f13225ee96" :question/math-academy-id "q-83429" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯ \\\\  & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an expansion for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we multiply this series by ${x}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ={x}^{2}(x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{120}-⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is Piecewise Smooth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cccc9be8-40ad-59d1-85aa-0c32e8379a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-10$ and ${f}_{max}=5$" :db/id 17592186404260} {:answer/id #uuid "ec57171d-50ae-555b-9220-9f9b5b798b9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/id 17592186404261} {:answer/id #uuid "097476c4-4c40-568d-90a8-2be9e9dfcb7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/id 17592186404262} {:answer/id #uuid "ae56b583-e6d7-5084-af2a-bd8d9b194ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id 17592186404263} {:answer/id #uuid "d5491fbd-ca0b-57cf-bb3e-94617777e540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id 17592186404264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5491fbd-ca0b-57cf-bb3e-94617777e540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id 17592186404264} :answer-field/id #uuid "24e0c5a8-3acd-57db-aba7-34b3ec30ab80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "74b4ac53-7bdb-5efd-8618-b21e911d16f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83479" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8ce943f8a77fe5756fdaf77185d8b59493ef0da95da3605f1f2416786f9ec394.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=xy-4$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y,\\,x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0,\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,4)$ and $(2,4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,4) \\\\  & =(t)(4)-4 \\\\  & =4t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,4)$ and $(2,4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =(t)({t}^{2})-4 \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3}-4)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution $t=0$ inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one candidate point, $(0,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,4),\\,(-2,4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(-2,4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-12$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-12$ at the point $(-2,4)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4$ at the points $(2,4).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d23ea584-4a5e-5d3f-b114-caef1a56d6ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id 17592186404267} {:answer/id #uuid "178d8403-eb54-593d-abc3-5b52a35b7885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186404268} {:answer/id #uuid "ee26e4a3-b2b4-56a4-a778-0012ee7e2935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186404269} {:answer/id #uuid "38e4fc3c-40eb-5ddc-a361-e415766dbedb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}" :db/id 17592186404270} {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186404271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186404271} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d75aab-799f-5138-9f8f-42b050f51ab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef24453f-f045-5d0a-8ed3-0ca5b930bc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-8360" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=0\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c2a855-07a3-5855-9f6c-1a914953ac53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5069fb02-022e-544e-b7ca-c45166ca4bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+x}" :db/id 17592186404274} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcfa690-e3cd-5cc8-9177-2dc5f0ed3023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186404275} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec33d3c-cfb8-5e66-a78e-a3afbcab3a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186404276} {:answer/id #uuid "2b98ed15-9816-5e8c-8df1-651d5c3a7b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186404277} {:answer/id #uuid "e5065de5-7957-55f6-bf19-b00b1fba3806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{y-{x}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186404278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ec33d3c-cfb8-5e66-a78e-a3afbcab3a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id 17592186404276} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19317f0-552c-5bb1-aa73-1ed888b17d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29260781-8760-5b93-aff1-74357dc9fb2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8369" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}-(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}+(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous."}] [{:db/id 17592186404279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8e2916-1175-544e-9d6d-7e8a9cd2eca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404281} {:answer/id #uuid "812a013d-6c97-5c8d-8555-41c27f26371f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404282} {:answer/id #uuid "62777a5a-2c5b-5c2c-bc49-88fd1f72656d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404283} {:answer/id #uuid "58ff7c16-1b9d-5ada-98a5-763a6cfe2824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404284} {:answer/id #uuid "1184eb62-ac2c-5e6b-abd1-f1c4d51d39b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58ff7c16-1b9d-5ada-98a5-763a6cfe2824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8de3def-44e0-5291-9c5d-3c0a5037be6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c42e4814-ec50-5416-8161-bbb51b0ed62b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83710" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91c8360c-3a50-5dde-b1bc-982c3982cc9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404288} {:answer/id #uuid "f6699882-8461-5d24-9c25-bf00717bab1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404289} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b4c43c-1866-559f-80a2-eb60c22ddff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404290} {:answer/id #uuid "77ed7e08-d0f9-52c5-bb87-4330aef31eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404291} {:answer/id #uuid "4129fe51-c35a-5376-82e4-222db49da083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9b4c43c-1866-559f-80a2-eb60c22ddff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404290} :answer-field/id #uuid "e147ba3d-c970-55a9-ac96-8187378e9cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "303c15f5-3e83-55c0-a519-b60eaa51efba" :question/math-academy-id "q-83854" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9c721ea-d96e-5bf0-bc9c-8798a2a4ddf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404295} {:answer/id #uuid "4f82210a-e608-50a1-bf42-9e7411d614b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404296} {:answer/id #uuid "94c87bfa-134c-55bd-87ae-8cfa1aedb050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404297} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e1ec20-80aa-55b5-9af6-1c4b410f8f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404298} {:answer/id #uuid "606da9ae-0a33-5676-904e-bbed5b045212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9c721ea-d96e-5bf0-bc9c-8798a2a4ddf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id 17592186404295} :answer-field/id #uuid "99303798-a587-5408-b480-605526c75a44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "497d051f-d1fb-59f1-b865-99c679ab4f76" :question/math-academy-id "q-83860" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c249cf3a-7864-5132-80fa-c18a3ea915c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercepts of a Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dd6f7f5-37b3-532d-8604-ed7c9c933ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 1)" :db/id 17592186404302} {:answer/id #uuid "27d07b8f-b1e9-5fce-8842-2396695c113b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id 17592186404303} {:answer/id #uuid "0328b079-91e5-5b86-b53f-6e81abca1363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 3)" :db/id 17592186404304} {:answer/id #uuid "6217ec90-1f6d-59be-8656-6ad6c5565558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id 17592186404305} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0207ca-8734-51b5-98fe-ceb135d74c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no $y$-intercepts" :db/id 17592186404306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae0207ca-8734-51b5-98fe-ceb135d74c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no $y$-intercepts" :db/id 17592186404306} :answer-field/id #uuid "387fbfdb-9f04-5077-9421-33470d17faf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1131433c-1254-58aa-b6db-67a84792b7de" :question/math-academy-id "q-84046" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $9{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ 9(0)^{2}-{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ -{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions.\nSo, there are no $y$-intercepts."}] [{:db/id 17592186404307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c249cf3a-7864-5132-80fa-c18a3ea915c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercepts of a Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35b03bdf-0e6c-570c-a516-bba9f81cd6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id 17592186404309} {:answer/id #uuid "90d6b647-06d9-5006-80fb-4f3c7d8c4b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2)$ and $(0,6)$" :db/id 17592186404310} {:answer/id #uuid "38c85c74-56cb-53b6-99b2-2ff6bfc5b6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id 17592186404311} {:answer/id #uuid "4361a63d-c6aa-5054-981d-0dbdb3ae3845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 4)" :db/id 17592186404312} {:answer/id #uuid "63643cf4-ce96-571d-b4c0-9f8c8508a137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no $y$-intercepts" :db/id 17592186404313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35b03bdf-0e6c-570c-a516-bba9f81cd6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id 17592186404309} :answer-field/id #uuid "b803308c-82e6-5338-8f67-691fc6a2b7e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3982bc28-35e9-548b-a313-78e4ed431710" :question/math-academy-id "q-84048" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ (0-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 9-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ y & =-6,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,-6)$ and $(0,2).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e82133f4-cf40-52df-89fc-b9095a2ed393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=13$" :db/id 17592186404316} {:answer/id #uuid "243d60de-8290-5d22-a02c-f2dd575ecea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id 17592186404317} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2cbf42-1425-51b3-9612-2bbf89e799f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id 17592186404318} {:answer/id #uuid "f5be6a6c-ff71-53df-b6a3-b84c12d5df62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=0$" :db/id 17592186404319} {:answer/id #uuid "896a5cf1-06e8-57d7-a9d3-ce00f8cd43ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id 17592186404320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f2cbf42-1425-51b3-9612-2bbf89e799f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id 17592186404318} :answer-field/id #uuid "8345e678-341d-5cbb-a53f-7923d2b2c4f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca9903e3-49ec-5aad-b531-9a0f3deac3f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84592" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ a & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & c & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ a & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & b\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cba355-0ee9-5493-b5b4-b01907ba7853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id 17592186404323} {:answer/id #uuid "88bc01f1-e620-5d5f-b135-3c829509a914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id 17592186404324} {:answer/id #uuid "c44c5852-2bff-553c-b41d-6c60dda93e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id 17592186404325} {:answer/id #uuid "e6647b56-89a1-5a81-85bf-14ccfa47147b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id 17592186404326} {:answer/id #uuid "814df066-a51f-5318-8a89-ed554769eecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id 17592186404327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6647b56-89a1-5a81-85bf-14ccfa47147b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id 17592186404326} :answer-field/id #uuid "9151fede-a06c-511f-acdd-16c15e3d7d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37e87bc7-80c8-5654-8c99-e96b9d9f6c6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-85640" :question/problem "![](images/97/97c98411b3455c78c9e061cf54fa76eabfccc369e9e364a5366eb58bd5d2935d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7},$ what is the value of $cot⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/8c/8c7889d63cf5894236bc5f5e24feb64ecf5a47acb2c07c807a4e65458a991232.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is negative in the fourth quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{cos⁡\\theta }{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{7})}{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bce3c75-6a0d-53d7-9a0d-6b6d8acd31bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id 17592186404330} {:answer/id #uuid "2613efdb-cd4c-5ddd-8173-e348bdcb96f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id 17592186404331} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7b0ef2-5302-5b29-89fc-f8f26371b54c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{12}" :db/id 17592186404332} {:answer/id #uuid "39dd9d4a-1c32-59d2-b731-ba230d302e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id 17592186404333} {:answer/id #uuid "b99f4a1b-d16f-5aa6-a399-a33703bd010f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id 17592186404334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bce3c75-6a0d-53d7-9a0d-6b6d8acd31bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id 17592186404330} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48430d0-09a9-535a-843b-f4fdfbee498f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0902cd66-9d18-50eb-a894-3b1edfc4b17b" :question/math-academy-id "q-85643" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3be6fa94deb7fc38d870e5a31cc81c3cfd6b9ab84c21340e7e88f7246b1058bc.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{7},$ what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/17/17e6320115f2cf8b5ffad6abc099169774536af20d2559b453aaa97c1286d96d.png)\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{24}}{7} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}=-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{6}}=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b68c25b-c193-5a40-829b-fd51b04788c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186404337} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd1cdc0-3122-5e73-85f9-af559fd24792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186404338} {:answer/id #uuid "7c200ff8-dd75-5421-837d-9ef23ff5522e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186404339} {:answer/id #uuid "5671ea71-84dd-554c-92ae-a5caaefc57b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-2\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186404340} {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186404341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186404341} :answer-field/id #uuid "3733f599-455f-5b42-9817-59fac3c5944c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7895edb1-f2c2-5302-ad9e-dc0b6ea9c033" :question/math-academy-id "q-86413" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}-4w+11=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}-4w+11) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}+w) & =11 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}w+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =11 \\\\ 4[{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1 & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =11+1 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=12.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |w+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ w+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ w & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a96b493-4dc4-5e3d-affb-5104b074be81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=14" :db/id 17592186404344} {:answer/id #uuid "a2847f48-b197-5c55-ac63-4e530e4e9ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=20" :db/id 17592186404345} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3d04fe-ed8a-51ec-a836-0b919705ce38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=25" :db/id 17592186404346} {:answer/id #uuid "93a6ab5a-4ea6-5f88-bef2-511b7baac0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id 17592186404347} {:answer/id #uuid "f625d56d-5576-52e1-a8c2-d2b4aa1f2141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=15" :db/id 17592186404348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2847f48-b197-5c55-ac63-4e530e4e9ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=20" :db/id 17592186404345} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3a12275-0df5-56dd-8578-50b10f58f3d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e1734459-a432-53d0-859c-83686d8f7d22" :question/math-academy-id "q-86414" :question/problem "The equation $-3{x}^{2}+36x-94=0$ can be written in the form $3(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}+36x-94) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-36x+94 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-36x+94 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-36x & =-94 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-12x] & =-94 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}] & =-94 \\\\ 3[{(x-6)}^{2}-36] & =-94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x-6)}^{2}-3\\cdot 36 & =-94 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2}-108 & =-94 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2} & =-94+108 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2} & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-6$, $q=14.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=14-(-6)=14+6=20.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id 17592186404351} {:answer/id #uuid "fff341dd-b41a-5304-ad09-cc4af765adfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id 17592186404352} {:answer/id #uuid "0b80197a-b970-5c44-9c91-75cf359e8575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=5$ and $w=\\frac{11}{2}$" :db/id 17592186404353} {:answer/id #uuid "fe959228-e459-5b19-9cf7-7d8a7111d3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id 17592186404354} {:answer/id #uuid "89145d06-76ec-5ca5-93f9-df5b33533d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $w=15$" :db/id 17592186404355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id 17592186404351} :answer-field/id #uuid "417df0a0-d7e8-5502-826d-e0777e84a95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "211d779e-92bf-5786-beb2-56b9bec0ebf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86415" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}+40w-75=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}+40w-75) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w & =-75 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}-10w) & =-75 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}-2\\cdot 5w+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}] & =-75 \\\\ 4[{(w-5)}^{2}-25] & =-75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2}-4\\cdot 25 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2}-100 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =-75+100 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=25.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w-5)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w-5)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w-5| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w-5 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =5\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =5+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ w & =5-\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead01c60-0b8e-5265-95fa-6b4cdaec742d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id 17592186404358} {:answer/id #uuid "db0835b3-9555-5f10-b783-ce2279912fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id 17592186404359} {:answer/id #uuid "c48ddddc-9d40-5c48-8b18-1935496f2905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id 17592186404360} {:answer/id #uuid "23d9111e-7ce1-561b-9f4f-29ebc2c46ba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id 17592186404361} {:answer/id #uuid "31a2fcdf-48af-528a-bc62-4f070d949ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id 17592186404362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c48ddddc-9d40-5c48-8b18-1935496f2905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id 17592186404360} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c6b9bc-2cad-5aef-8fbc-0df5f811dfbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32f5ebff-a024-5f63-b943-142f3a504252" :question/math-academy-id "q-86709" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2xy+6x-3$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-4.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,-3),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,-4),$ $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ and $(2,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/96/96096b5f8944785f1af1bbc4fd4942e081b4b3999887c0fa8d5d76008af454d5.png)\n\nSince the point $(0,-3)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,-4),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-3) & =2(0)(-3)+6(0)-3 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(-2,-4) & =2(-2)(-4)+6(-2)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-1,-1) & =2(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)+6(1)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(2,-4) & =2(2)(-4)+6(2)-3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,-4),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is Piecewise Smooth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c6ddfc8-d3e9-5bef-b036-cd7895639820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id 17592186404365} {:answer/id #uuid "c9416bc5-9684-5819-b799-c1e0f49a858d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-6$ and ${f}_{max}=6$" :db/id 17592186404366} {:answer/id #uuid "36a97ef2-1a82-57a0-968f-54bc87d7f1d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/id 17592186404367} {:answer/id #uuid "d805fe20-95dc-51e9-a39d-77b1b85929f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=6$" :db/id 17592186404368} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9268c4-7438-538b-8f4d-d2e4cbcf3323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-6$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/id 17592186404369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36a97ef2-1a82-57a0-968f-54bc87d7f1d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/id 17592186404367} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd6de7a6-023f-5e00-9b0c-d361277d5ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25973738-10c4-5a96-a292-a5b1bfa48a02" :question/math-academy-id "q-86712" :question/problem "![](images/20/20f279f5936987374965cbba01e7e089d88dae1c62767b1ab8eb9ebaea0e5ce9.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=6x-2xy$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-{x}^{2}/2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}6-2y,\\,-2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6-2y=0 \\\\ -2x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,3).$ Since the point $(0,3)$ does not belong to the closed region of $D,$ we do not include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(0) \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3}+2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation has no solution inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$ So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain two candidates:\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-12$ | $12$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-12$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $12$ at the point $(2,0).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233ac657-1b6c-5153-ae47-c73f6a18839b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id 17592186404372} {:answer/id #uuid "1398af4c-7ac7-570a-a8f9-3e7b4fb50230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id 17592186404373} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7069d6-ca3e-5408-8724-9f786c398052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id 17592186404374} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9e5045-1ffc-5c1d-8753-b170299e5ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id 17592186404375} {:answer/id #uuid "015fd256-8e43-5c2a-9c03-aa641f0627f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" :db/id 17592186404376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233ac657-1b6c-5153-ae47-c73f6a18839b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id 17592186404372} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a29c76-a92d-5a15-9982-689804fc2dba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8392959c-a393-5152-9307-9f96b5859277" :question/math-academy-id "q-86722" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-4xy$ on the closed elliptical region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 9}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4y,\\,-4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4y=0 \\\\ -4x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(3,0)$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-8$ | $10$ | $-8$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the points $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf37b4ad-f1a1-5a13-8dd8-d35dc56030e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id 17592186404379} {:answer/id #uuid "5980b150-496c-55d6-8d60-52afa76e9b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-11$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/id 17592186404380} {:answer/id #uuid "d5651eb6-a478-51ea-b582-8eee402be230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id 17592186404381} {:answer/id #uuid "e56bac34-6636-58da-9694-caed75b4198b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id 17592186404382} {:answer/id #uuid "08b74c80-69bd-5d43-8f58-45d6f43cbc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-11$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id 17592186404383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5651eb6-a478-51ea-b582-8eee402be230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id 17592186404381} :answer-field/id #uuid "6009c72a-9f51-5ad2-ae87-7dc2fbcb6754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24be252f-8729-57e6-8082-158ca3588445" :question/math-academy-id "q-86724" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6x+{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region  $D={(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(3,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-2,0)$, and $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/75/752c84f5ab0fb122102629e9ceada21c1bba0b8f0e93b3fffe0639e4284badab.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(3,0)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}-6(-2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =17 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}-6(2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the point $(-2,0).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5abea3ab-76a7-5598-8ede-b4cf88dd47d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues of a General 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19327b5d-0a87-5236-be0f-a4fc408fd3c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-2,-1}" :db/id 17592186404386} {:answer/id #uuid "576f8439-f4e9-50c2-87d9-f9f73bc72cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-2,1}" :db/id 17592186404387} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e67c97-48f5-50c6-a340-ea3827974147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-1,1}" :db/id 17592186404388} {:answer/id #uuid "f6484944-66bf-551b-bebc-dde27bbefb01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-2,-1,1}" :db/id 17592186404389} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d3b84a-b250-5e99-b0bf-b2e2d4abe21c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {2,-1,1}" :db/id 17592186404390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6484944-66bf-551b-bebc-dde27bbefb01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-2,-1,1}" :db/id 17592186404389} :answer-field/id #uuid "e326b9f3-50f4-5bf4-9fe5-a7fbee316948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b75d1ac-303a-511c-86a2-e86c1eaa7201" :question/math-academy-id "q-86758" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$O=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,O-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nO-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $det(O-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(O-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1)+(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $2:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2\n$$\n\nLet's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}+2(1)^{2}-(1)-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the equation. So, we can factor $(\\lambda -1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65bec174-0235-5a32-a0d2-2892af4ddaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Events are Mutually Exclusive"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a7fb06d-1012-5494-b8ed-015056975eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)\\ne P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id 17592186404393} {:answer/id #uuid "12b1de4e-720b-5103-b02a-93df60a43fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" :db/id 17592186404394} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdbc724-6263-5eb1-9f31-2c494f2f3956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)<P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id 17592186404395} {:answer/id #uuid "31a10f8b-ef54-5e35-b68f-279c0bb07e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id 17592186404396} {:answer/id #uuid "88f3e987-8871-5966-8f22-ab50bf9c636c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\ne P(B)$" :db/id 17592186404397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31a10f8b-ef54-5e35-b68f-279c0bb07e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id 17592186404396} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ba2dd8-2220-580b-91e1-140112b524be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94a980d8-2b2e-5cf9-9a95-55c26c4f6102" :question/math-academy-id "q-87289" :question/problem "Given $P(A)=\\frac{1}{4},$ $P(B)=\\frac{3}{10},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{19}{40},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{19}{40} & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{10}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{19}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+12-19}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events."}] [{:db/id 17592186404398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1fa984a-4005-5f18-91a1-e7be739a26f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id 17592186404400} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a24b59-219c-5ab9-84d8-65b6683803d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{15}" :db/id 17592186404401} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a658d2-9bd0-5f27-8ae6-95960ef0e599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id 17592186404402} {:answer/id #uuid "4508f230-b67f-58f6-9371-8d3f3d56004f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id 17592186404403} {:answer/id #uuid "809b05b5-a1d9-5cff-895e-6d353eace3c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id 17592186404404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "809b05b5-a1d9-5cff-895e-6d353eace3c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id 17592186404404} :answer-field/id #uuid "a831c2fd-e777-5d48-bdde-b20b03fa96ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e20e394-569b-5fe6-8d7e-276b93ef613e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87305" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{1}{15}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{3}{5},$ find $P(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-1}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c64c1a-b7d5-51ae-b5d2-9a62f232cbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id 17592186404407} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5066c8-e0c4-5b38-9465-2f4d0e3f690f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id 17592186404408} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c58684-50d7-5ea8-90a7-fc01059630ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id 17592186404409} {:answer/id #uuid "3584fd28-1d6f-517b-b3eb-c4379212f7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id 17592186404410} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca51417-9fbb-52cf-9f57-772cce3140b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{25}" :db/id 17592186404411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3584fd28-1d6f-517b-b3eb-c4379212f7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id 17592186404410} :answer-field/id #uuid "2318f6dc-36b2-5ed9-8af3-6f105cfafa3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "610824ef-12b9-5ca4-a472-9a8e2ccc0745" :question/math-academy-id "q-87308" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{18}{25}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{4}{5},$ find $P(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{18}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{20-18}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186157918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac76d62e-6bea-515a-af06-e4fae7bc1338" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Exponential Functions with Different Exponents and a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "147b7050-1bb7-56bf-a406-2b9183de6cfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id 17592186404413} {:answer/id #uuid "a45e44b9-d508-50d9-9942-ac9f170c45cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -3$ or $x\\ge 0$" :db/id 17592186404414} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd7a005-6153-5c6c-8264-dec69be6c819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solution" :db/id 17592186404415} {:answer/id #uuid "eec96819-d9b8-55eb-bf60-48817f138fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 3" :db/id 17592186404416} {:answer/id #uuid "1649e747-fd9d-5142-b2bd-bf10ded844c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 0$ or $x\\ge 3$" :db/id 17592186404417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1649e747-fd9d-5142-b2bd-bf10ded844c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 0$ or $x\\ge 3$" :db/id 17592186404417} :answer-field/id #uuid "84492f7a-3823-5c2e-b8ff-3d045410e8f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "485dd702-22ef-5672-ba6d-aa433fac1c74" :question/math-academy-id "q-87772" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${3}^{4x}{x}^{2}\\ge {3}^{4x+1}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4x}{x}^{2} & \\ge {3}^{4x+1}x \\\\ {3}^{4x}{x}^{2}-{3}^{4x+1}x & \\ge 0 \\\\ {3}^{4x}({x}^{2}-3x) & \\ge 0 \\\\ {3}^{4x}x(x-3) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${3}^{4x}$ and get\n\n$$\nx(x-3)\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ The parabola is at or above the $x$-axis when $x\\le 0$ or $x\\ge 3.$\n\n![](images/3b/3bfb82543e7c05d0ca0b8f6c6973025400ad1253acbc57214173dbd3e8c37fef.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is  $x\\le 0$ or $x\\ge 3.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98dff91-dac6-54f4-874e-412ff82fc7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id 17592186404420} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6b92be-72f3-51e8-a494-51777f66072a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id 17592186404421} {:answer/id #uuid "1d09f0af-8d98-573b-a3ff-0be88001c588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id 17592186404422} {:answer/id #uuid "12dbc12d-ca72-5f5e-816c-d12c14fc89e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡12}" :db/id 17592186404423} {:answer/id #uuid "fc401782-1079-528b-98fb-c84a43f43898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡12}" :db/id 17592186404424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f98dff91-dac6-54f4-874e-412ff82fc7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id 17592186404420} :answer-field/id #uuid "36d269bb-4337-5bf6-b3aa-e727abe356a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "019afbc4-64db-5396-aa9e-b5972e6733b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-87862" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-{2}^{6x-1}<{12}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since ${12}^{x}$ is always positive and $-{2}^{6x-1}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n-{2}^{6x-1}<{12}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b27ef2-1d45-5405-8db2-6a0f32fffd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id 17592186404427} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbabc41-ee20-588f-9fb5-7d7683297f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<3-{log}_{3}⁡(6)" :db/id 17592186404428} {:answer/id #uuid "20a1148d-a678-5339-a89f-18e31fc89eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<5-{log}_{6}⁡(2)" :db/id 17592186404429} {:answer/id #uuid "b669b97b-5f13-5c9a-b39a-fd990c4e287f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id 17592186404430} {:answer/id #uuid "b42e9afb-b44e-5084-853e-ad95a5af9d53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>6-{log}_{5}⁡(3)" :db/id 17592186404431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23b27ef2-1d45-5405-8db2-6a0f32fffd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id 17592186404427} :answer-field/id #uuid "2889b1bf-5c46-5be4-86d3-5d866d1cf34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5f64ef7-fc02-5b6d-a8c5-440eac71e226" :question/math-academy-id "q-87893" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x}+6>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x}+6 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x} & >-6 \\\\ {3}^{1-5x} & >-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{3}^{1-5x}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality ${3}^{1-5x}>-10$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7656d23-06c9-5d22-b62a-3636bca66ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Base or the Height of a Triangle Given Its Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef35ba9-a1a6-5901-a68b-21603e2051d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id 17592186404434} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf3609b-ef5e-5b0b-9c1a-f2b156f94734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id 17592186404435} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f39be9-b1e0-5587-868d-9f77d0afc794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id 17592186404436} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9dd512-8f07-5391-be34-d4707734b6d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id 17592186404437} {:answer/id #uuid "7c69f1fa-94c7-5dbc-8f48-c65d49727acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id 17592186404438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdf3609b-ef5e-5b0b-9c1a-f2b156f94734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,{cm}^{2}" :db/id 17592186404435} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d266bcc-fa01-524b-89f8-39a6f9df7aea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7bcc27a5-b987-5302-94f2-988d56293740" :question/math-academy-id "q-87910" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c19425664f79df1f46863f59268ebd27e20defce41b21e877926bbcadc9e5c3.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $△ABC$ and $△CDE$ shown above. If the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=7\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 42 & =\\frac{7\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 7CD & =84 \\\\ CD & =12\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/00/0014faf05eaf15d70f885802e5b820b0c72d42a96f400442f827ed229de8b240.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 12}{2} \\\\  & =30{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "479e5736-efe1-5e94-929f-3dcd6d13a733" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Exponential Functions with the Same Exponents and a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4466cd39-9e73-567d-823b-c399bff02272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186404441} {:answer/id #uuid "3172eec7-1b0f-5cfa-83f3-1e0bdcb73644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}<x<2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186404442} {:answer/id #uuid "b5687ebc-d415-5e04-8b82-d0d23c606f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-2\\sqrt{3}$ or $x>2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id 17592186404443} {:answer/id #uuid "7254dc37-da09-582b-9081-9f0dd6e6a378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-2$ or $x>2$" :db/id 17592186404444} {:answer/id #uuid "29d347e2-48fa-5c3d-9ae5-0c4b2283a50f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-\\sqrt{3}$ or $x>\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id 17592186404445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5687ebc-d415-5e04-8b82-d0d23c606f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-2\\sqrt{3}$ or $x>2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id 17592186404443} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2157a1d-8075-5da7-8704-cf68a742b357" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99779233-4022-57ea-8b13-fcf906c4aeff" :question/math-academy-id "q-88308" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-{9}^{2x-4}{x}^{2}<-5\\cdot {9}^{2x-4}-7\\cdot {9}^{2x-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{9}^{2x-4}{x}^{2} & <-5\\cdot {9}^{2x-4}-7\\cdot {9}^{2x-4} \\\\ -{9}^{2x-4}{x}^{2}+5\\cdot {9}^{2x-4}+7\\cdot {9}^{2x-4} & <0 \\\\ (-{x}^{2}+5+7){9}^{2x-4} & <0 \\\\ (12-{x}^{2}){9}^{2x-4} & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${9}^{2x-4}$ and solve the resulting inequality using familiar techniques:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-{x}^{2} & <0 \\\\ -{x}^{2} & <-12 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >12 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{12} \\\\ |x| & >2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to the inequality above is $x<-2\\sqrt{3}$ or $x>2\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e77e8eb-4f76-565b-b3a2-5c91767ba39f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id 17592186404448} {:answer/id #uuid "de87823a-03c0-572e-9778-3d0e131e7ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id 17592186404449} {:answer/id #uuid "edee0d23-5e90-53e9-9a9b-3e624f83441e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id 17592186404450} {:answer/id #uuid "45a9656a-bcf9-5ffd-9994-d663146b2ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x" :db/id 17592186404451} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c913bf-1bbb-5e26-96bd-2c1ec496da34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id 17592186404452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de87823a-03c0-572e-9778-3d0e131e7ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id 17592186404449} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dec29b5-6835-594f-89d3-a76d54e5866b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe1794bb-4b33-510b-8172-929bfa639d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-8854" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+16x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\,=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-8\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+8\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ -8\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2}$, $\\beta =0.$\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "857d696d-6a3f-5fff-a5e1-b05148f28f55" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Sentences Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b5b70db-2b05-51ba-84eb-8426d1e48801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is romantic" :db/id 17592186404455} {:answer/id #uuid "203b1139-b63c-55f9-93a5-2e112bba5b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is romantic but not comic" :db/id 17592186404456} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd3bdcc-15fc-5268-bfaf-aeee4a0fee8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is comic" :db/id 17592186404457} {:answer/id #uuid "541680c1-2b24-55c6-8afc-6a4bedb19ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is romantic or comic" :db/id 17592186404458} {:answer/id #uuid "7aecce06-d66d-5e1f-8e08-ac26cc73bd78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is romantic and comic" :db/id 17592186404459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "541680c1-2b24-55c6-8afc-6a4bedb19ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The movie is romantic or comic" :db/id 17592186404458} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ec9b14-67d1-5d44-aaf5-9a87460b11fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0026e046-e84c-5c78-8cfa-12a3749b6a2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-89252" :question/problem "Simplify the sentence \"The movie is romantic, or the movie is romantic or comic.\"" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the phrase \"the movie is romantic or comic\" as the equivalent phrase \"the movie is romantic or the movie is comic.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The movie is romantic, or the movie is romantic or the movie is comic.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The movie is romantic or the movie is romantic, or the movie is comic.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"The movie is romantic or the movie is romantic\" to just \"The movie is romantic.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The movie is romantic, or the movie is comic.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely as\n\n\"The movie is romantic or comic.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186404460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4b6a5f-9aed-5dad-9356-4038bf9c0acf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse Laplace Transform Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48531a52-74be-58a5-ad7b-ff876a370cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=2{e}^{2t}+3{e}^{-3t}" :db/id 17592186404462} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9edfb3-bb01-53ba-acc4-d8149555d614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t" :db/id 17592186404463} {:answer/id #uuid "82f55f6a-fc56-52cb-9c7f-8a3c3e42bcc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=10{t}^{2}+{e}^{-3t}" :db/id 17592186404464} {:answer/id #uuid "3971cac1-f8e8-5bb8-bd17-d8d02ea2b90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-3t}" :db/id 17592186404465} {:answer/id #uuid "53069da8-16e0-5ffb-a9a6-f7574465ddca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t" :db/id 17592186404466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3971cac1-f8e8-5bb8-bd17-d8d02ea2b90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-3t}" :db/id 17592186404465} :answer-field/id #uuid "aea651d3-f2f0-5676-bbf4-bf4713440432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c32439a8-2f0a-52f2-ba4c-04ee07df7da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-89821" :question/problem "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=\\frac{10}{(s-2)(s+3)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{(s-2)(s+3)}=\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{2}{s+3}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition property of the Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{2}{s+3}} & =2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-2}}-2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{10}{(s-2)(s+3)}$ is $f(t)=2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-3t}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42525832-e186-538f-baf8-bb4418e9820b" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Inequalities Presented in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7c2a5d4-298c-5631-a9d7-aba5b729e118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y<3 \\\\ y>2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404469} {:answer/id #uuid "01bc8bf9-9741-5be3-bf6c-963968a707d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y\\le 3 \\\\ y\\ge 2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404470} {:answer/id #uuid "da29e297-529e-5fe2-b38a-d2962daba4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y<2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404471} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5ca256-25d0-5c51-b2d0-61bafb216142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y<3 \\\\ y<2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404472} {:answer/id #uuid "57ca1c9c-b7c8-5c26-8e55-f2e99026a45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y>2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57ca1c9c-b7c8-5c26-8e55-f2e99026a45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y>2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404473} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5be21f-60e7-597b-83dc-3c20339aa469" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "262609c0-5557-5285-b43c-f82fdb8825a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-90054" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8c1d5902a76a68e5f8358d4c3668e1be184ec7241184df4ff1363d41995f3aa.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $x+y=3.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-x+3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\"  inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-x+3,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+y>3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=2x.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict.  Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y>2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbd52408-5f2f-58c9-9a88-bd992fe8752c" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Strict Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57e3339b-5c2d-5e02-8e56-922ef2cb88bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404476} {:answer/id #uuid "b2167803-87c2-5c4e-aa85-d576aac9cc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404477} {:answer/id #uuid "aa93daa1-24f9-58db-9545-6bf0487628c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>4x+3 \\\\ y\\ge \\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404478} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf061e8-e3ee-5abf-a78a-299067a79064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>4x+3 \\\\ y<\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404479} {:answer/id #uuid "6051d13a-7c7c-5804-88c5-53711a5cb6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2167803-87c2-5c4e-aa85-d576aac9cc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404477} :answer-field/id #uuid "94914b35-e16f-5398-9a50-4baddb153e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66b0d8c5-2487-53cc-b840-b06edcc12d56" :question/math-academy-id "q-90323" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e863789ff989ee1cb3d3fe6c22fe0d4daa1dac76d6fbfbebdad801006ca4c969.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=4x+3.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<4x+3.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{4}x-2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>\\frac{1}{4}x-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y<4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a258070e-5c3c-595e-81d6-3e0f9b9a5daf" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Mixed Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daea2326-2376-5164-b217-f74ca9ead6c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404483} {:answer/id #uuid "8b73faca-4331-5468-aa5b-5f3ece1295f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\le -x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404484} {:answer/id #uuid "83a1c4b0-cc77-5dcc-8de0-37f06af0e319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404485} {:answer/id #uuid "b45d0b54-a0ec-5724-b49e-549e13b6d4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge x+2 \\\\ y<-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404486} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2a88ff-6f4a-5ac9-a039-72c31f12f559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83a1c4b0-cc77-5dcc-8de0-37f06af0e319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id 17592186404485} :answer-field/id #uuid "f86fe5f2-d23c-5598-bb24-07f9f597ec1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e5e2c49-2afd-5324-972b-43974f8bfdd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-90327" :question/problem "![](images/76/762e53967ddb3ccddfa1c9b7a2aa303d66fc1b761c9be8f409c0b91ecb0201de.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities represents the shaded region shown in the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=x+2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-x+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97ea3ae4-022f-5830-abec-118a76a06fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186404490} {:answer/id #uuid "536feed6-9dfe-5307-aec5-0edb198d16a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186404491} {:answer/id #uuid "20fcb72e-c053-5056-a323-074281dda331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186404492} {:answer/id #uuid "51bc6cbd-e4d3-52a7-a0e3-484410623715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186404493} {:answer/id #uuid "e96fb37a-ec85-52be-af50-069c63122601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186404494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20fcb72e-c053-5056-a323-074281dda331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186404492} :answer-field/id #uuid "210542c5-de8c-5b63-a6a2-7bc9744e8618" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fa44e0d-f47d-55dc-9787-703e1dcaa8bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-90493" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nI. $10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$\nII. $2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$\nIII. $2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4)=50{y}^{4}{e}^{x}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y)=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$ gives an exact equation."}] [{:db/id 17592186404495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67cf3cc3-3635-5acb-a7ed-452bb9111c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186404497} {:answer/id #uuid "45d32fb4-03c1-5310-b998-78f449edc9a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186404498} {:answer/id #uuid "497b8fa1-01e8-5353-b89a-f5299641c340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186404499} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb2a7c0-0bcf-56f1-9412-5bbd52e46ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186404500} {:answer/id #uuid "52739661-1a40-5904-b3b8-993cc50b384a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186404501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67cf3cc3-3635-5acb-a7ed-452bb9111c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186404497} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa88942a-1208-5ce3-a956-2912d19d6e95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "056f268e-6f23-5cca-ba50-28679fda2fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-90580" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\nI. $f(x)=7{x}^{7}$\nII. $g(x,y)=x+y$\nIII. $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z-2xyz+x{z}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is homogeneous of degree $7.$  It is a polynomial of degree $7,$ with no terms of degree other than $7.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $1.$ It is a polynomial of degree $1,$ and all of its terms are of degree $1.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is  homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c59fbbc-2dc0-562b-b432-ce3c7f59dad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ or $6$" :db/id 17592186404504} {:answer/id #uuid "c01408ce-3680-5eb5-8582-5d15eb08699d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ or $2$" :db/id 17592186404505} {:answer/id #uuid "0e45d023-bccd-58f6-9704-f559be5f8da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6$ or $1$" :db/id 17592186404506} {:answer/id #uuid "1b463397-33ab-5e64-907b-e8904f8a0935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $2$" :db/id 17592186404507} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e036bd-fcc3-5ff2-9e88-b80ab4086884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ or $3$" :db/id 17592186404508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6e036bd-fcc3-5ff2-9e88-b80ab4086884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ or $3$" :db/id 17592186404508} :answer-field/id #uuid "117667a9-4c0a-559e-becf-2c255a4fa945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8bfd769-670f-5f6b-bec7-13134d36de0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-90706" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =3.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea90192a-98aa-5857-a246-925646efadb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-5x}" :db/id 17592186404511} {:answer/id #uuid "58fb9126-dcca-5a22-be00-7d49e5cf7948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{e}^{3x}" :db/id 17592186404512} {:answer/id #uuid "dad7dae3-861a-5560-878f-de18703b5976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-5x}-{e}^{-3x}" :db/id 17592186404513} {:answer/id #uuid "28ac766a-b6d1-5aa1-a97b-4a4386277974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}" :db/id 17592186404514} {:answer/id #uuid "9709ebe4-8c4a-5c48-8164-358a57e70e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-5x}+3{e}^{-3x}" :db/id 17592186404515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58fb9126-dcca-5a22-be00-7d49e5cf7948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{e}^{3x}" :db/id 17592186404512} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1363158-fb5d-52a2-aee6-d038f5cfedc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec0270d1-70c2-5347-87eb-345d880dbd64" :question/math-academy-id "q-90725" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+15y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+15{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-5.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{5x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id 17592186404516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9183d1-23e9-5f90-a209-d6b19d245c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}$" :db/id 17592186404518} {:answer/id #uuid "226f3fd1-f941-5569-80fa-d157db347e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$" :db/id 17592186404519} {:answer/id #uuid "3b46698e-ccc1-5f9f-8f8c-b005abd4b4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}$" :db/id 17592186404520} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4bce52-0e47-55a1-ac84-7a9f035eef89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$" :db/id 17592186404521} {:answer/id #uuid "38987e9b-2b2d-5194-a0c2-03820aac76b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{3}$" :db/id 17592186404522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "226f3fd1-f941-5569-80fa-d157db347e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$" :db/id 17592186404519} :answer-field/id #uuid "7222ce8b-9f86-5907-ac8e-304c68fbadee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc8df3f8-8c99-53c4-bedf-bdbc4a64bd8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-91157" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -13 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above ${a}_{33}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -13 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73f5eb09-e668-558b-b5c4-5f72bb86b5f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-8{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404525} {:answer/id #uuid "b30692bb-6bff-5975-b521-cac176cbe736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+8{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404526} {:answer/id #uuid "0481513e-3cd0-504e-8e8e-90f5fc5b8b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-8{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404527} {:answer/id #uuid "7e64580b-23e3-5c32-bb1a-f2f9628a7159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+8{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404528} {:answer/id #uuid "85047f08-97d3-5876-b80f-412bddff211b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-8{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-7{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73f5eb09-e668-558b-b5c4-5f72bb86b5f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-8{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404525} :answer-field/id #uuid "6844cde6-2278-591a-a355-02a1ecec361e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26e3c3e9-0f2f-55d8-b677-4d51648ddadf" :question/math-academy-id "q-91159" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -10 & -11 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above and below ${a}_{21}=-1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-7{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -10 & -11 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-8{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -10 & -11 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-7{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-8{R}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186162576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f91cfd00-78b8-5e81-b3da-fb66e4a255b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404531} {:answer/id #uuid "ba816428-2269-5d70-a23d-de4d9459d350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404532} {:answer/id #uuid "6b221426-9041-561c-a939-a652454c20be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404533} {:answer/id #uuid "7377369a-9c28-53fe-95a8-20a033d61460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404534} {:answer/id #uuid "4d223b12-1545-5649-89b4-f8afd0248272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba816428-2269-5d70-a23d-de4d9459d350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}$" :db/id 17592186404532} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ebe645c-97a3-5257-bc32-0836ea3ff9c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "200a95d9-6cef-55b2-a6a6-5c194d5765aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-91163" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above and below ${a}_{23}=-3$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186404536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f1ec99-e166-5986-a07d-f07be0dfeb31" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "367d5c7f-38e0-520e-83c3-49e14d8a0120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$" :db/id 17592186404538} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd078f9-4afb-5657-b580-57e60ff58958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-3{R}_{1}$" :db/id 17592186404539} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eefb87-41c8-5096-b981-c10f00b1b3e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}$" :db/id 17592186404540} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf61f67-9290-5f2d-a040-b0ae165dd343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$" :db/id 17592186404541} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fa6549-8cf1-506e-bea9-e621c1c2285b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}$" :db/id 175